        "summary": "# Report 2605363 – Right axillary dissection – AI-assisted interpretation for the attending pathologist\n\nSource: `report_2605363_EN.pdf` (1 page; admission 04/29/2026, reported 05/05/2026; F, 41 y, outpatient). Anatomical pathology report only; no clinical history, prior biopsy result, or breast specimen is included in this accession.\n\n## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n\n1. **\"RCB III\" assigned on an axillary specimen alone is not derivable and should be treated as provisional.** RCB (Symmans/MDACC) is a composite breast + nodal index: RCB = 1.4(f_inv·d_prim)^0.17 + [4(1 − 0.75^LN)·d_met]^0.17; classes RCB-I ≤ 1.36, RCB-II 1.36–3.28, RCB-III > 3.28. Using this report's nodal data (LN = 26, d_met = 10 mm) the nodal term is **1.87** — effectively its ceiling for a 10 mm deposit (1 − 0.75^26 ≈ 0.999) and, by itself, in the **RCB-II** band. RCB-III requires a breast tumour-bed contribution with f_inv × d_prim > ~1.0 mm (e.g. 10 mm bed with > ~10 % invasive cellularity; 5 mm bed at 30 % → 3.37 = RCB-III; 20 mm bed at 5 % → 3.27 = RCB-II). If the breast shows pCR, the case is RCB-II (1.87). The final class must be computed after integration with the breast resection; the label \"treatment response index … RCB III (no signs of treatment response)\" also conflates a quantitative residual-disease index with qualitative therapy-effect assessment.\n2. **Neoadjuvant therapy is implied (RCB / \"treatment response\" language) but nowhere documented.** No regimen, dates, pre-treatment cN, or M status. The \"yp\" prefix, the statement \"no signs of treatment response\", and the RCB assignment are all contingent on confirming NAT; untreated nodes are histologically indistinguishable from therapy-refractory nodes.\n3. **Biomarkers referenced but absent.** \"Performed on a prior study\" — no ER/PR/HER2/Ki-67 results, accession, date, or specimen type are cited. The receptor profile **cannot be inferred from nuclear grade**: grade 3 nuclei occur in TNBC, HER2-positive, and high-grade luminal B carcinomas alike; no profile is assumed in this analysis. The decision not to retest on residual disease merits reconsideration (see Recommendations) given the absence of any therapy effect and the subtype-dependence of post-neoadjuvant adjuvant options.\n4. **Primary tumour not characterised in this report.** Deposits are described only as \"carcinoma, nuclear grade 3\". WHO 5th ed. histotype and Nottingham grade cannot be assigned from nodal morphology; \"nuclear grade 3\" must not be transcribed as histologic grade 3. Breast origin is presumed from context but is not explicitly stated, and the existence/location of the breast resection (concurrent, separate accession, or not yet performed) is not recorded.\n5. **Specimen limitations.** Unoriented specimen → axillary levels (I/II/III, incl. level III/infraclavicular) not assignable. Status of the soft-tissue specimen edge relative to the 5 mm ENE is not stated (relevant to axillary RT planning). Not specified whether the 1 cm deposit measurement includes the extranodal component (no effect on N category).\n6. **Minor internal inconsistencies.** Microscopy: \"26 … show metastasis\" (only the largest measured) vs. Diagnosis: \"26 with macrometastasis\" — the > 2 mm size of every deposit is not documented (does not alter ypN3a). Microscopy lists \"foreign body reaction\"; Diagnosis substitutes \"cicatricial change\" — the aetiology (prior SLNB/TAD, clip/marker, core biopsy, or therapy) is not addressed and retrieval of any clipped/marked node is not recorded.\n\n## SPECIMEN\n\nRight total axillary lymph node dissection (ALND), unoriented 12 × 8 × 6 cm fibroadipose fragment in formalin, 50 LNs (up to 1.5 cm) in 19 blocks; presumed post-neoadjuvant setting; no breast specimen in this accession.\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\n\nMetastatic high-grade (nuclear grade 3) carcinoma, presumed breast primary, in 26 of 50 right axillary lymph nodes (macrometastases, largest 10 mm) with extranodal extension up to 5 mm and no histological therapy effect — **ypN3a (AJCC 8th)**, residual nodal disease/non-pCR; anatomic stage IIIC (any T, N3, M0) pending ypT and M; **RCB class not determinable from this specimen** (nodal component 1.87; reported RCB-III provisional pending breast tumour-bed data).\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n\n- **Nodal ratio:** 26/50 positive (LNR 0.52; 52 %) → ypN3a (≥ 10 positive axillary nodes, ≥ 1 deposit > 2 mm; threshold exceeded > 2.5-fold).\n- **Largest deposit:** 10 mm (1.0 cm) — macrometastasis.\n- **Extranodal extension:** present, up to 5 mm (> 2 mm; extensive category). Soft-tissue edge status not reported.\n- **Nuclear grade:** 3 in nodal deposits (Nottingham grade not assessable on nodes).\n- **Treatment response:** none identified in the evaluated nodes (no regression described). Soft tissue: fat necrosis, foreign-body reaction, scarring — aetiology unspecified.\n- **RCB class:** report states RCB-III \"for this dissection\"; nodal term calculates to 1.87 (RCB-II band alone); final class requires breast tumour bed (d1 × d2, % invasive cellularity, % in situ) → **provisional**.\n- **Biomarker status:** not performed on this specimen; prior ER/PR/HER2 (± Ki-67) referenced but not reported → unknown in this report.\n- **Other:** 41-year-old female (premenopausal age range); 6-day turnaround (04/29 → 05/05/2026).\n\n## INTERPRETATION\n\n**Nodal burden and ENE.** Twenty-six macrometastatic nodes with a 10 mm largest deposit place this axilla firmly in the highest AJCC nodal category (ypN3a, or pN3a if no NAT was given). Level III involvement would independently satisfy N3a but cannot be evaluated in an unoriented specimen; it does not change the category. ENE is not a staging variable in AJCC 8th but is a recognised adverse locoregional factor; at 5 mm it exceeds the customary 2 mm cut-off and, together with a LNR of 0.52, marks a high axillary recurrence risk and informs regional nodal irradiation planning. Whether tumour reaches the soft-tissue edge of the specimen is unstated and clinically relevant.\n\n**Stage.** Under AJCC 8th, any T with N3 and M0 is **anatomic stage IIIC**, so the stage group is fixed at IIIC irrespective of the ypT category (ypT0–ypT4) provided cM0; if M1 was established before NAT the case remains stage IV regardless of response. ypT must be taken from the breast resection. This is not a pCR (pCR requires ypT0/is ypN0). The AJCC 8th prognostic stage group is designed for upfront-surgery patients and in any case requires Nottingham grade and ER/PR/HER2 — none available here — so the post-therapy stage should be reported as anatomic ypTNM, with the pre-treatment clinical prognostic stage retained alongside.\n\n**Treatment response.** If NAT is confirmed, the complete absence of therapy effect across 26 involved nodes indicates a chemoresistant phenotype. Residual nodal disease after NAT, particularly at the N2–N3 level, carries a worse prognosis than the equivalent nodal category in an untreated axilla, and this is compounded by grade 3 nuclei, ENE, high LNR, and young age.\n\n**RCB.** The nodal contribution (1.87) is already at its maximum for a 10 mm deposit, so the reported class hinges entirely on the breast: virtually any measurable residual invasive primary (f_inv × d_prim > ~1 mm) yields RCB-III, whereas a breast pCR yields RCB-II. RCB-III is the class with the highest distant-relapse and mortality risk in all receptor subtypes; RCB-II with this nodal profile would still be high-risk. Either way, residual disease is extensive and the case requires subtype-directed post-neoadjuvant systemic therapy and comprehensive locoregional management — decisions that depend on the missing biomarker profile. Note also that if an SLNB/TAD preceded NAT (a possible explanation for the fat necrosis/foreign-body reaction/scar), RCB and ypN are assessed on the post-therapy ALND alone and the pre-therapy nodal findings must be recorded separately.\n\n**Primary tumour type and biomarkers.** WHO 5th ed. histotype cannot be classified from this report; the deposits are compatible with, but not specific for, high-grade IBC-NST (statistically the most likely type in the absence of documented special-type features), and lobular/special types are not excluded because no architecture or IHC is described. Breast origin is presumed from the clinical context (prior biomarker study) and should be confirmed. No receptor profile is assumed: nuclear grade does not predict ER/PR/HER2 status with clinically usable accuracy.\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n\n1. **Integrate with the breast resection** (same or separate accession): ypT, residual tumour-bed d1 × d2, % invasive cellularity, % in situ → compute the formal RCB index (MDACC calculator) and confirm or correct the \"RCB III\" class; report a single combined RCB for the case.\n2. **Confirm clinical history:** NAT regimen and completion date; pre-treatment cTNM (cN, imaging nodal burden) and M status (cM0 vs pre-NAT M1); prior axillary procedures (SLNB/TAD), biopsy-clip placement, and whether the marked node was retrieved (specimen radiograph if applicable) — correlate with fat necrosis/foreign-body reaction/scar.\n3. **Retrieve and cite the prior biomarker results** (ER, PR, HER2 IHC ± ISH, Ki-67; accession, date, specimen type) in this report. **Consider retesting on residual nodal disease** — adequate FFPE substrate — particularly if prior results were negative/equivocal, obtained on a limited core, or if heterogeneity is suspected; post-NAT receptor discordance is well documented and the adjuvant pathway for residual disease is subtype-dependent.\n4. **Confirm breast origin and histotype/Nottingham grade** from the prior core/breast specimen (WHO 5th ed.). Only if a primary is unavailable: IHC on the nodal deposit (GATA3, TRPS1, mammaglobin, GCDFP-15, ER; E-cadherin/p120 if lobular is considered; CK7/CK20 to exclude non-mammary origin).\n5. **Complete CAP/ICCR nodal elements:** macro- vs micrometastasis breakdown for all 26 nodes; ENE extent category (> 2 mm) and whether the 10 mm measurement includes extranodal tumour; soft-tissue specimen-edge status relative to ENE; explicit statement that levels are not assignable (unoriented) — communicate to surgeon/radiation oncologist.\n6. **Add a formal staging line:** ypN3a (AJCC 8th); anatomic stage IIIC (any T, N3, M0) pending ypT and M; note prognostic stage group not applicable post-NAT; retain pre-treatment clinical stage.\n7. **MDT/clinical flags:** age 41 meets germline testing criteria by age alone (≤ 50 y) irrespective of subtype; high-risk residual disease → subtype-directed post-neoadjuvant therapy and comprehensive regional nodal RT planning.",
        "trajectory": [
