        "summary": "## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n\n**Major (affect RCB, stage or therapy)**\n\n1. **The reported RCB-III cannot be derived from an axillary specimen, so it is provisional.** RCB combines 4 breast tumour-bed variables (d1, d2, %CA, %CIS) with 2 nodal variables. Only the nodal ones are reported (LN = 26, d_met = 10 mm), giving a nodal component of **1.87**. The nodal component alone cannot exceed the RCB-III cut-off of 3.28; that would need d_met ≈ 271 mm. \"RCB III\" therefore assumes residual invasive breast carcinoma with f_inv × d_prim > ~1.03 mm. If the breast is ypT0/ypTis (breast term = 0), the correct result is **RCB-II (index 1.87)**. \"No signs of treatment response\" is not an RCB criterion, and RCB is a combined breast-plus-node index, not a score for a dissection on its own.\n2. **Biomarkers are missing and have not been imputed.** ER/PR/HER2/Ki-67 are referred to a \"prior study\" but not reported. Nuclear grade does not predict receptor status: NG3 occurs in HR+/HER2−, HER2+ and triple-negative tumours. An inferred profile would be unsafe for prognostic staging and therapy selection. Status is **unknown until the prior results are retrieved**. The comment that repeat testing is \"not contributory\" is debatable, because this is non-responding high-grade residual disease with plenty of tumour to test.\n3. **The primary tumour is not characterised.** The diagnosis says only \"carcinoma\"; the site of origin and WHO histotype are not stated. Breast origin is implied (RCB is used; biomarkers were done on a prior study) but not documented. NG3 alone does not allow WHO 5th ed. typing (NST vs lobular vs special type), so **no histotype is assigned here**.\n4. **Staging data are incomplete and neoadjuvant therapy (NAT) is not documented.**\n   - The report states no ypN category.\n   - ypT cannot be assessed (no breast tissue in the specimen).\n   - M is a clinical category (pM0 is not valid) and is not stated.\n   - The side of the primary is not documented. ypN3a assumes an ipsilateral (right) primary; contralateral axillary nodes would be M1.\n   - The yp prefix and RCB both assume a standard neoadjuvant regimen, which is not recorded. RCB is validated mainly after NAC ± anti-HER2 therapy. Without qualifying NAT, the category is pN3a and RCB does not apply.\n\n**Specimen limitations / internal inconsistencies**\n\n5. **Unoriented specimen.** Axillary level (I–III) and apical status cannot be determined. This does not affect ypN3a but matters for regional RT planning.\n6. **Node count may be imprecise.** 50 LN (up to 15 mm) in 19 blocks means nodes shared cassettes, so bisected nodes or fragments may have been counted twice. The microscopic text says \"metastasis\", while the diagnosis calls all 26 macrometastases, with no macro/micro/ITC breakdown. ypN3a is robust (26 is far above 10); the exact count and LNR are less certain.\n7. **ENE is incompletely characterised.** The extent is 5 mm (>2 mm), but the number of nodes with ENE is not stated, nor whether this is ENE or a separate extranodal deposit. It is unclear whether the 10 mm deposit size includes contiguous ENE (CAP includes it). If it does not, d_met is underestimated: the nodal component would be about 2.01 at 15 mm, and the class would not change.\n8. **Soft-tissue findings differ between sections.** The microscopic section lists fat necrosis and foreign-body reaction. The diagnosis lists fat necrosis and cicatricial change: the foreign-body reaction is dropped, and scarring is never described microscopically. This suggests a prior axillary procedure (node biopsy, clip/marker, prior SLNB), which raises three points:\n   - Retrieval of the clipped node is not documented.\n   - Nodes from any prior SLNB must be added in for the final ypN and LNR.\n   - Confirm the scarring is not regressed nodal tissue among the 24 negative nodes. That would be a mixed response, contradicting \"no response\".\n\n## SPECIMEN\nRequest 2605363 (F, 41 y): right axillary lymph node dissection (ALND), unoriented, 12 × 8 × 6 cm; 50 LN (≤1.5 cm) in 19 blocks; admitted 04/29/2026, reported 05/05/2026. A post-neoadjuvant setting is implied but not documented.\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\n**Right ALND: metastatic nuclear grade 3 carcinoma (presumed ipsilateral breast primary; histotype not specified) in 26/50 lymph nodes, reported as macrometastases (largest 10 mm), with ENE (5 mm) and no histological treatment response. Nodal stage ypN3a (AJCC 8th). The reported RCB-III is provisional until breast tumour-bed data are available; the nodal component alone is 1.87, which would be RCB-II if no invasive carcinoma remains in the breast. ER/PR/HER2 are not available in this report.**\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n| Parameter | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Nodal ratio | **26/50 (LNR 0.52)**; nodal yield adequate (≥10) |\n| Largest deposit | **10 mm**, macrometastasis |\n| Extranodal extension | **Present, up to 5 mm (>2 mm)**; number of nodes with ENE not stated |\n| Nuclear grade | **NG3** in nodal deposits; Nottingham grade of the primary not reported |\n| Treatment response | **None identified in involved nodes** (CAP category: no definite response in the metastatic carcinoma) |\n| RCB class | **RCB-III as reported, not verifiable**: nodal component 1.87; breast variables missing |\n| Biomarker status | **ER / PR / HER2 / Ki-67: not in this report (prior study); unknown and not imputed** |\n| Nodal stage | **ypN3a** |\n| Overall stage | ypTX ypN3a, M not stated: **anatomic stage IIIC-equivalent if cM0** (IV if M1) |\n| Other | Fat necrosis and foreign-body reaction/scarring in soft tissue; probably a prior procedure (confirm) |\n\n## INTERPRETATION\n- **Nodal burden and ypN**\n  - 26 involved axillary LN, at least one deposit >2.0 mm: this meets **ypN3a** (≥10 axillary LN).\n  - The category holds even with a plausible miscount. Level III involvement would qualify as N3a by itself but cannot be assessed here.\n  - ypN3b/c cannot be assessed because the internal mammary (IMN) and supraclavicular basins were not sampled.\n  - With a 50-node yield, LNR 0.52 means more than half the nodal basin is involved: high-volume residual nodal disease.\n  - 0.52 falls in the intermediate band (0.21–0.65) of published LNR schemes. Those schemes come mainly from surgery-first cohorts, so the absolute count (≥10) remains the main descriptor.\n- **ENE:** ENE >2 mm does not change the AJCC 8th N category. It is an adverse feature associated with regional recurrence and is relevant to regional RT decisions.\n- **Treatment response**\n  - No involved node shows regression features, so there is no nodal response. If NAC was given, this is chemoresistant nodal disease.\n  - The soft-tissue fat necrosis, foreign-body reaction and scarring fit a prior procedure better than therapy effect (not confirmed).\n  - No therapy-related atypia is described, so NG3 probably reflects the tumour's intrinsic grade. The grade used for staging must still come from the pretreatment core.\n- **RCB**\n  - The nodal component is [4(1 − 0.75^26) × 10]^0.17 = **1.87**, so the class cannot be lower than RCB-II.\n  - RCB-III needs f_inv × d_prim > ~1.03 mm in the breast. That is almost any measurable residual invasive carcinoma, e.g. more than ~10% invasive carcinoma cellularity over a 10 × 10 mm bed.\n  - With no nodal response, residual breast disease is likely and RCB-III is plausible. It remains an extrapolation until the tumour-bed data are added.\n  - Either class excludes pCR and indicates moderate-to-extensive residual burden.\n- **Stage**\n  - ypTX ypN3a; M not stated.\n  - AJCC 8th pathological prognostic stage groups do not apply after NAT, and there is no separate post-therapy stage-group table.\n  - On the anatomic table, any T with N3 and M0 is **IIIC**, so ypT cannot change the group if the patient is cM0. If M1 (including M1 before therapy), the stage is IV.\n  - The AJCC prognostic grouping that applies is the pretreatment clinical prognostic stage, which needs the prior grade and ER/PR/HER2.\n- **Overall prognosis**\n  - ypN3a, ENE >2 mm, NG3, no nodal response and RCB at least II (probably III) in a 41-year-old add up to very high-risk residual disease.\n  - RCB-III and ypN3 are associated with the worst long-term outcomes after NAC across subtypes.\n  - How high the risk is, and which post-neoadjuvant treatments apply, depend on subtype. Neither can be defined until ER/PR/HER2 are retrieved.\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n1. **Integrate the breast resection** (it may be under a separate accession).\n   - Collect: ypT (largest contiguous invasive focus), tumour-bed d1 × d2, %CA, %CIS, LVI and margins.\n   - Recalculate RCB with the MD Anderson calculator: LN = 26 (plus positive nodes from any prior SLNB) and d_met = 10 mm (more if contiguous ENE applies).\n   - Amend the report with the RCB index and class. If the breast term is 0 (ypT0/is), the class is RCB-II (1.87).\n   - If no breast tumour-bed data will be available, state that RCB cannot be fully calculated and report the nodal parameters (LN = 26; d_met = 10 mm).\n2. **Retrieve the pretreatment core biopsy.**\n   - Pathology: WHO histotype, Nottingham grade, ER/PR (% and intensity; ER-low 1–10%), HER2 (IHC 0/1+/2+/3+ ± ISH), Ki-67.\n   - Clinical: baseline cTNM, and whether an axillary node was biopsy-proven and clipped.\n3. **Consider repeating ER/PR/HER2 (± Ki-67) on the largest nodal deposit.** There is abundant viable tumour without therapy effect. This matters most if baseline HER2 was negative or equivocal, ER was low or negative, or the core was small. ASCO/CAP allow repeat HER2 testing on the resection when the baseline core was negative and the tumour is grade 3.\n4. **If breast origin was never confirmed histologically:** GATA3/TRPS1 (± SOX10 if triple-negative). Add E-cadherin only if lobular morphology is being considered.\n5. **Addendum after review:**\n   - Nodes: verify node separation and count; macro/micro/ITC numbers.\n   - ENE: number of nodes with ENE; ENE vs separate deposit; whether the 10 mm measurement includes ENE.\n   - Clip/marker: identify it, with a specimen radiograph if available.\n   - Negative nodes: look for fibrosis or histiocytic aggregates in the 24 negative nodes (CAP/ICCR treatment-effect categories).\n   - Soft tissue: reconcile \"foreign-body reaction\" with \"cicatricial change\".\n6. **Clinical correlation:**\n   - NAT regimen, dates and whether it was completed.\n   - Any prior SLNB or other axillary procedure.\n   - Side of the primary.\n   - Distant staging to establish cM.\n   - Imaging of the level III, supraclavicular and IMN basins (ypN3b/c; RT planning).\n7. **MDT:**\n   - Confirm germline BRCA1/2 status (age 41; high-risk residual disease).\n   - If the tumour is triple-negative or HER2+, consider reporting stromal TILs in the residual disease.",
