        "summary": "# Spectra Single Report - report_2605363_EN - Pathology Interpretation\n\n## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n\n- **Incomplete staging source:** This report contains axillary dissection only, without primary breast specimen (pT). Nodal stage can be assigned, but overall pathological stage cannot be derived from this specimen alone.\n- **RCB class provisional / methodologically limited:** Report states TREATMENT RESPONSE INDEX FOR THIS DISSECTION: RCB III. Formal RCB requires primary tumour bed dimensions, cellularity, in situ component plus nodal parameters. RCB class cannot be validly calculated from dissection alone; treat as reported estimate only.\n- **Neoadjuvant context unclear:** Report assesses treatment response and RCB but provides no systemic therapy history. If treatment-naive, \"no signs of treatment response\" is expected and non-informative. Clinical correlation required to determine whether this is post-neoadjuvant residual disease vs primary surgery.\n- **Biomarkers not available in this report:** COMMENTS states biomarkers were performed on a prior study and not repeated. Results are not included here. Receptor/HER2 status is therefore unknown from this source; no surrogate profile can be inferred from nuclear grade.\n- **Histologic type not classifiable:** Diagnosis is metastasis from \"nuclear grade 3 carcinoma\" without WHO 5th edition histologic type. Primary tumour type cannot be classified from nodal morphology as described.\n- **High nodal yield to verify:** 50 lymph nodes from a single 12 x 8 x 6 cm unoriented right axillary dissection in 19 blocks is unusually high; consider fragmentation vs true count, but accept as reported pending block review.\n- **Fat necrosis / foreign body reaction / cicatricial change vs therapy effect:** Soft tissues show fat necrosis, foreign body reaction and cicatricial change, yet no signs of treatment response in nodes. Distinction between post-procedural change and therapy-related fibrosis requires correlation with prior biopsy/surgery and therapy history.\n- **Extranodal extension reporting:** Perinodal soft tissue involvement up to 5 millimeters constitutes extranodal extension (ENE); extent and margin relevance should be confirmed, as no orientation markers were received.\n\nNone of the above invalidates the nodal metastasis diagnosis; all require correlation rather than re-diagnosis.\n\n## SPECIMEN\n\nRight axillary dissection, 41-year-old female, Request No. 2605363 (Admission 04/29/2026, Report 05/05/2026) — 50 lymph nodes examined.\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\n\nAxillary nodal metastatic carcinoma, nuclear grade 3, involving 26 of 50 nodes with extranodal extension, no histologic treatment response identified in nodes — pN3a (AJCC 8th), reported RCB III (see limitations).\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n\n- **Nodal ratio:** 26/50 positive (52%) with macrometastasis.\n- **Largest deposit size:** 1 centimeter.\n- **Extranodal extension (ENE):** Present — perinodal soft tissue involvement in an area of up to 5 millimeters.\n- **Nuclear grade:** 3 in metastatic carcinoma.\n- **Treatment response:** No signs of treatment response observed in evaluated nodes.\n- **RCB class:** Reported as RCB III for this dissection; formal RCB not calculable without primary tumour bed data.\n- **Biomarker status:** Not present in this report; prior study referenced without results — unknown (ER/PR/HER2/Ki-67 not assignable here).\n- **Additional tissue findings:** Fat necrosis, foreign body reaction, cicatricial change in soft tissues.\n\n## INTERPRETATION\n\nNodal burden is high and prognostically adverse: 26 macrometastatic axillary nodes meets AJCC 8th edition pN3a threshold (≥10 axillary metastases). Largest deposit 1 cm confirms macrometastatic disease, not micrometastasis or ITCs. Concurrent ENE up to 5 mm is an additional adverse prognostic feature associated with increased locoregional recurrence risk and is relevant for adjuvant radiation and surgical planning, though ENE does not change AJCC 8th N category.\n\nNo histologic evidence of treatment effect was identified in nodes. In a post-neoadjuvant setting this indicates chemoresistant residual nodal disease, consistent with the reported RCB III (extensive residual burden / minimal response) concept; however, without primary breast tumour bed parameters and treatment history, formal RCB scoring and ypTNM cannot be completed. If no neoadjuvant therapy was given, absence of response has no prognostic meaning.\n\nBy WHO Classification of Tumours: Breast Tumours, 5th edition, no specific histologic type can be assigned from this report — classified only as metastatic carcinoma, nuclear grade 3. No surrogate biomarker profile is inferred; grade 3 alone does not define ER/HER2 status.\n\nOverall pathological stage: **incomplete from available data — pTX (not assessable in this specimen) pN3a**. Anatomic / prognostic AJCC stage requires pT, M, and biomarkers plus prior primary pathology; isolated pN3a indicates at minimum high-stage nodal disease but formal overall stage (e.g., IIIC vs IV) cannot be assigned here. No distant metastasis data provided.\n\nIn sum: extensive persistent/high-burden axillary metastatic NG3 carcinoma with ENE and no nodal treatment response, reported as RCB III, requiring primary-site and clinical correlation for complete staging and management.\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n\n1. Retrieve and review prior breast primary pathology and prior biomarker study (ER, PR, HER2 by IHC/ISH, Ki-67) referenced in COMMENTS; do not infer profile — incorporate documented results.\n2. Correlate with clinical history: neoadjuvant chemotherapy / endocrine / anti-HER2 therapy, timing, prior axillary biopsy/surgery to explain fat necrosis / cicatricial change vs therapy effect.\n3. Correlate with primary breast tumour data: clinical/radiologic size, pT, histologic type, grade, margins, lymphovascular invasion, and imaging for residual / contralateral disease.\n4. Multidisciplinary review for post-dissection adjuvant planning given pN3a + ENE (5 mm): radiation oncology and medical oncology input; confirm ENE extent on slides if management-defining.\n5. No repeat biomarker profile on this dissection per reporting pathologist; reconsider only if prior results unavailable, discordant grade/type suspected, or trial/therapy change requires retesting, per guidelines.\n6. Confirm lymph node count / block mapping if nodal yield affects registry or trial eligibility, given 50 nodes in 19 blocks without orientation.\n7. Ensure M status and overall AJCC 8th anatomic/prognostic stage are assigned only when pT, biomarkers, and clinical M are available.",
