        "summary": "# Pathology Report Analysis — Request No. 2605363\n\n**Specimen received 04/29/2026 · Reported 05/05/2026 · Female, 41 y · Outpatient**\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n\n1. **RCB class is not derivable from this specimen.** The RCB index is a *case-level* composite requiring the bidimensional residual tumour bed size (d_prim), % overall invasive cellularity and % in situ component (f_inv) from the **breast** specimen, in addition to the nodal terms. This accession contains axillary tissue only. On the nodal data alone (LN = 26, d_met = 10 mm), the nodal term computes to ≈1.87 — **below the RCB-III threshold of >3.28**. RCB-III becomes mathematically valid only once a primary-tumour term >1.41 is added (i.e. f_inv × d_prim ≳ 1.0; e.g. a 30 mm tumour bed with ≥4% residual invasive cellularity). The report's own wording — \"treatment response index **for this dissection**\" — concedes this. **Recommend re-issuing the RCB class as provisional, or as an addendum after the breast specimen is integrated.** Clinically RCB-III is highly probable, but as currently stated it is unsupported by the data in this accession.\n2. **Neoadjuvant therapy is implied but never documented.** RCB assignment and \"no signs of treatment response\" presuppose a post-neoadjuvant setting, yet no regimen, treatment dates, or pre-treatment cT/cN are recorded. Without this, the **\"yp\" prefix cannot be formally applied** and post-therapy prognostic tools (CPS+EG) cannot be scored.\n3. **No breast specimen / no ypT.** Overall pathological stage cannot be finalised from this accession. Clarify whether the primary was resected in a separate/prior accession or remains in situ (a delayed, isolated axillary dissection).\n4. **No biomarker data in this accession.** The comment defers ER/PR/HER2 to a prior study. This is defensible for diagnosis but **debatable for management**: in chemorefractory residual disease, repeat ER/HER2 on the residual deposit carries direct therapeutic consequence (T-DM1 eligibility if HER2+; HER2-low reclassification; capecitabine/olaparib pathways if HER2−). At minimum, the prior results must be transcribed into this report so the synoptic is not left biomarker-blank.\n5. **Internal tension between \"no treatment response\" and the stromal findings.** Fat necrosis, foreign-body giant-cell reaction and cicatricial change are documented in perinodal soft tissue, yet nodal therapy effect is reported as absent. Confirm these represent procedural/clip-site or fat-necrosis change rather than under-recognised nodal regression (hyalinised fibrosis, mucin pools, foamy macrophage aggregates). The distinction is material to RCB, as treated nodes with fibrous scar but no viable tumour are scored as negative.\n6. **Grading is incomplete for classification purposes.** Only **nuclear grade 3** is given, derived from metastatic deposits. A Nottingham (Elston–Ellis) histologic grade cannot be assigned from nodal metastases, and post-NAC grading of the primary is itself of limited validity. Do not report the nuclear grade as an overall histologic grade.\n7. **Deposit measurement vs. ENE.** Node size on macroscopy (up to 1.5 cm) and largest metastasis (1.0 cm) are distinct parameters and are not in conflict — but confirm the 10 mm figure is the largest *contiguous metastatic deposit* (the value required by both AJCC and RCB) and that the 5 mm extranodal component was recorded separately rather than folded into it.\n8. **Specimen not oriented; Berg levels not separable.** N category is unaffected (≥10 positive nodes = pN3a regardless), but apical/level III status cannot be stated — relevant to regional nodal irradiation field design.\n9. **Minor:** the signature block renders as \"[ANONYMIZED PATHOLOGIST ? ONCOLOGIST]\" (character corruption); verify the authenticated signatory line.\n\n---\n\n## SPECIMEN\n\nRight total axillary lymph node dissection (50 nodes, 19 blocks); no breast/primary tumour tissue submitted in this accession.\n\n---\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\n\n**Extensive macrometastatic involvement of the right axilla (26/50 nodes) by nuclear grade 3 invasive carcinoma, most consistent with invasive breast carcinoma of no special type (NST), with extranodal extension and no histologic evidence of neoadjuvant treatment effect — (y)pN3a; anatomic Stage IIIC (M0 assumed), pending ypT. Chemorefractory residual disease; RCB-III as stated by the reporting pathologist, to be finalised on the breast specimen.**\n\n---\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n\n| Parameter | Result |\n|---|---|\n| **Nodal ratio** | **26/50 positive (LNR 0.52)** — node yield well above adequacy standards |\n| **Largest deposit** | **10 mm** (macrometastasis; all involved nodes reported as macrometastatic) |\n| **Extranodal extension** | **Present**, up to **5 mm** beyond the capsule into perinodal soft tissue |\n| **Nuclear grade** | **3** (high grade); Nottingham grade not assignable from nodal tissue |\n| **Treatment response** | **None identified** in nodes (no regressive fibrosis/acellular tumour bed described) |\n| **RCB class** | **RCB-III** per report — *axillary component only; nodal term ≈1.87 in isolation; requires breast specimen to validate* |\n| **Biomarker status** | **Not performed on this specimen**; deferred to prior study — ER/PR/HER2/Ki-67 **absent from this report** |\n| **Other** | Fat necrosis, foreign-body reaction, cicatricial change in soft tissue; 24 nodes uninvolved |\n\n---\n\n## INTERPRETATION\n\nThe axillary content defines a **high-burden, chemoresistant nodal phenotype**. Twenty-six involved nodes places this unequivocally in **AJCC 8th edition N3a** (≥10 positive axillary nodes with at least one deposit >2.0 mm); the 10 mm largest deposit independently confirms macrometastatic disease and excludes the micrometastasis/ITC categories. Because the report invokes treatment response, the category is properly rendered **ypN3a** once neoadjuvant administration is documented. With M0, **any ypT combined with N3 yields anatomic Stage IIIC**; this is the ceiling of locoregional staging and is insensitive to primary tumour size, so the missing ypT does not alter the anatomic stage group — only the ypT descriptor and the AJCC *prognostic* stage group remain outstanding. The prognostic stage group requires ER/PR/HER2 and grade: a grade 3, HR+/HER2− or triple-negative profile holds the case at Stage IIIC, whereas a favourable ER+/PR+/HER2+ profile can downstage it under the AJCC 8th pathological prognostic tables — a further reason the biomarker set must be reconciled into this report.\n\nThe **extranodal extension of 5 mm** is an adverse modifier that does not change the N category in breast carcinoma but materially affects management. It exceeds the 2 mm threshold used in most axillary-management and radiotherapy datasets, correlates with residual nodal burden beyond the dissected field, and, in combination with N3a disease, is a firm indication for comprehensive post-mastectomy/regional nodal irradiation including supraclavicular and internal mammary consideration. The absence of specimen orientation prevents a statement on level III/apical involvement, which should be conveyed to radiation oncology as a planning uncertainty rather than a negative finding.\n\nThe **treatment-response assessment is the dominant prognostic driver**. Complete absence of regressive change across 26 involved nodes, with retained nuclear grade 3 morphology, indicates intrinsic chemoresistance rather than partial cytoreduction. Post-neoadjuvant nodal status is the strongest surviving prognostic variable in this setting, and ypN3a with no response occupies the poorest stratum: event-free and overall survival are substantially inferior to any pCR or low-RCB outcome, and the risk is front-loaded to the first three years. The scarring, fat necrosis and foreign-body reaction in perinodal fat are most plausibly procedural (biopsy/clip site) or degenerative, and should not be over-read as therapy effect — but they warrant deliberate exclusion of subtle nodal regression before \"no response\" is finalised, since converting even a few nodes to \"treated/negative\" would lower both the LN count and the RCB index.\n\nRegarding **RCB**, the index as reported must be read as an **axillary-only, provisional estimate**. Substituting LN = 26 and d_met = 10 mm into the nodal term gives approximately 1.87, which falls in the RCB-II range; the class advances to RCB-III only when the breast tumour-bed term is added. Given that any measurable residual primary with even minimal cellularity pushes the composite past 3.28, **RCB-III is the expected final class**, but it should be issued as such only in an integrated report or addendum that records d_prim, % invasive cellularity and % in situ. RCB-III concordantly identifies the group with the least benefit derived from neoadjuvant therapy and the greatest need for escalated adjuvant systemic treatment.\n\nOn **tumour typing**, the metastases are described as cohesive macrometastatic deposits of nuclear grade 3 carcinoma, without the discohesive, single-file or signet-ring morphology that would suggest a lobular primary, and without micropapillary, mucinous or apocrine features. On the available morphology the primary is best classified as **invasive breast carcinoma of no special type (NST), high grade**, per WHO 5th edition; this remains provisional pending review of the primary. As to the **likely biomarker profile**, a grade 3 tumour showing complete chemorefractoriness with massive nodal burden is most compatible with **HR-positive/HER2-negative, luminal B-like biology**, in which low pCR rates and RCB-III outcomes predominate; triple-negative disease is less likely given that non-response is the minority behaviour in that subtype, but if present it would carry the most adverse implication and would open the capecitabine pathway. HER2-positive disease is the least likely but the **most consequential to miss**, since unresponsive HER2+ residual disease is a direct indication for adjuvant T-DM1. This uncertainty is precisely why the deferral of repeat biomarker testing should be revisited. At 41 years the patient is presumptively premenopausal, which bears on ovarian function suppression and on germline testing thresholds.\n\n---\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n\n1. **Integrate the breast specimen** (same or prior accession) to establish ypT, tumour bed bidimensional dimensions, % overall invasive cellularity and % in situ component; recompute the RCB index formally (MD Anderson calculator) and issue the definitive class by addendum. Complete the full ypTNM and AJCC 8th prognostic stage group.\n2. **Document neoadjuvant details** — regimen, cycles, start/stop dates, pre-treatment cT/cN by imaging and core biopsy — to license the \"yp\" prefix and to permit **CPS+EG scoring**, which meaningfully refines prognosis in ypN3 residual disease.\n3. **Retrieve and transcribe prior ER/PR/HER2/Ki-67 and the original histologic type and Nottingham grade** into this report so the synoptic is complete.\n4. **Reconsider repeat ER/PR/HER2 (ASCO/CAP-compliant IHC ± reflex ISH) on the residual nodal metastasis.** Chemorefractory residual disease has a non-trivial receptor-discordance rate, and the result is actionable: T-DM1 (KATHERINE) if HER2+; capecitabine (CREATE-X) if HER2−/TNBC; HER2-low status for future T-DXd eligibility. Select the block containing the 10 mm deposit.\n5. **Level-sections ± pancytokeratin (AE1/AE3) on nodes showing fibrous scar or hyalinisation**, to confirm that \"no treatment response\" is accurate and that no treated/regressed nodes have been miscategorised. Routine IHC on morphologically uninvolved nodes is not otherwise indicated given established macrometastases.\n6. **E-cadherin ± p120 catenin** if re-review of the metastatic deposits shows any discohesive or single-file pattern, to exclude an invasive lobular primary.\n7. **Confirm retrieval of the clipped/marked node** and verify laterality concordance with the right breast primary; clarify whether levels I–III were all submitted despite the absence of orientation.\n8. **Germline BRCA1/2 and multigene panel testing** — age 41 with high-grade, high-burden residual disease; also determines **olaparib (OlympiA)** eligibility in HER2− high-risk residual disease.\n9. **Systemic staging** (CT chest/abdomen/pelvis with bone scan, or FDG PET-CT) to exclude occult M1 disease before adjuvant planning, given N3a burden with extranodal extension.\n10. **Multidisciplinary tumour board referral** for escalated adjuvant systemic therapy and for radiotherapy planning (comprehensive regional nodal irradiation, with the level III/apical and internal mammary uncertainty explicitly communicated).\n11. Genomic recurrence-risk assays (Oncotype DX/MammaPrint) are **not indicated** in node-positive residual disease after neoadjuvant therapy.\n\n---\n\n*Interpretive analysis generated to support the attending pathologist; not a substitute for the authenticated diagnostic report. All conclusions are limited to the material and data contained in accession 2605363.*",
