        "summary": "CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n- Methodological limitation: RCB class assigned as RCB III based solely on axillary dissection (50 LNs, 26 positive) without primary breast tumor bed parameters (tumor bed size, cellularity, % DCIS). Formal RCB per Symmans et al. cannot be calculated from nodal material alone; current RCB III should be qualified as extensive residual nodal disease / nodal-only RCB equivalent.\n- Specimen limitation: Unoriented irregular adipose fragment 12x8x6 cm, 50 LNs up to 1.5 cm processed in 19 blocks (>1 LN/block). Potential undersampling for micrometastases/ITC and subtle treatment response. No pT available for overall staging.\n- Interpretive nuance: Soft tissues described as fat necrosis, foreign body reaction, and cicatricial change, yet microscopic assessment states no signs of treatment response. Requires clinical correlation to exclude iatrogenic/post-biopsy change vs therapy effect. No regression fibrosis, foamy macrophages, or other therapy-related histology reported in nodes.\n- Staging dependency: Overall pathological stage cannot be fully derived from nodal dissection alone without primary tumor pT and clinical M. pN category alone drives stage.\n- None other; 26 macrometastases clearly documented, ENE documented.\n\nSPECIMEN\nRight axillary lymph node dissection, unoriented (admission 04/29/2026, Report 05/05/2026, Req 2605363, 41-year-old female): 12 x 8 x 6 cm adipose tissue, 50 LNs dissected, 19 blocks.\n\nAI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\nMetastatic invasive breast carcinoma of no special type (NST), nuclear grade 3, involving axillary nodes - 26 of 50 nodes with macrometastasis, largest deposit 1.0 cm, extranodal extension present up to 5 mm, no histologic evidence of treatment response, compatible with nodal residual burden RCB III / chemoresistant disease - ypN3a (pN3a if de novo) - at least Stage IIIC if M0 pending pT.\n\nKEY FINDINGS\n- Nodal ratio: 26/50 positive = 52%, all macrometastases per report (largest 1.0 cm / 10 mm, >2 mm threshold for macro).\n- Largest deposit size: 1.0 cm (10 mm) metastasis; nodes up to 1.5 cm gross.\n- Extranodal extension (ENE): Present, perinodal soft tissue involvement up to 5 mm - extensive ENE (>2 mm) per AJCC 8th prognostic factor, CAP reporting element.\n- Nuclear grade: Grade 3 carcinoma in nodal metastases (high-grade, poor differentiation).\n- Treatment response: No signs of treatment response observed in evaluated nodes per diagnosis; no tumor bed fibrosis, necrosis, or histiocytic response attributed to therapy.\n- RCB class: Reported as RCB III (no signs of treatment response) - extensive residual disease, chemoresistant category; however calculation is nodal-only, not full RCB.\n- Biomarker status: Not performed on current sample per Comments (\"biomarkers were performed on a prior study\"). Inferred most likely high-grade basal-like triple-negative phenotype (ER-/PR-/HER2-) or HER2-enriched high-grade carcinoma based on nuclear grade 3, age 41, extensive nodal burden, and lack of treatment response. Definitive profile requires review of prior biomarker report. If primary is invasive carcinoma NST grade 3, Nottingham grade 3 equivalent.\n\nINTERPRETATION\nThis is an extensive axillary disease burden specimen: Right axillary dissection without orientation yielding 50 LNs, 26 with metastatic carcinoma of nuclear grade 3. By WHO Classification of Breast Tumours 5th edition, nodal morphology of high-grade carcinoma with no special features documented is most consistent with invasive breast carcinoma of no special type (NST), grade 3 (ductal type). Lobular phenotype not suggested; verification with primary tumor histology required.\n\nAJCC 8th edition nodal staging: ≥10 positive ipsilateral axillary nodes = pN3a. With 26 positive nodes, category is pN3a. In post-neoadjuvant setting (implied by RCB reporting attempt), designation is ypN3a, with 26 ypN+ and extensive treatment-resistant disease. All deposits are macrometastases (>2 mm), largest 10 mm. ENE present with perinodal soft tissue involvement 5 mm meets criteria for extensive extranodal extension, adverse prognostic factor for locoregional recurrence and survival, does not upstage N per AJCC but is mandatory CAP/AJCC prognostic element.\n\nTreatment response and Residual Cancer Burden: Histologically no regression changes in nodes. In post-neoadjuvant context this indicates lack of nodal response. Reported as RCB III - by Symmans criteria, RCB III = extensive residual disease, poor prognosis, chemoresistant. True RCB requires primary tumor bed cellularity, dimensions, and in situ percentage in addition to nodal number/size of largest metastasis. With nodal-only data, RCB III here reflects high residual nodal burden (26 nodes, 1 cm largest deposit) consistent with RCB-III category clinically, but not a validated total RCB score.\n\nStaging synthesis: Without breast specimen, pT = pTX. Assuming M0, with pN3a, overall pathological stage is at least pStage IIIC (AJCC 8th: any T N3 M0 = IIIC; T1-3 N3a = IIIC) if invasive primary present. If no prior NAT, pN3a. If post-NAT, ypN3a consistent with ypStage IIIC residual disease. Prognostically, nodal ratio 52%, 26 macrometastatic nodes, extensive ENE to 5 mm, nuclear grade 3, and RCB III imply high risk of distant metastasis and poor disease-free/overall survival. Fat necrosis and foreign body giant cell reaction with cicatricial change in axillary soft tissue likely reflects prior intervention (biopsy/surgery) rather than therapy effect given pathologist interpretation.\n\nRECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n- Correlate with clinical history: Confirm neoadjuvant therapy (NAT) status, regimen (chemotherapy +/- anti-HER2), timing, and clinical/radiologic response to validate yp vs p staging and appropriateness of RCB reporting.\n- Retrieve and review prior breast primary pathology and prior biomarker report referenced in Comments: histologic type, Nottingham grade, ER/PR/HER2 IHC and HER2 ISH if equivocal, Ki-67%. Given nuclear grade 3, most likely triple-negative breast carcinoma (TNBC) NST or HER2-positive high-grade carcinoma; do not repeat without indication but ensure prior report is available in case.\n- If no prior biomarkers available or if clinically indicated (e.g., initial core was triple-negative/HER2-low, or long interval), consider biomarker retesting on nodal metastasis (ER, PR, HER2, Ki-67) per ASCO/CAP guidelines for metastatic recurrence, with appropriate controls.\n- Confirm M status clinically; document overall AJCC 8th prognostic stage once pT from breast surgery is available, or cT from clinical exam/imaging if breast not resected. Requires multidisciplinary tumor board discussion given pN3a / ypN3a RCB-III.\n- Report ENE extent (5 mm) in synoptic format as extensive ENE >2 mm; consider comment on margin/soft tissue involvement status for radiation planning.\n- No additional levels/IHC needed on current axillary material unless occult micrometastases questioned, given already extensive positivity; however ensure adequate sampling documentation for 50 nodes in 19 blocks meets CAP adequacy.",
