        "summary": "CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n\n- RCB-III is not valid as reported. Residual Cancer Burden is calculated from the primary tumor bed (dimensions, invasive cellularity, percent in situ) together with the positive-node count and the largest nodal metastasis. It cannot be assigned from an axillary dissection, and “no signs of treatment response” is not the definition of RCB-III (index >3.28). Using only the available nodal inputs (26 positive nodes, largest deposit 10 mm), the Symmans nodal term is approximately 1.87. That result is RCB-II if the breast is ypT0/ypTis. RCB-III requires a further primary-bed contribution above about 1.41 and is not established here.\n- Neoadjuvant therapy is implied by the response statement and the RCB label but is not documented. The yp prefix is therefore unconfirmed. If no systemic therapy was given, the category is pN3a and RCB does not apply.\n- Biomarkers are cited from a prior study and are not in this report. Repeat testing was declined. Nuclear grade 3 does not determine ER, PR, or HER2; no profile is assigned.\n- Specimen limits: no breast primary is included, so ypT/pT, WHO type, and Nottingham grade cannot be closed; there are no orientation markers, so an apical/level III node cannot be identified. The count of macrometastases already meets N3a, so missing orientation does not change the N category.\n- Response wording is inconsistent. Microscopy limits absent treatment effect to the nodes. The diagnosis states no treatment response without that qualifier and separately records fat necrosis and cicatricial change; microscopy also records foreign-body reaction. Those stromal findings are not nodal regression.\n- Nuclear grade 3 appears only in the microscopic description and is not carried into the diagnosis. Deposit sizes other than the largest are not itemized; the diagnosis does classify all 26 as macrometastases.\n\nSPECIMEN\nRight axillary dissection, unoriented, 50 lymph nodes (request 2605363).\n\nAI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\nMetastatic nuclear grade 3 carcinoma in 26/50 right axillary nodes, macrometastases, largest deposit 10 mm, extranodal extension 5 mm — AJCC 8th edition ypN3a if neoadjuvant therapy is confirmed, otherwise pN3a; anatomic stage IIIC if M0. Provisional WHO 5th edition correlation: invasive breast carcinoma, no special type. Reported RCB-III is not accepted.\n\nKEY FINDINGS\n- Nodal ratio: 26/50 (52%; LNR 0.52)\n- Largest deposit: 10 mm (1 cm), macrometastasis\n- Extranodal extension: present, up to 5 mm (reported separately from deposit size; not summed)\n- Nuclear grade: 3 (Nottingham histologic grade not given)\n- Treatment response: no response in the nodes\n- RCB class: not assignable; reported RCB-III unsupported (nodal term ~1.87 → RCB-II if no residual invasive breast carcinoma)\n- Biomarkers: not in this report. No result inferred. Grade 3 remains compatible with triple-negative, HER2-positive, or ER-positive high-grade disease; the prior study must be used.\n\nINTERPRETATION\nTwenty-six macrometastases in 50 axillary nodes meet AJCC 8th edition N3a (≥10 axillary metastases, at least one >2 mm). The largest deposit is 10 mm. Extranodal extension of 5 mm is an adverse locoregional finding but is not an N-category modifier in the breast chapter and does not raise the category above N3a. No internal mammary or supraclavicular nodes are in this specimen, so the pathologic category from this dissection is N3a rather than N3b or N3c. Clinical non-axillary nodal disease is not assessed here.\n\nIf neoadjuvant systemic therapy is confirmed, the category is ypN3a; otherwise it is pN3a. Anatomic stage group is IIIC for any T, including T0/Tis, with N3 and M0. Missing breast-tumor size therefore does not change the anatomic stage group, but M status is not in this report, so IIIC stands only if M0. Prognostic stage group cannot be assigned: ER, PR, and HER2 are absent, and only nuclear grade — not Nottingham grade — is available. Nuclear grade 3 does not prove histologic grade 3.\n\nThe nodes show viable metastatic carcinoma without treatment effect. In a post-neoadjuvant setting that is residual, non-responding nodal disease. It excludes pathologic complete response. RCB-0 requires no invasive carcinoma in breast and nodes; RCB-I is an index ≤1.36. Neither applies. It is still not RCB-III. The nodal term alone (~1.87) falls in RCB-II (index >1.36 and ≤3.28) when the breast tumor bed contributes nothing. More than minimal residual invasive carcinoma in the breast would push the combined index above 3.28, so RCB-III is possible but not demonstrated. The issued “RCB III (no signs of treatment response)” equates a qualitative nodal statement with a quantitative class and should not be used for prognosis until the primary bed is included (positive-node count 26, largest nodal metastasis 10 mm).\n\nAbsolute nodal count already places this in the highest non-metastatic N category; LNR 0.52 is supplementary and does not restage the case. Five-millimeter extranodal extension adds locoregional risk without an AJCC upstage. Absent nodal regression, if therapy was given, is further adverse. Phenotype is open, so the systemic prognostic group is open. Among nuclear grade 3 carcinomas the prior biomarker study should be read for triple-negative, HER2-positive, and ER-positive high-grade (luminal B) disease; these share high nuclear grade and differ in management implications. None is established on this accession.\n\nWHO Classification of Tumours, Breast Tumours, 5th edition: the nodal text describes only a nuclear grade 3 carcinoma, with no architecture and no special-type features. Invasive breast carcinoma of no special type is the provisional corresponding category, not a type proven on this material. Pleomorphic lobular carcinoma and other special types are not excluded. Primary laterality is not stated.\n\nSoft-tissue fat necrosis, foreign-body reaction, and cicatricial change are separate from the nodal metastases and are not scored as nodal treatment effect.\n\nRECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n- Link the prior biopsy or resection: histologic type, Nottingham grade, and ER/PR/HER2 (Ki-67 if performed). Do not substitute a grade-based profile. Repeat receptors on the metastasis only if that study is unavailable, inadequate, or equivocal.\n- Correlate the breast primary for ypT/pT, tumor-bed dimensions, invasive cellularity, and percent in situ, and recalculate RCB with LN = 26 and d_met = 10 mm. Withdraw standalone RCB-III.\n- Confirm whether neoadjuvant systemic therapy was given, and the regimen, before locking yp versus p. If none was given, drop RCB.\n- Clinical or radiologic M category to separate anatomic stage IIIC from IV.\n- Reconcile stromal fat necrosis, foreign-body reaction, and cicatrix with prior biopsy, clip, or surgery.\n- Confirm the known primary diagnosis and laterality against this right axillary metastasis; lineage studies only if the prior diagnosis cannot be retrieved.\n- Carry nuclear grade 3 and extranodal extension (5 mm) into the diagnosis line for tumor-board use. Level III status cannot be recovered from this unoriented specimen and is not required for N3a.\n- Age 41 years: germline-testing correlation once phenotype is known, per institutional criteria.",
