        "summary": "CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n- Axillary dissection only: no breast primary in this specimen; pT cannot be assigned here (ypTX). Standard RCB requires primary tumour-bed dimensions and cellularity; RCB III is stated for this dissection alone.\n- Histologic subtype not specified (reported only as nuclear grade 3 carcinoma); biomarkers not repeated and prior results are not in this report.\n- Soft-tissue fat necrosis, foreign-body reaction, and cicatricial change coexist with explicit absence of treatment effect in involved nodes—correlate with prior axillary procedure versus true nodal non-response.\n- 26 macrometastases / 50 nodes with 5 mm ENE and zero nodal regression after presumed NAC is internally consistent with the stated RCB III but is an aggressive residual-disease profile requiring confirmation of M status and the prior biomarker study.\n\nSPECIMEN\nRight axillary dissection (12 × 8 × 6 cm; 50 nodes in 19 blocks), post-treatment (yp), 41-year-old woman.\n\nAI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\nypN3a (AJCC 8th) residual high-grade invasive carcinoma NST, 26/50 axillary macrometastases with 5 mm ENE, no nodal treatment effect, RCB III; overall ypStage IIIC (ypTX ypN3a, M not assessed on this specimen).\n\nKEY FINDINGS\n- Nodal ratio: 26/50 (52%); all reported as macrometastases → ypN3a\n- Largest deposit: 10 mm\n- ENE: present, 5 mm (extensive, >2 mm)\n- Nuclear grade: 3\n- Treatment response: none in evaluated nodes\n- RCB class: III (extensive residual disease)\n- Biomarkers: not performed on this sample (prior study cited, results not provided). Most likely profile given NG3 plus chemo-resistant residual burden: ER-positive / HER2-negative, luminal B-like; confirm on the prior report (TNBC and HER2+ remain possible but less typical of zero nodal response).\n\nINTERPRETATION\nThis is a post-neoadjuvant right axillary clearance with heavy residual nodal disease. Twenty-six macrometastases among 50 recovered nodes fulfil AJCC 8th ypN3a (≥10 axillary nodes, deposit >2 mm). The 10 mm largest deposit and 5 mm extranodal extension indicate established macrometastatic, extensively extra-capsular disease; ENE does not upstage beyond N3a in breast AJCC 8th but is an adverse locoregional risk factor.\n\nAbsence of treatment effect in involved nodes, together with RCB III assigned on this dissection, indicates chemo-resistant residual cancer. The lymph-node RCB component alone (LN = 26, dmet = 10 mm) is sufficient for the extensive-residual (RCB-III) range even without a scored primary bed. Combined with unknown pT (ypTX) and no distant-disease assessment in this report, the nodal category drives overall pathological stage ypIIIC if M0.\n\nNodal morphology (NG3 carcinoma without described lobular, papillary, or other special-type features) supports metastatic invasive carcinoma of no special type (WHO Breast Tumours, 5th ed.). Fat necrosis and cicatricial/foreign-body change in surrounding soft tissue are not interpreted as nodal regression.\n\nPrognostically this is unfavourable residual disease: high nodal ratio, N3a, extensive ENE, high nuclear grade, and RCB III. In a 41-year-old woman this residual burden warrants multidisciplinary review for adjuvant systemic intensification, regional RT planning (axilla/SCF), and restaging as clinically indicated.\n\nRECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n- Retrieve and report the prior biomarker profile (ER/PR/HER2/Ki-67, plus HER2 IHC/ISH per ASCO/CAP); repeat on residual nodal tumour only if the original result is unavailable, equivocal, or clinically discordant.\n- Correlate with the breast primary (core and/or excision/mastectomy): histologic type, ypT, tumour-bed cellularity, LVI, margins, and a complete (primary + nodal) RCB score.\n- Confirm clinical/radiologic M status; if M0, document ypT_ ypN3a cM0 = ypStage IIIC.\n- Multidisciplinary correlation for adjuvant therapy and regional radiotherapy given ypN3a + ENE 5 mm + RCB III.\n- Consider germline genetic counselling/testing context (age 41, high-grade, heavy nodal burden) per institutional protocol, without delaying the above pathologic correlations.",
