        "summary": "# AI Assistant Interpretation — Request No. 2605363\n\n## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n- **No primary (breast) tumour specimen in this report.** Only the right axillary dissection is documented; ypT category, margin status, and primary-tumour-bed parameters (residual invasive size, cellularity, %DCIS) are not available here, so the AJCC prognostic stage group and the RCB score cannot be independently verified from this document alone — RCB is a composite index requiring both primary-tumour-bed and nodal data.\n- **RCB‑III is stated as the \"treatment response index for this dissection\"** — confirm that this value already incorporates the concurrently resected primary tumour bed (from a companion specimen/report) rather than being derived from nodal data in isolation.\n- **Biomarker (ER/PR/HER2 ± Ki‑67) results are not reported here**; the comment defers to an unspecified \"prior study\" without citing values or accession number — creates a documentation gap for the current episode of care.\n- **No orientation markers** on the macroscopic specimen limit anatomic-level mapping (I/II/III) of the 26 positive nodes; level III (infraclavicular) involvement cannot be confirmed or excluded from this report, which is relevant to definitive pN3a sub-classification.\n- **Fat necrosis, foreign-body reaction, and cicatricial change** are noted — consistent with prior treatment effect but could obscure additional small metastatic foci; extent of sampling/levels examined per node not specified.\n- No explicit statement regarding distant metastasis (M category); M0 is assumed pending clinical/radiologic correlation.\n\n## SPECIMEN\nRight axillary lymph node dissection, 50 nodes, post-neoadjuvant chemotherapy setting (residual cancer burden assessment).\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\nResidual metastatic high-grade (nuclear grade 3) carcinoma, morphology consistent with invasive carcinoma of no special type (NST), in 26 of 50 right axillary lymph nodes, with extranodal (perinodal) extension and no evidence of pathologic treatment response — consistent with **ypN3a**, **RCB-III**.\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n| Parameter | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Nodal ratio | 26/50 positive (52%) |\n| Largest metastatic deposit | 1 cm (macrometastasis, >2 mm) |\n| Extranodal extension | Present, up to 5 mm into perinodal soft tissue |\n| Nuclear grade | Grade 3 (high grade) in metastatic foci |\n| Treatment response | None identified — non-responding nodal disease post-NAC |\n| RCB class | RCB-III (per report, for this dissection) |\n| Biomarker status | Not performed on current specimen; deferred to prior (unspecified) study. Not directly stated here — if correlation is required, a grade 3 tumour showing **no pathologic response to neoadjuvant chemotherapy** is statistically more consistent with a hormone receptor–positive/HER2-negative (luminal, high-grade) profile than with triple-negative or HER2-enriched disease, which more typically show higher pCR rates; this is an inference for correlation, not a confirmed result. |\n\n## INTERPRETATION\nTwenty-six of 50 axillary nodes (52% nodal ratio) harbour macrometastatic, grade 3 carcinoma with a largest deposit of 1 cm and extranodal extension up to 5 mm, with no morphologic evidence of chemotherapy-related change. Under AJCC 8th edition criteria, ≥10 positive axillary nodes with at least one deposit >2 mm satisfies **ypN3a**, independent of the presence of extranodal extension (ENE is not a formal N-modifier in the current breast staging schema but is a recognized adverse locoregional-recurrence risk factor and radiotherapy-planning consideration). Per the AJCC 8th edition anatomic staging table, any T category with N3/M0 corresponds to **anatomic Stage IIIC**; therefore, pending confirmation of M0 status and despite the primary tumour (ypT) not being documented in this specimen, the axillary findings alone are sufficient to place this case at a minimum of ypStage IIIC. Final AJCC *prognostic* stage grouping (which additionally weighs grade and ER/PR/HER2 status) cannot be finalized until the primary tumour bed report and confirmed biomarker profile are available.\n\nThe absence of any treatment effect in the nodal disease, combined with a high nodal burden and ENE, corresponds to **RCB-III**, the residual-disease category associated with the least favorable event-free and overall survival outcomes among the RCB classes, particularly when paired with HR+/HER2− or triple-negative biology. Taken together — ypN3a, extranodal extension, non-response to neoadjuvant chemotherapy, and RCB-III — this profile denotes extensive, high-grade, chemoresistant regional nodal disease with substantial risk for both locoregional recurrence (supporting regional nodal irradiation) and distant relapse, warranting consideration of escalated/alternative adjuvant systemic therapy once the confirmed receptor profile is available.\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n- Correlate with the concurrent primary breast tumour resection specimen (ypT category, margins, residual cellularity, %in-situ component) to complete and validate the RCB calculation and finalize AJCC anatomic/prognostic staging.\n- Retrieve and document the actual ER/PR/HER2 (and Ki‑67, if performed) results from the \"prior study\" referenced in the comment section, including report/accession identifier, for the permanent record of this episode.\n- Consider re-testing biomarkers on the residual (post-NAC) tumour if there is any clinical suspicion of receptor discordance, per ASCO/CAP guidance for residual disease after neoadjuvant therapy.\n- Correlate with the pretreatment core-biopsy report for baseline histologic subtype, grade, and biomarker status to assess pre-/post-NAC concordance.\n- Clinical and imaging correlation to exclude infraclavicular (level III)/supraclavicular nodal disease and to formally establish M category for complete staging.\n- Confirm total nodal yield/sampling adequacy and levels examined given the absence of orientation markers on the specimen.\n- Multidisciplinary tumor board discussion regarding regional nodal irradiation (given ENE) and escalation of adjuvant systemic therapy (e.g., capecitabine, T-DM1, or CDK4/6 inhibition depending on confirmed receptor/HER2 status) in the context of RCB-III/non-response.",
