        "summary": "## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n- **RCB III is reported for the dissection alone.** Breast tumour-bed dimensions, residual cellularity and in situ fraction are unavailable; formal RCB cannot be independently verified. Absence of nodal treatment effect alone does not define RCB III.\n- Prior biomarkers are referenced but not supplied. **ER/PR/HER2 status and molecular phenotype cannot be inferred from nuclear grade 3.**\n- Neoadjuvant treatment history, primary-tumour data and distant-metastasis status are absent. Nuclear grade 3 must not be equated with Nottingham histological grade 3.\n\n## SPECIMEN\nRight axillary dissection; report 2605363, dated 05/05/2026.\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\nExtensive right axillary macrometastatic carcinoma, nuclear grade 3, with extranodal extension. Mammary origin requires primary-tumour correlation; the supplied nodal description does not establish a specific WHO (5th edition) breast carcinoma histotype.\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n| Parameter | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Nodal burden | **26/50** nodes with macrometastases; ratio **0.52 (52%)** |\n| Largest metastatic deposit | **10 mm (1 cm)** |\n| Extranodal extension | Present; perinodal soft-tissue involvement **up to 5 mm** |\n| Nuclear grade | **3**; Nottingham grade not established |\n| Treatment response | No histological treatment response identified in evaluated nodes |\n| RCB | **Class III, as reported; not independently validated** |\n| Biomarkers | Prior profile referenced; ER, PR, HER2 and Ki-67 results unavailable here |\n\n## INTERPRETATION\nFor a breast primary, the 26 macrometastatic axillary nodes meet **AJCC 8th-edition pN3a** criteria; use **ypN3a** if neoadjuvant systemic therapy is confirmed. Extranodal extension is an adverse feature but does not change the N3a category. Primary pT/ypT is unavailable. With clinically established **M0**, N3 disease maps to **anatomic stage IIIC (any T)**; without M status, an overall stage group cannot be finalized. A biomarker-based pathological prognostic stage is unsupported and should not be extrapolated to a post-neoadjuvant specimen.\n\nThe high nodal burden and extranodal extension indicate extensive regional disease and adverse pathological prognostic features. If post-neoadjuvant, substantial residual nodal carcinoma remains without demonstrable nodal treatment effect; this does not establish the breast primary's response. **RCB III**, if validated using integrated breast and nodal data, denotes extensive residual cancer burden; it is not synonymous with absence of treatment response.\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n- Confirm treatment sequence and retrieve primary biopsy/resection findings to establish mammary origin, WHO histotype, Nottingham grade, pT/ypT and primary-site response.\n- Obtain the original ER/PR/HER2 results and Ki-67 if performed; assess concordance before considering repeat testing.\n- Verify the integrated RCB index/class using breast tumour-bed dimensions, residual cellularity and in situ fraction together with the nodal data.\n- Correlate with clinical/radiological distant staging and pretreatment staging to complete the appropriate TNM assessment.",
