        "summary": "## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n- **RCB III is assigned to the dissection**, but no breast tumour-bed measurements, cellularity, in-situ fraction or RCB score are supplied. Whole-case RCB III cannot be verified from this nodal-only report; absent nodal regression does not establish absent breast response. Fat necrosis/scarring is not documented as regression of nodal tumour.\n- The breast primary, prior biomarker results, treatment chronology and distant-disease status are not supplied. Breast histotype, receptor subtype, the need for a `yp` prefix and the definitive overall stage therefore remain unconfirmed.\n\n## SPECIMEN\nRight axillary lymph-node dissection (report dated 05 May 2026).\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\nExtensive right axillary macrometastatic, nuclear grade 3 carcinoma with extranodal extension (ENE); **AJCC 8 breast pN3a** if associated with an ipsilateral breast primary (**ypN3a** if post-neoadjuvant therapy). Primary breast histotype cannot be classified from this specimen under WHO Breast Tumours, 5th ed.\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n- **Nodal burden:** 26/50 positive (52%), all reported as macrometastases; largest deposit **1 cm (10 mm)**.\n- **ENE:** perinodal soft-tissue involvement up to **5 mm**.\n- **Grade:** nuclear grade **3** in the metastases; primary-tumour histological grade unavailable.\n- **Treatment response / RCB:** no response identified in examined nodes; **RCB class III reported**, not independently calculable here.\n- **Biomarkers:** performed on an earlier study but results absent; **ER, PR and HER2 status unknown** and not reliably predictable from nuclear grade.\n\n## INTERPRETATION\nAt least 10 axillary macrometastases establish **pN3a** (or **ypN3a** after confirmed neoadjuvant therapy); ENE signifies additional regional extension but does not change the N category. The heavy persistent nodal burden, lack of demonstrable nodal regression and reported RCB III indicate an adverse residual-disease profile, although breast response and formal whole-case RCB cannot be assessed here. The nodal morphology establishes metastatic carcinoma, not a specific breast carcinoma histotype. No primary pT or M category is available, so a definitive overall pathological or prognostic stage cannot be assigned. **If breast origin and M0 are confirmed, any T with N3a corresponds to AJCC 8 anatomic stage IIIC**; prognostic staging additionally requires the primary tumour data and actual biomarker results.\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\nReview the matched breast-primary/pretreatment pathology for histotype, tumour size and histological grade; retrieve the prior ER/PR/HER2 results rather than infer a profile. Confirm neoadjuvant treatment history and clinical distant-metastasis assessment, and obtain breast tumour-bed findings and a whole-case RCB score/class for integrated post-treatment evaluation.",
