        "summary": "# Tumour Board Diagnostic Summary – Endometrial Pathology Report No. 2605694 (EN)\n\n**Source:** `/app/attachments/report_2605694_EN.pdf` (single-page Anatomical Pathology Report – Histological Study; no images, no additional pages).\n**Patient:** female, 47 years, outpatient. Admission 05/07/2026; report date 05/11/2026. Reporting pathologist: anonymised (pathologist–oncologist).\n**Scope of available data:** the pathology report only. No clinical history, menopausal status, imaging, operative findings, tumour markers or family history are available. Nothing below that is stated as a fact goes beyond the report text; interpretive statements are labelled as such.\n\n---\n\n## 0. What the report actually contains (verbatim basis for every answer)\n\n| Item | Report content |\n|---|---|\n| Specimens (labelled \"RESECTION (3 SAMPLES)\") | Block 1 \"Endocervix\": mucohemorrhagic material, combined volume 0.5 cc. Block 2 \"Endometrium\": mucohemorrhagic material, combined volume 3 cc. Block 3 \"Endometrial polyps\": multiple light-brown tissue fragments with raw surfaces, combined volume 1 cc. |\n| Microscopic description | \"See diagnosis\" – no descriptive microscopy is given. |\n| Diagnosis | \"Endometrioid pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated, FIGO II, without evidence of vasculoneural invasion in this sample.\" \"Some polypoid fragments with adenocarcinoma involvement.\" \"No normal endocervix identified.\" \"Scarce representation of basal endometrium.\" |\n| Comments | Immunohistochemistry is **suggested** (not performed) on Block 3: Keratin cocktail, p53, p16, oestrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, beta-catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2. |\n| Not mentioned anywhere | POLE testing; any IHC result; tumour size; myometrium; cervical stroma; adnexa; lymph nodes; background endometrium (hyperplasia/EIN); grading criteria used. |\n\n**Nature of the material (interpretation):** although the laboratory header says \"resection\", the gross description (0.5 cc + 3 cc of fragmented mucohemorrhagic material plus 1 cc of polyp fragments) is that of endocervical/endometrial sampling (curettage/biopsy) with polypectomy. **No hysterectomy specimen, no myometrium, no cervical wall, no adnexa and no lymph nodes were received.** This single fact constrains most of the answers below.\n\n---\n\n## DIAGNOSIS\n\n**Endometrioid endometrial adenocarcinoma (\"endometrioid pattern\"), moderately differentiated – FIGO grade 2**, diagnosed on the combined endocervical/endometrial/polyp samples, with explicit involvement of some of the endometrial polyp fragments (carcinoma involving endometrial polyp[s], Block 3). The report does not otherwise state the per-block distribution of tumour.\n\n- Under the WHO Classification of Tumours, 5th edition (2020), FIGO grades 1–2 are grouped as **low-grade endometrioid carcinoma** (binary grading); grade 3 = high-grade. In FIGO 2023 terminology this is a \"non-aggressive\" histotype – *provided p53 proves wild-type* (see Molecular Subgroup).\n- **Critical clarification for the board:** the \"FIGO II\" in the diagnostic line follows \"moderately differentiated\" and denotes the **FIGO grade (2)**, not a FIGO **stage II**. It must not be transcribed as stage II; nothing in a curettage specimen allows a stage to be assigned.\n- Caveats on the diagnosis itself:\n  - The histotype is a morphological call on fragmented material with no microscopic description and no confirmatory IHC. The panel the pathologist suggests is the appropriate one to (a) confirm endometrial rather than endocervical origin (ER/PR/p16 – relevant because an endocervical sample was submitted and \"no normal endocervix identified\"), (b) exclude a serous carcinoma with glandular/endometrioid-like morphology (p53, p16), and (c) complete the molecular classification (p53, MLH1/PMS2/MSH2/MSH6).\n  - Grade assigned on preoperative sampling can be revised (usually upward) on the hysterectomy specimen; the criteria used (% solid non-squamous growth, nuclear grade) are not documented because the microscopic description was omitted.\n  - Background endometrium (atypical hyperplasia/EIN) is not described; only \"scarce representation of basal endometrium\".\n\n---\n\n## FIGO STAGE\n\n**Cannot be assigned from this specimen.** No FIGO stage – neither 2009 nor 2023 – can be given on curettage/polypectomy material, and the report itself does not state a stage.\n\nReasoning:\n1. FIGO staging of endometrial carcinoma is surgico-pathological. Every stage-defining element is absent from this material: depth of myometrial invasion (no myometrium present; even the basal endometrium is \"scarcely represented\"), cervical stromal invasion, serosal/adnexal involvement, LVSI quantification, pelvic/para-aortic nodal status, peritoneal and distant disease.\n2. **Cervical status is indeterminate.** \"No normal endocervix identified\" does not say whether the endocervical sample (Block 1, 0.5 cc) contained carcinoma or simply contained no evaluable endocervical mucosa. Even if tumour fragments were present in endocervical curettings, that would not establish cervical stromal invasion (FIGO 2009: endocervical glandular involvement only = stage I; stromal invasion is required for stage II) and could represent tumour dislodged from the cavity. Cervical stromal invasion can only be established on MRI and, definitively, on the hysterectomy specimen.\n3. \"FIGO II\" in the report = grade 2 (see Diagnosis).\n4. **Provisional clinical framing only (assumption, not a finding):** clinically presumed uterus-confined disease. If hysterectomy confirms a low-grade endometrioid carcinoma without extrauterine spread, the anatomical range would be FIGO 2023 stage IA1 (confined to polyp/endometrium) to IA2 (<50% myoinvasion, no/focal LVSI), IB (≥50%), IIA (cervical stroma) or IIB (substantial LVSI) – none of which is currently distinguishable. Under FIGO 2023 the molecular class also modifies stage (any stage I–II POLEmut → IAm POLEmut; p53abn with any myoinvasion → IICm p53abn), so the final stage requires **both** the hysterectomy with nodal assessment **and** the completed molecular classification.\n\n---\n\n## MOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification)\n\n**Cannot be assigned from the available material – \"molecular classification pending / not performed\".**\n\n- The WHO 5th edition (and ESGO–ESTRO–ESP 2025) integrated classification requires three analyses applied hierarchically: **POLE** exonuclease-domain pathogenic-variant testing → **MMR** protein IHC (MLH1, PMS2, MSH2, MSH6) → **p53** IHC; NSMP is the residual group when all three are normal. Multiple-classifier tumours are assigned by the genomic driver (POLEmut > MMRd > p53abn).\n- In this report, p53 and the four MMR proteins were only **suggested** on Block 3; **no IHC result is reported**, and **POLE testing is not mentioned at all**. Therefore none of POLE-ultramutated, MMR-deficient, p53-abnormal or NSMP can be assigned, and NSMP must not be assumed by default (it is a diagnosis of exclusion).\n- Why this is the single most consequential missing item for the board:\n  - **p53abn** would move a \"grade 2\" tumour into the high-risk group if any myoinvasion is present (FIGO 2023 IICm p53abn; adjuvant chemo-radiotherapy).\n  - **POLEmut** would confer an excellent prognosis with omission of adjuvant therapy at stage I–II (FIGO 2023 IAm POLEmut).\n  - **MMRd** would trigger Lynch syndrome work-up (MLH1 promoter methylation if MLH1/PMS2 loss; genetic counselling/germline testing otherwise), is relevant at 47 years, and determines immunotherapy eligibility should the disease prove advanced or recur.\n  - **NSMP** is further stratified by **ER status** (ESGO 2025: NSMP low-grade *and* ER-positive vs NSMP high-grade *or* ER-negative), which is why the suggested ER/PR stains matter beyond diagnosis; nuclear beta-catenin (CTNNB1) is an additional adverse marker in low-grade NSMP.\n- ESGO 2025 explicitly encourages molecular testing on biopsy/curettage material, so the existing blocks (Block 3 ± Block 2) are appropriate for this work-up now, before surgery.\n\n---\n\n## LYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION\n\n**Absent (not identified) in the sampled material.** Report wording: \"without evidence of vasculoneural invasion in this sample\" – i.e., neither vascular nor perineural invasion was seen.\n\nCaveat (interpretation): LVSI cannot be excluded on curettage/polypectomy material because it is assessed at the invasive front within the myometrium of the hysterectomy specimen, where it must also be **quantified** (none / focal <5 vessels / substantial ≥5 vessels on at least one H&E slide). Substantial LVSI alone would change stage (FIGO 2023 IIB) and risk group (high-intermediate). Final LVSI status is therefore **pending the hysterectomy**; the pathologist's own qualifier \"in this sample\" acknowledges this.\n\n---\n\n## MYOMETRIAL INVASION\n\n**Cannot be estimated – no myometrium is present in the specimen.**\n\n- The material consists of fragmented mucohemorrhagic endometrial tissue and polyp fragments, with only \"scarce representation of basal endometrium\"; the endometrial–myometrial junction is not represented, so neither presence nor depth (<50% vs ≥50%) of invasion can be judged. Any percentage would be speculation.\n- The involvement of polypoid fragments raises the possibility that part of the carcinoma arises within a polyp, but the diagnosis is issued for the combined samples and is not stated to be confined to the polyps, so confinement to a polyp (FIGO 2023 IA1) cannot be concluded.\n- Depth must be assessed pre-operatively by contrast-enhanced pelvic MRI (or expert transvaginal ultrasound) and definitively on the hysterectomy specimen.\n\n---\n\n## KEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES\n\n**Established from the report (favourable so far):**\n- Endometrioid histotype (non-aggressive histotype in FIGO 2023 terms, pending p53).\n- FIGO grade 2 / moderately differentiated = low-grade endometrioid carcinoma (WHO binary grading).\n- No lymphovascular or perineural invasion identified in the sampled material.\n- Carcinoma involving endometrial polyp fragments – compatible with, but not proof of, an early lesion.\n- Age 47 (ESGO 2025 lists age <60 and low grade as factors favouring omission of vaginal brachytherapy in intermediate-risk disease).\n\n**Unknown or potentially unfavourable (drivers of current uncertainty):**\n- **Molecular class unknown** (p53, MMR, POLE not done) – the dominant determinant of risk group and adjuvant therapy; a p53abn result would convert this into high-risk disease.\n- **ER/PR and beta-catenin status unknown** – ER negativity or nuclear beta-catenin (CTNNB1 mutation) worsen prognosis within NSMP low-grade tumours; ER also predicts endocrine responsiveness.\n- **Depth of myometrial invasion unknown** (no myometrium sampled).\n- **Cervical stromal involvement unknown** (\"no normal endocervix identified\"; tumour presence in Block 1 not stated).\n- **LVSI status not definitively assessable** on curettage.\n- **Tumour size, nodal status, adnexal/serosal/peritoneal status unknown** – no imaging or surgical data in the file.\n- **Lynch syndrome risk unassessed** – MMR IHC pending in a patient aged <50.\n- **Specimen/report limitations:** small volumes (0.5 cc, 3 cc, 1 cc); \"See diagnosis\" in place of a microscopic description; grading criteria, per-block tumour distribution and background endometrium undocumented; preoperative grade may change on hysterectomy.\n\n---\n\n## RECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT\n\nBecause neither stage nor molecular subgroup is established, definitive adjuvant recommendations cannot yet be made; the recommended pathway is to close the data gaps and then apply the ESGO–ESTRO–ESP 2025 molecular-integrated risk groups (which use FIGO 2023 staging). Recommendations are framed accordingly.\n\n### 1. Complete the pathology work-up on the existing blocks (before surgery)\n- **Perform** – not merely suggest – the recommended IHC panel on Block 3 (± Block 2 if tumour is better represented): p53, MLH1, PMS2, MSH2, MSH6, ER, PR, p16, beta-catenin, keratin cocktail. ESGO 2025: molecular classification should be done for all endometrial carcinomas; ER IHC is recommended in all cases; MMR IHC (two- or four-antibody approach) is the preferred Lynch pre-screen for every patient.\n- **Add POLE exonuclease-domain testing** covering the 11 pathogenic variants (not in the suggested panel). It may be omitted only if final pathology places the tumour in a low-risk stage I category where it would not alter adjuvant treatment; given that stage, myoinvasion, LVSI and p53 status are all unknown, request it now or ensure it can be added promptly.\n- If MLH1 (± PMS2) loss → MLH1 promoter hypermethylation testing; if unmethylated, or if MSH2/MSH6/isolated PMS2 loss → genetic counselling and germline testing (Lynch syndrome). Equivocal MMR IHC → MSI-PCR; equivocal p53 → TP53 sequencing.\n- Request a **pathology addendum**: full microscopic description; per-block findings (specifically whether Block 1 endocervical material contains carcinoma or is non-representative); grading criteria (% solid growth, nuclear grade); background atypical hyperplasia/EIN; synoptic (ICCR/CAP) biopsy format; explicit statement that \"FIGO II\" means grade 2.\n- Retain blocks for correlation; molecular testing need only be repeated on the hysterectomy specimen if biopsy tissue is scant/equivocal or a new tumour component appears.\n\n### 2. Pre-treatment clinical staging\n- Contrast-enhanced **pelvic MRI** (or expert transvaginal ultrasound) for depth of myometrial invasion, **cervical stromal invasion** (specifically indicated here because cervical status is indeterminate), adnexa and pelvic nodes.\n- Chest imaging; CT chest/abdomen/pelvis if MRI shows deep invasion/cervical or extrauterine disease or if high-risk features emerge (e.g., p53abn, high grade on review). Serum CA-125 optional.\n- Clinical assessment: pelvic/speculum examination for cervical or vaginal extension, menopausal status, BMI, comorbidities and surgical fitness, personal/family cancer history (Lynch).\n\n### 3. Primary treatment (fit patient, presumed uterus-confined disease)\n- **Total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy** by a **minimally invasive approach**, avoiding any intraperitoneal tumour spillage or morcellation; peritoneal survey. Omentectomy is not required for endometrioid histology (would be reconsidered only if IHC reclassifies the tumour as serous/undifferentiated).\n- **Sentinel lymph node biopsy** (indocyanine green, cervical injection) with pathological ultrastaging in all presumed uterus-confined disease; if no SLN is detected on a side, side-specific systematic lymphadenectomy for high-intermediate/high-risk disease and can be considered for presumed intermediate risk; not needed for low-risk disease.\n- If cervical stromal involvement is confirmed: simple total hysterectomy with free margins remains standard; more extensive (radical) procedures only if required to achieve free margins.\n- **Ovarian preservation:** not routinely recommended here – ESGO 2025 criteria are premenopausal age <45 years, FIGO 2023 IA1/IA2 and low risk by molecular classification, with bilateral salpingectomy, and never in Lynch/BRCA carriers. At 47 with molecular and myoinvasion status unknown, BSO is the standard; discuss only after MDT review if the patient is premenopausal and strongly requests it.\n- **Fertility-sparing (progestin) treatment:** not applicable as standard care – reserved for grade 1 endometrioid carcinoma without myometrial invasion on imaging in patients wishing to conceive; this tumour is grade 2 and myoinvasion is unassessed.\n- If medically unfit for surgery: definitive radiotherapy (image-guided intrauterine brachytherapy alone can be considered for low-grade tumours without deep invasion; EBRT + brachytherapy otherwise).\n\n### 4. Adjuvant therapy – to be decided after final surgical pathology + molecular class (ESGO–ESTRO–ESP 2025 risk groups)\n| Final finding (this grade-2 endometrioid tumour) | Risk group | Adjuvant recommendation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **POLEmut**, any stage I–II (→ IAm POLEmut) | Low | No adjuvant therapy |\n| **MMRd or NSMP low-grade ER-positive**, no or <50% myoinvasion, no/focal LVSI (IA1/IA2) | Low | No adjuvant therapy (observation) |\n| **MMRd or NSMP low-grade ER-positive**, ≥50% myoinvasion, no/focal LVSI (IB); **NSMP low-grade ER-positive** with cervical stromal invasion (IIA) | Intermediate | Vaginal brachytherapy should be considered; no adjuvant therapy is an option, especially age <60 (applies at 47) or low grade |\n| **MMRd** with cervical stromal invasion (IIA); **MMRd or NSMP low-grade ER-positive** with substantial LVSI (IIB) | High-intermediate | EBRT recommended; vaginal brachytherapy alternative if SLN/nodal staging pN0; no adjuvant therapy can be considered if pN0, no substantial LVSI and low grade |\n| **p53abn** with any myoinvasion (IICm p53abn), or **NSMP ER-negative** with myoinvasion (IA2/IB) or stage II | High | EBRT with concurrent and adjuvant chemotherapy (carboplatin–paclitaxel) or sequential chemo-radiotherapy; chemotherapy ± brachytherapy alternative |\n| **p53abn or NSMP ER-negative** confined to polyp/endometrium, no myoinvasion (IA1) | Uncertain | Adjuvant therapy generally not recommended; MDT discussion |\n| Stage III–IVA (nodes, adnexa, serosa, vagina/parametria, peritoneum), MMRd/NSMP/p53abn | High | Chemo-radiotherapy or chemotherapy; for **MMRd** stage III–IVA, chemotherapy combined with an immune checkpoint inhibitor (± EBRT) should be considered |\n| Unresectable/residual/metastatic disease | Advanced | MMRd: carboplatin–paclitaxel + checkpoint inhibitor (dostarlimab, durvalumab or pembrolizumab) then ICI maintenance; non-MMRd: carboplatin–paclitaxel (± ICI); low-grade ER-positive, low-volume/indolent disease: endocrine therapy (progestins preferred; aromatase inhibitors/tamoxifen alternatives) |\n\n### 5. Hereditary risk, follow-up and survivorship\n- Universal Lynch pre-screening by MMR IHC (with MLH1 methylation where indicated) and genetic counselling if abnormal or if family history is suggestive – particularly pertinent at 47 years.\n- Risk-adapted, personalised follow-up per ESGO 2025 (informed by final molecular class and treatment); patient education on recurrence symptoms (vaginal bleeding), menopause management after BSO, lifestyle counselling, psycho-oncological support, and consideration of clinical-trial enrolment (e.g., molecularly stratified adjuvant trials).\n- **Re-present at tumour board** (a) once IHC ± POLE results are available and MRI is done, to finalise the surgical plan, and (b) after final hysterectomy/SLN pathology, to assign the definitive FIGO 2023 stage with molecular annotation and the adjuvant strategy.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line for the board\n\nLow-grade (FIGO grade 2, moderately differentiated) endometrioid endometrial adenocarcinoma diagnosed on endocervical/endometrial curettage and polypectomy material (Request 2605694, 47-year-old woman), with carcinoma involving endometrial polyp fragments and **no lymphovascular or perineural invasion in the sample**. **FIGO stage, depth of myometrial invasion, cervical stromal status and molecular subgroup are all not assessable from this specimen** – the \"FIGO II\" in the report is the grade, not the stage, and the recommended IHC (p53, MMR proteins, ER, PR, p16, beta-catenin, keratin cocktail) has not yet been performed; POLE testing has not been requested. Recommended next steps: perform IHC + POLE on the existing blocks, pelvic MRI, then total hysterectomy with BSO and sentinel lymph node biopsy (minimally invasive), followed by adjuvant therapy assigned strictly by the ESGO–ESTRO–ESP 2025 molecular-integrated risk group once the final stage and molecular class are known.",
