        "summary": "# Tumour Board Diagnostic Summary: Endometrial Carcinoma\n\n**Report:** `report_2605694_EN.pdf`, Anatomical Pathology Report (Histological Study), Request No. 2605694\n**Patient:** female, 47 years, outpatient. Admission 05/07/2026; report date 05/11/2026 (dates as printed).\n**Evidence base:** this one-page report is the only source. No imaging, operative, IHC/molecular, family-history or other clinical data were provided. I used FIGO 2023, the WHO 5th edition classification and the ESGO-ESTRO-ESP 2025 guidelines only to interpret the report and to frame recommendations. Nothing from those sources is presented as a finding in this patient.\n\n## Report findings (as documented)\n\n| Block | Label | Macroscopic description |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | \"Endocervix\" | Mucohemorrhagic material, combined volume 0.5 cc |\n| 2 | \"Endometrium\" | Mucohemorrhagic material, combined volume 3 cc |\n| 3 | \"Endometrial polyps\" | Multiple light brown tissue fragments with raw surfaces, combined volume 1 cc |\n\n- **Microscopic description:** \"See diagnosis.\" There is no separate microscopic text.\n- **Diagnosis** (one combined diagnosis under \"ENDOCERVIX, ENDOMETRIUM, ENDOMETRIAL POLYPS, RESECTION (3 SAMPLES)\"):\n  - \"Endometrioid pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated, FIGO II, without evidence of vasculoneural invasion in this sample\"\n  - \"Some polypoid fragments with adenocarcinoma involvement\"\n  - \"No normal endocervix identified\"\n  - \"Scarce representation of basal endometrium\"\n- **Comment:** immunohistochemistry is *suggested* on Block 3: Keratin Cocktail, P53, P16, Estrogen Receptors, Progesterone Receptors, Beta-Catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6 and PMS2. **No IHC results are reported, and no addendum was provided.**\n\n> **Key interpretive points**\n> 1. **\"FIGO II\" is the FIGO grade, not the FIGO stage.** It appears next to \"moderately differentiated\", which means FIGO grade 2. It does not mean stage II.\n> 2. **The specimen is fragmented resection/curettage material, not a hysterectomy.** A surgical stage and the depth of myometrial invasion cannot be derived from it.\n> 3. **IHC was only recommended, so the molecular subgroup cannot be assigned.** POLE testing was not requested at all.\n> 4. **\"No normal endocervix identified\" means the endocervical sample is not representative, so cervical status is unknown.** This finding is *not* evidence of cervical stromal invasion.\n\n---\n\n## DIAGNOSIS:\n**Endometrioid adenocarcinoma of the endometrium, FIGO grade 2 (moderately differentiated).** Under FIGO 2023, grade 1–2 endometrioid carcinoma is low grade and counts as a \"non-aggressive\" histological type.\n\n- Adenocarcinoma involves some of the polypoid (endometrial polyp) fragments.\n- Other statements in the report:\n  - no vasculoneural invasion in this sample\n  - no normal endocervix identified\n  - scarce basal endometrium\n- **Limitations:**\n  - There is no microscopic description. The architectural basis for grade 2 is therefore not documented. By FIGO definition, grade 2 means 6–50% solid non-squamous, non-morular growth.\n  - Apart from the polyp fragments, the report does not say which of the three samples contain carcinoma.\n  - The suggested p53/p16/ER/PR panel would also help confirm the endometrioid histotype and an endometrial rather than endocervical origin. This matters because no normal endocervix was identified.\n  - Histotype and grade should be confirmed on the hysterectomy specimen.\n\n## FIGO STAGE:\n**Cannot be assigned from the available material. The surgical-pathological FIGO stage is undetermined, and no \"most likely\" stage can responsibly be named from this specimen.**\n\nReasons:\n1. **\"FIGO II\" does not mean stage II.** In the report it follows \"moderately differentiated\" and describes FIGO grade 2. Recording stage II would wrongly imply cervical stromal invasion and could lead to overtreatment, such as radical surgery or radiotherapy.\n2. **FIGO staging (2009 and 2023) is surgical-pathological.** It needs hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (BSO) and nodal staging to define:\n   - depth of myometrial invasion\n   - cervical stromal invasion\n   - extent of lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI): focal, or substantial (≥5 vessels)\n   - adnexal, serosal, nodal and peritoneal status\n\n   FIGO 2023 also builds in histotype, grade, LVSI and molecular class (e.g., IAm POLEmut, IICm p53abn). None of this information is available.\n3. **Cervical involvement is neither shown nor excluded.**\n   - No normal endocervix was identified.\n   - The report does not state that carcinoma is present in the \"Endocervix\" sample.\n   - Carcinoma fragments in an endocervical sample could be displaced tumour from the uterine body. They would not prove cervical *stromal* invasion, which is the only cervical finding that defines stage II (FIGO 2023 IIA). Under FIGO 2009, involvement of endocervical glands alone is still stage I.\n4. **What can be said:**\n   - Carcinoma involves the endometrium and endometrial polyps.\n   - No reported finding documents cervical stromal invasion, substantial LVSI or disease outside the uterus.\n   - This is *compatible with* stage I but does not establish it.\n   - For a low-grade endometrioid tumour, the final FIGO 2023 stage could be anything from IA1 (confined to a polyp or the endometrium) through IA2, IB, IIA and IIB to III–IV. It would carry a molecular annotation, e.g., IAm POLEmut, or IICm p53abn if the tumour is p53-abnormal with any myometrial invasion.\n\n## MOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification):\n**Cannot be assigned from the available material.** IHC (p53, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 and the rest of the panel) was only suggested on Block 3; it was not performed or reported. POLE mutation analysis was not requested.\n\n- None of the four subgroups (POLE-ultramutated, MMR-deficient, p53-abnormal, NSMP) can be named.\n- **NSMP must not be assigned by default.** It is a diagnosis of exclusion that requires POLE wild-type, MMR-proficient and p53 wild-type results.\n- Low-grade endometrioid morphology does not predict the subgroup; all four subgroups can look like this.\n- **Tests needed to classify:**\n  - p53 IHC\n  - MMR IHC (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2), with reflex MLH1 promoter methylation testing if MLH1 or MLH1/PMS2 expression is lost\n  - **POLE exonuclease-domain sequencing covering all 11 pathogenic variants.** This is not in the suggested panel and cannot be done by IHC.\n  - If more than one classifier is abnormal, classify by the genomic driver: POLEmut takes precedence over MMRd, and MMRd over p53abn.\n- **Supporting biomarkers (refine prognosis but do not define the subgroup):**\n  - ER status: ESGO-ESTRO-ESP 2025 splits NSMP into \"low-grade and ER-positive\" versus \"high-grade and/or ER-negative\".\n  - Nuclear β-catenin: a surrogate for CTNNB1 mutation and an adverse factor within low-grade NSMP.\n\n## LYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION:\n**Absent: not identified in this sample.** The report says \"without evidence of vasculoneural invasion in this sample\". \"Vasculoneural\" covers lymphovascular and perineural invasion.\n\n- **This finding is provisional.** It is based on small fragments without myometrium or the tumour–myometrium interface, which is where LVSI is usually seen.\n- Definitive LVSI status and its extent (none, focal, or substantial = ≥5 vessels by WHO criteria) must be assessed on the hysterectomy. Substantial LVSI would upstage a low-grade endometrioid tumour to FIGO 2023 IIB.\n\n## MYOMETRIAL INVASION:\n**Not assessable. The depth cannot be estimated from this specimen.**\n\n- None of the three samples is described as containing myometrium.\n- The report explicitly notes \"scarce representation of basal endometrium\", so even the endometrial–myometrial junction is barely sampled.\n- As a result, none of these categories can be inferred:\n  - no invasion (IA1)\n  - invasion of less than 50% (IA2)\n  - invasion of 50% or more (IB)\n- **Absence of invasion must not be assumed from this biopsy.**\n- Next steps:\n  - Pre-operative estimate: pelvic MRI or expert transvaginal ultrasound.\n  - Definitive depth and its ratio to myometrial thickness: measured on the hysterectomy specimen.\n\n## KEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES:\n\n| Feature | Status from report | Effect on risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| Histotype | Endometrioid | Non-aggressive type (FIGO 2023); favourable |\n| Grade | FIGO grade 2 (moderately differentiated) | Low grade; favourable |\n| LVSI | Not identified in this sample | Favourable but provisional |\n| Polyp involvement | Some polypoid fragments involved | Stage IA1 only if confined to polyp/endometrium on hysterectomy; not proven |\n| Age | 47 years | Under 60, which matters for de-escalation if risk is intermediate. Diagnosis before 50 is a classic Lynch syndrome warning sign. |\n| Molecular subgroup | **Unknown** | Main driver of risk: POLEmut makes stage I–II disease low risk; p53abn with any myometrial invasion becomes IICm p53abn (high risk) |\n| ER/PR, β-catenin | **Unknown** | NSMP that is ER-negative with myometrial invasion is high risk despite low grade |\n| Depth of myometrial invasion | **Unknown (not assessable)** | Separates IA from IB |\n| Cervical stromal invasion | **Unknown** (no normal endocervix) | Would define IIA |\n| LVSI extent (focal vs substantial) | **Unknown** | Substantial would mean IIB |\n| Adnexa, serosa, nodes, peritoneum | **Unknown** (no staging or imaging) | Would define stage III–IV |\n\n- **Sampling limitations:**\n  - fragmented material (0.5 cc, 3 cc and 1 cc)\n  - no myometrium\n  - scarce basal endometrium\n  - no normal endocervix\n  - no separate microscopic description\n  - no block-by-block distribution of the carcinoma\n  - biopsy grade and histotype can change on the hysterectomy\n- **ESGO-ESTRO-ESP 2025 risk group: cannot be allocated.** Allocation needs both the FIGO 2023 stage and the molecular subgroup. For this low-grade endometrioid tumour, the possible range runs from:\n  - **low risk:** e.g., POLEmut stage I–II, or MMRd / NSMP-ER-positive stage IA\n  - to **high risk:** e.g., p53abn or NSMP-ER-negative with myometrial invasion, or stage III–IVA in any subgroup other than POLEmut (POLEmut stage III–IVA is \"uncertain risk\")\n\n## RECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT:\nNeither the stage nor the molecular subgroup is known, so a definitive adjuvant plan cannot be set yet. The recommended sequence is: complete the molecular classification, stage the disease surgically, then treat according to the integrated risk group.\n\n**1. Complete the pathology now (on Block 3, or Block 2 if Block 3 has too little tumour)**\n- Perform the suggested IHC: p53, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2, ER, PR and β-catenin, plus p16 and keratin cocktail as diagnostic aids.\n- **Add POLE mutation testing.**\n  - ESGO 2025 allows it to be omitted only in low-risk stage I disease, where the result would not change adjuvant treatment.\n  - The stage is unknown, so testing is warranted.\n  - A POLE wild-type result is also needed before a tumour can be called NSMP, or before p53abn can be accepted as the driver.\n  - The result decides treatment if final pathology shows stage IB, cervical stromal invasion, substantial LVSI or p53abn with myometrial invasion; in those cases a POLE mutation would allow de-escalation to no adjuvant treatment.\n- Test MLH1 promoter methylation if MLH1 (with or without PMS2) expression is lost.\n- Molecular testing on curettage material is valid and encouraged. It only needs repeating on the hysterectomy if tumour is scant, results are equivocal, or a new tumour component appears.\n- Ask the pathologist for an addendum that:\n  - confirms \"FIGO II\" means grade 2\n  - states which samples contain carcinoma, especially Block 1 (\"Endocervix\")\n  - adds microscopic details\n\n  Consider review by an expert gynaecological pathologist.\n- HER2 testing is not indicated yet. It becomes relevant if the tumour proves p53-abnormal with advanced or recurrent disease, or if the histotype is revised to serous carcinoma or carcinosarcoma (ESGO 2025).\n\n**2. Pre-operative work-up**\n- History: family history of Lynch-associated cancers, menopausal status, comorbidities and reproductive wishes.\n- Pelvic and speculum examination.\n- Pelvic MRI (preferred here, to assess cervical stromal involvement and myometrial depth) or expert transvaginal ultrasound.\n- Chest imaging. Consider CT of the abdomen and pelvis (or PET-CT) if there are high-risk molecular or imaging features, or suspicion of disease outside the uterus.\n\n**3. Primary surgery (for presumed uterus-confined disease)**\n- **Standard operation:** total (simple) hysterectomy with BSO and sentinel lymph node biopsy (indocyanine green by cervical injection, with ultrastaging of the nodes).\n  - A minimally invasive approach is preferred.\n  - Avoid morcellation and tumour spillage.\n  - If sentinel nodes do not map, perform side-specific lymphadenectomy for high-intermediate or high risk, and consider it for intermediate risk.\n- **Omentectomy:** not needed for endometrioid histology.\n- **Radical hysterectomy:** not indicated because of \"FIGO II\", which is a grade, not a stage. If imaging shows cervical stromal invasion, more extensive surgery is justified only when needed to achieve clear margins.\n- **Ovarian preservation:** ESGO 2025 limits it to premenopausal patients under 45 with FIGO 2023 IA1/IA2 disease that is low risk by molecular subgroup, with bilateral salpingectomy recommended. Ovaries should not be preserved in carriers of Lynch (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) or BRCA germline mutations. At 47 she falls outside the age criterion, so BSO is the standard (her menopausal status is not reported).\n- **Fertility-sparing treatment:** no wish for it is documented. Under ESGO/ESHRE/ESGE 2023 it is standard only for grade 1, stage IA disease without myometrial invasion. For grade 2 it would be decided case by case in an expert centre, with a second pathology opinion.\n\n**4. Adjuvant therapy, depending on final FIGO 2023 stage and molecular subgroup (ESGO-ESTRO-ESP 2025, low-grade endometrioid)**\n\n| Final molecular subgroup | Final FIGO 2023 stage | Risk group | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| POLEmut | Any stage I–II (reported as IAm POLEmut) | Low | No adjuvant therapy |\n| POLEmut | III–IVA | Uncertain | Decide case by case; de-escalation can be considered |\n| MMRd | IA1 / IA2 / IA3 | Low | No adjuvant therapy |\n| MMRd | IB | Intermediate | Consider vaginal brachytherapy (VBT); no adjuvant therapy is an option, especially under 60 |\n| MMRd | IIA (cervical stroma) or IIB (substantial LVSI) | High-intermediate | External beam radiotherapy (EBRT). VBT is an alternative, especially if nodes are negative (pN0). Observation can be considered if pN0, no substantial LVSI and low grade. |\n| MMRd | III–IVA | High | EBRT with concurrent and adjuvant chemotherapy, or sequential chemotherapy and radiotherapy. Chemotherapy with or without VBT is an alternative. Chemotherapy plus an immune checkpoint inhibitor (with or without EBRT) should be considered. |\n| NSMP, ER-positive | IA1 / IA2 / IA3 | Low | No adjuvant therapy |\n| NSMP, ER-positive | IB or IIA | Intermediate | Consider VBT; no adjuvant therapy is an option |\n| NSMP, ER-positive | IIB | High-intermediate | EBRT; VBT is an alternative if pN0 |\n| NSMP, ER-positive | III–IVA | High | Chemoradiotherapy or sequential chemotherapy and radiotherapy; chemotherapy with or without VBT |\n| NSMP, ER-negative | IA1 | Uncertain | Adjuvant therapy generally not recommended |\n| NSMP, ER-negative | IA2, IA3, IB, II, III–IVA | High | EBRT with chemotherapy (concurrent/adjuvant or sequential); chemotherapy with or without VBT is an alternative |\n| p53abn | No myometrial invasion (polyp/endometrium only) | Uncertain | Adjuvant therapy generally not recommended |\n| p53abn | Any myometrial invasion (IICm p53abn) or stage III–IVA | High | EBRT with concurrent and adjuvant chemotherapy, or sequential; chemotherapy with or without VBT is an alternative |\n| Any | III–IVA with residual disease, IVB, IVC | Advanced | **MMRd:** carboplatin–paclitaxel plus an immune checkpoint inhibitor (dostarlimab, durvalumab or pembrolizumab), then maintenance immunotherapy. **Non-MMRd:** carboplatin–paclitaxel; chemo-immunotherapy followed by immunotherapy maintenance (± a PARP inhibitor, e.g., durvalumab + olaparib) can be considered. **Low-grade, ER-positive, low-volume or slowly progressing disease:** endocrine therapy (progestins) is preferred. |\n\n**5. Genetics, follow-up and tumour board plan**\n- If MMR deficiency is not explained by MLH1 promoter methylation, or the family history is suggestive, offer genetic counselling and germline testing for Lynch syndrome. This has consequences for surveillance, testing of relatives, and BSO rather than ovarian preservation.\n- Follow-up should be tailored to the molecular subgroup and the treatment given. Inform the patient about specialised centres and clinical trials.\n- **Re-present at tumour board twice:**\n  1. after the IHC/POLE results and MRI, to plan surgery\n  2. after final surgical pathology, to set the definitive FIGO 2023 stage, molecular annotation and adjuvant decision",
