        "summary": "# Gynaecological Pathology – Tumour Board Diagnostic Summary\n## Report 2605694_EN – Endometrial Pathology Report\n\n**Source document:** `/app/attachments/report_2605694_EN.pdf` – Imported Spectra lab report, used as complete source of truth. No external facts invented.\n\n**Patient / administrative context as reported:**\n- Patient: [ANONYMIZED], ID: [ANONYMIZED]\n- Gender: FEMALE, Age: 47 years\n- Request No.: 2605694\n- Service: OUTPATIENT\n- Admission: 05/07/2026, Report Date: 05/11/2026\n- Physician / Entity: [ANONYMIZED]\n- Report type: PATHOLOGY LABORATORY – ANATOMICAL PATHOLOGY REPORT - HISTOLOGICAL STUDY\n- Sign-out: [ANONYMIZED PATHOLOGIST - ONCOLOGIST]; Processed by: [ANONYMIZED PATHOLOGIST]\n\n**Specimens as labelled and processed:**\n- Labeled \"Endocervix\": mucohemorrhagic material with combined volume of 0.5 cc. Block 1.\n- Labeled \"Endometrium\": mucohemorrhagic material with combined volume of 3 cc. Block 2.\n- Labeled \"Endometrial polyps\": multiple light brown tissue fragments with raw surfaces, combined volume of 1 cc. Block 3.\n- Overall procedure coded in DIAGNOSIS line as: \"ENDOCERVIX, ENDOMETRIUM, ENDOMETRIAL POLYPS, RESECTION (3 SAMPLES)\"\n- This is fragmented curettage / biopsy / polypectomy-type material, NOT a hysterectomy, myometrial resection, lymphadenectomy or other surgical staging specimen. No uterine wall, serosa, adnexa, parametria, vagina, lymph nodes, omentum or distant sites submitted.\n\n**Microscopic description:** \"See diagnosis.\" – no separate microscopic narrative provided.\n\n**Diagnosis verbatim:**\n> ENDOCERVIX, ENDOMETRIUM, ENDOMETRIAL POLYPS, RESECTION (3 SAMPLES):\n> - ENDOMETRIOID PATTERN ENDOMETRIAL ADENOCARCINOMA, MODERATELY DIFFERENTIATED, FIGO II, WITHOUT EVIDENCE OF VASCULONEURAL INVASION IN THIS SAMPLE.\n> - SOME POLYPOID FRAGMENTS WITH ADENOCARCINOMA INVOLVEMENT.\n> - NO NORMAL ENDOCERVIX IDENTIFIED.\n> - SCARCE REPRESENTATION OF BASAL ENDOMETRIUM.\n\n**Comments verbatim:**\n> On Block 3, immunohistochemistry studies are suggested, especially to determine biomarker profile including Keratin Cocktail, P53, P16, Estrogen Receptors, Progesterone Receptors, Beta-Catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, and PMS2.\n\n---\n\n## DIAGNOSIS:\n**Endometrioid-pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated, FIGO histologic Grade 2.**\n\nReasoning:\n- Histological type as stated: endometrioid pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma.\n- Differentiation as stated: moderately differentiated.\n- \"FIGO II\" in the diagnosis line follows \"MODERATELY DIFFERENTIATED\" and in this context is interpreted as **FIGO histologic grade 2** (Grade 1 = well differentiated, Grade 2 = moderately differentiated, Grade 3 = poorly differentiated for endometrioid carcinoma under the traditional FIGO grading system), not as FIGO stage II. Rationale: (1) syntactic position after differentiation; (2) specimen is limited fragmented endometrial / endocervical / polyp material without myometrium, intact cervix, or extrauterine tissue required to assign stage; (3) report explicitly states \"NO NORMAL ENDOCERVIX IDENTIFIED,\" so cervical stromal status – required for stage II – cannot be assessed; the report does not state cervical stromal invasion. This grade-vs-stage ambiguity should be clarified with the signing pathologist, but on available text Grade 2 is the only supportable reading.\n- Additional diagnostic details: some polypoid fragments show adenocarcinoma involvement; scarce representation of basal endometrium; no normal endocervix identified in the material examined.\n\n## FIGO STAGE:\n**Cannot be assigned definitively from the available material – insufficient data for surgical staging. No FIGO surgical (or 2023 FIGO) stage can be established on this biopsy/curettage alone.**\n\nReasoning:\n- FIGO staging of endometrial carcinoma is surgical/pathological and requires at minimum total hysterectomy with assessment of: depth of myometrial invasion, cervical stromal involvement, uterine serosa/adnexa/vagina/parametria, lymph node status, and distant metastasis, plus histology/grade/LVSI/molecular for 2023 FIGO.\n- None of these staging elements is present here:\n  - No myometrium submitted – only mucohemorrhagic material and small tissue fragments (0.5 cc + 3 cc + 1 cc; Blocks 1-3); only \"SCARCE REPRESENTATION OF BASAL ENDOMETRIUM.\"\n  - No assessable endocervix – \"NO NORMAL ENDOCERVIX IDENTIFIED.\" Therefore cervical stromal invasion (which defines stage II in both 2009 and 2023 FIGO) is neither documented nor excludable.\n  - No lymph nodes, imaging findings, intraoperative findings, or extrauterine sites reported.\n- In particular, the \"FIGO II\" in the diagnosis line must NOT be taken as FIGO Stage II. Stage II requires histologically confirmed invasion of cervical stroma (2009 FIGO II; 2023 FIGO II), which is not described. To call Stage II on this material would be unsupported and misleading.\n- Most likely provisional clinical framing is carcinoma diagnosed on endometrial sampling / polyp with unknown depth and extent – i.e. incompletely staged disease pending full work-up and definitive surgery. Formal stage can only be assigned after guideline-concordant clinical staging (pelvic examination, endometrial + endocervical assessment, contrast-enhanced MRI/pelvic ultrasound, chest/abdomen imaging) and definitive surgical staging (hysterectomy + bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy + sentinel-node / nodal assessment ± omental / peritoneal staging as indicated).\n\n## MOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification):\n**Cannot be assigned from the available material. Data insufficient: POLE-ultramutated vs MMR-deficient vs p53-abnormal vs NSMP (no specific molecular profile) cannot be distinguished.**\n\nReasoning:\n- WHO 5th edition integrated endometrial carcinoma classification requires in hierarchical order: (1) POLE exonuclease domain mutation testing (sequencing) for POLE-ultramutated; (2) MMR protein IHC (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) for MMR-deficient; (3) p53 IHC (abnormal vs wild-type pattern) for p53-abnormal; (4) NSMP if none of the above and endometrioid histology.\n- This report contains **no IHC results and no molecular sequencing results**. It contains only a **recommendation** in COMMENTS: \"On Block 3, immunohistochemistry studies are suggested, especially to determine biomarker profile including Keratin Cocktail, P53, P16, Estrogen Receptors, Progesterone Receptors, Beta-Catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, and PMS2.\"\n- Suggested but not performed ≠ reported profile. Therefore assignment based on an IHC profile is impossible.\n- Additional gaps: suggested panel includes P53 and the four MMR proteins (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) which would address two of the three WHO decision points if performed and reported with proper controls and pattern interpretation (including MLH1 promoter methylation work-up if MLH1/PMS2 lost), but (a) results are absent, (b) POLE mutation testing is not listed in the suggestion and is mandatory to exclude POLE-ultramutated before assigning MMRd/p53abn/NSMP, and (c) ER/PR, P16, Beta-Catenin and Keratin Cocktail are not WHO molecular-subgroup determinants (they are ancillary/prognostic or differential-diagnosis markers).\n- Action required: perform and report MMR IHC with internal control assessment, p53 IHC with mutant-pattern vs wild-type interpretation, and POLE exonuclease domain (exons 9-14) sequencing, with integration per WHO/ESGO-ESTRO-ESP hierarchy, before any molecular subgroup label is applied.\n\n## LYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION:\n**Not identified in this sample – reported as absent with limited-sample caveat.**\n\nReasoningverbatim: \"WITHOUT EVIDENCE OF VASCULONEURAL INVASION IN THIS SAMPLE.\"\n- \"Vasculoneural\" is interpreted here as lymphovascular (translation variant for lymphovascular invasion – LVI/LVSI). Taken at face value: LVSI absent / not detected in Blocks 1-3 as examined.\n- Critical limitation explicitly stated by pathologist: \"IN THIS SAMPLE.\" Material is scant fragmented curettings (total ~4.5 cc across 3 blocks) with scarce basal endometrium. Focal or substantial LVSI can easily be missed without myometrium and without adequate sampling. Focal vs substantial/extensive LVSI (2023 FIGO / ESGO-ESTRO-ESP risk criterion, typically ≥5 vessels) cannot be distinguished here.\n- Therefore for tumour-board purposes: LVSI-negative on biopsy only – **not** definitive LVSI-negative for risk stratification; must be re-assessed on definitive hysterectomy with adequate sampling and reporting of focal vs substantial LVSI.\n\n## MYOMETRIAL INVASION:\n**Cannot be estimated. Depth unknown – no myometrium available for evaluation.**\n\nReasoning:\n- Macroscopic: only mucohemorrhagic material and small polyp fragments; no uterine wall strip, no measured myometrial thickness, no tumour-to-serosa distance.\n- Microscopic corollary: \"SCARCE REPRESENTATION OF BASAL ENDOMETRIUM\" only; no myometrium described, no percentage invasion (e.g. <50% vs ≥50%), no MELF/microcystic pattern, no serosal or parametrial assessment.\n- Any statement of \"superficial,\" \"deep,\" \"<50%\" or \"≥50%\" myometrial invasion would be invention and must not be made. Definitive depth requires hysterectomy (with gross mapping and microscopic measurement) correlated with preoperative MRI/transvaginal ultrasound.\n\n## KEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES:\n**Summary of features driving (and limiting) risk stratification on current data:**\n\nPositive / adverse established features:\n- Confirmed malignant histology: endometrioid-pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma involving endometrium and polypoid fragments (\"SOME POLYPOID FRAGMENTS WITH ADENOCARCINOMA INVOLVEMENT\") – i.e. multifocal sampled disease, not atypical hyperplasia/EIN.\n- Histologic grade: moderately differentiated / FIGO Grade 2 endometrioid – intermediate grade; Grade 2 endometrioid generally intermediate prognosis between Grade 1 and Grade 3, but grade alone does not define ESGO-ESTRO-ESP risk without stage, LVSI and molecular.\n- Age 47 years, pre-/perimenopausal age – younger than median endometrial cancer age; relevant for Lynch-syndrome consideration (especially with pending MMR), fertility already less central at 47 but germline / family-history review still indicated, and for surgical planning (ovarian management, genetics referral).\n\nFavourable / reassuring but provisional features (limited-sample only):\n- No lymphovascular invasion identified in the sampled fragments – if confirmed as truly negative and focal-only on hysterectomy, favours lower risk; if substantial LVSI found on resection, upstages risk substantially (2023 FIGO IIB / high-intermediate-high risk).\n\nUnknown / missing – these unknowns dominate risk and preclude ESGO-ESTRO-ESP / FIGO 2023 risk assignment:\n- Myometrial invasion depth/percentage, uterine size, serosal breach – unknown.\n- Cervical involvement (glandular vs stromal) – unknown; no normal endocervix to evaluate.\n- Lymph node status, adnexal/serosal/vaginal/parametrial spread, peritoneal cytology, distant metastasis – unknown.\n- LVSI extent (focal vs substantial), perineural invasion, margin status – cannot be assessed on fragments.\n- Molecular subgroup (POLEmut vs MMRd vs p53abn vs NSMP), plus ER/PR, L1CAM, CTNNB1/Beta-catenin, P16 – not yet tested; p53-abnormal would confer high risk regardless of other low-risk features, POLEmut would confer excellent prognosis and de-escalation potential, MMRd influences immunotherapy options and Lynch triage.\n- Lower-uterine-segment, multifocal polyp disease extent and background endometrium (hyperplasia vs atrophy) – not detailed beyond scarce basal endometrium.\n\nNet assessment: **biopsy-proven Grade 2 endometrioid endometrial carcinoma, incompletely staged, molecularly unclassified, LVSI-negative on limited sampling only – risk group unassignable until definitive surgery + full histopathology + IHC/molecular.** Must not be labelled low-risk on biopsy alone.\n\n## RECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT:\n**No definitive adjuvant (radiotherapy/chemotherapy/hormonal/immunotherapy) recommendation can be made from stage and molecular subgroup because neither is established. Recommended approach is urgent complete diagnostic work-up + guideline-concordant surgical staging + completion of the recommended (and expanded) biomarker testing, decided in a multidisciplinary gynaecologic-oncology tumour board. Outline only – local ESGO-ESTRO-ESP / NCCN / FIGO 2023 protocols and patient preferences/comorbidity govern final decisions:**\n\n1. **Urgent gynaecologic-oncology referral and tumour-board presentation.** 47-year-old with biopsy-proven Grade 2 endometrioid endometrial adenocarcinoma on outpatient sampling (05/07/2026 admission, 05/11/2026 report). Clarify with pathologist the \"FIGO II\" wording (confirm Grade 2 vs inadvertent stage label) and request pathology review / second opinion on Blocks 1-3 if institutional policy requires.\n\n2. **Complete the pathology/biomarker work-up before or in parallel with surgery:**\n   - Perform the IHC suggested on Block 3 with proper controls and pattern-based reporting: MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 (with MLH1 promoter methylation / BRAF as reflex if MLH1/PMS2 lost, plus Lynch genetics referral if MMRd especially at age 47); p53 (abnormal mutant-pattern: overexpression/null/cytoplasmic vs wild-type); ER/PR (prognostic and potential hormonal-therapy relevance); P16, Beta-Catenin, Keratin Cocktail as suggested for differential/ancillary context but not as substitutes for molecular subgroup.\n   - Add **POLE exonuclease domain mutation testing** (not in the suggested list but mandatory for WHO integrated classification and for 2023 FIGO IImPOLEmut / de-escalation decisions). Preserve Block 3 tissue; ensure sufficient tumour cellularity given scant material.\n   - Report LVSI with focal vs substantial quantification on resection, and ensure 2023 FIGO + WHO molecular-integrated synoptic reporting on hysterectomy.\n\n3. **Preoperative clinical staging:**\n   - Detailed history (bleeding, family cancer/Lynch spectrum, comorbidities, medications, fertility not relevant at 47 but contraception/pregnancy exclusion still needed, anaesthetic risk), focused pelvic/speculum/bimanual/rectovaginal examination, endocervical assessment (given \"no normal endocervix\" – exclude synchronous cervical pathology; consider endocervical curettage/colposcopy as indicated).\n   - Transvaginal ultrasound + contrast-enhanced pelvic MRI for myometrial depth, cervical stromal invasion, adnexal masses, nodal assessment; CT chest/abdomen/pelvis or as per local protocol to exclude extrauterine spread; baseline labs, CA-125 per institutional practice (non-specific, for correlation only).\n   - Counselling that biopsy LVSI-negative and polyp-limited appearance do not exclude deep invasion or nodal disease.\n\n4. **Definitive surgical staging (cornerstone, assuming operable disease confined clinically to uterus):**\n   - Total extrafascial hysterectomy + bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy + sentinel lymph-node mapping with ultrastaging (preferred where expertise exists) or systematic pelvic ± para-aortic lymphadenectomy per intraoperative findings and local guidelines; peritoneal washings/cytology, omental assessment, and thorough intra-abdominal exploration as indicated. Ovarian preservation is generally not appropriate for invasive carcinoma at 47; discuss menopause/HRT implications.\n   - Intraoperative frozen section only if it changes nodal surgery per local protocol; definitive decisions rest on final paraffin histology with full FIGO 2023 staging including molecular (FIGO 2023 stage IAmPOLEmut, IICm p53abn, etc.).\n   - Fertility-sparing / hormonal management is NOT appropriate on current data (invasive Grade 2 carcinoma with polyp involvement, not Grade 1 without invasion).\n\n5. **Adjuvant therapy – deferred until final stage + molecular + LVSI extent:**\n   - Do not commit to observation vs vaginal brachytherapy vs external-beam radiotherapy ± concurrent/adjuvant chemotherapy on biopsy alone. Final recommendation follows ESGO-ESTRO-ESP 2021/2023 risk stratification integrating: 2023 FIGO stage, grade, histotype, LVSI (focal vs substantial), nodal status, and molecular subgroup (e.g. POLEmut Stage I-II may allow de-escalation/observation; p53abn generally prompts intensification with chemoradiotherapy even in early stage; MMRd informs immunotherapy eligibility in advanced/recurrent setting and Lynch cascade testing).\n   - Discuss clinical-trial eligibility once molecular results available.\n\n6. **Genetics, counselling and follow-up:**\n   - Pedigree for Lynch-associated cancers (colorectal, endometrial, ovarian, urothelial, etc.); refer to genetics if MMRd, young onset, or suggestive family history. Counsel on surgical menopause, bone/cardiovascular health, sexuality, and surveillance schedule post-treatment.\n   - EnsureGP/patient receives clear message: diagnosis confirmed, stage and molecular pending, curative-intent surgical pathway planned, no adjuvant decision yet.\n\n*This summary is grounded solely in Report 2605694_EN as extracted above. Any management must be validated against full clinical examination, imaging, operative findings, final hysterectomy pathology, completed MMR/p53/POLE testing, and applicable national guidelines via the responsible gynaecologic-oncology multidisciplinary team.*",
