        "summary": "# Tumour Board Diagnostic Summary — Endometrial Pathology\n\n**Report:** Anatomical Pathology Report – Histological Study, Request No. **2605694**\n**Patient:** Female, **47 years** (anonymized), outpatient service\n**Admission:** 05/07/2026 · **Report date:** 05/11/2026\n**Specimens (3 samples, resection):**\n- Block 1 — \"Endocervix\": mucohemorrhagic material, combined volume **0.5 cc**\n- Block 2 — \"Endometrium\": mucohemorrhagic material, combined volume **3 cc**\n- Block 3 — \"Endometrial polyps\": multiple light-brown tissue fragments with raw surfaces, combined volume **1 cc**\n\n> **Critical framing for the board:** this is a **small-volume endometrial/endocervical resection–curettage specimen (total ≈4.5 cc, 3 blocks)**, *not* a hysterectomy. Microscopic description states only \"See diagnosis.\" No myometrium, no uterine serosa, no adnexa, no parametria and no lymph nodes are present. Therefore several staging and risk parameters below are, by specimen type, **not assessable** rather than negative.\n\n---\n\n## DIAGNOSIS\n\n**Endometrioid pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated — FIGO grade 2 (G2).**\n\nVerbatim from the report:\n\n> \"ENDOCERVIX, ENDOMETRIUM, ENDOMETRIAL POLYPS, RESECTION (3 SAMPLES):\n> – ENDOMETRIOID PATTERN ENDOMETRIAL ADENOCARCINOMA, MODERATELY DIFFERENTIATED, FIGO II, WITHOUT EVIDENCE OF VASCULONEURAL INVASION IN THIS SAMPLE.\n> – SOME POLYPOID FRAGMENTS WITH ADENOCARCINOMA INVOLVEMENT.\n> – NO NORMAL ENDOCERVIX IDENTIFIED.\n> – SCARCE REPRESENTATION OF BASAL ENDOMETRIUM.\"\n\n- **Histological type:** endometrioid adenocarcinoma (endometrioid pattern) — a *non-aggressive / low-grade* histotype in the WHO 5th edition and FIGO 2023 framework.\n- **Grade:** **FIGO grade 2** (moderately differentiated). The report's \"**FIGO II**\" is a **grading** statement, not a stage: it is written in the same clause as, and as the numeric equivalent of, \"MODERATELY DIFFERENTIATED,\" which is the definition of FIGO grade 2 (6–50% solid non-squamous/non-morular growth). It must **not** be transcribed into the tumour-board record as \"FIGO stage II\" — that would wrongly imply cervical stromal invasion, which is neither stated nor assessable here (see below).\n- **Additional findings:** carcinoma involves **some of the polypoid fragments** (Block 3), i.e. the tumour has a documented polyp-associated component. **No normal endocervix was identified** in Block 1, and there is **scarce representation of basal endometrium** in the endometrial sampling.\n- Not reported / not evaluable in this material: tumour size, unequivocal serous/clear-cell/undifferentiated or carcinosarcoma component (none described), MELF-type invasion, and background endometrium (hyperplasia/atypical hyperplasia not characterized).\n\n---\n\n## FIGO STAGE\n\n**Cannot be assigned from this specimen — pathological (surgical) FIGO stage is indeterminate. Clinically the disease is presumed early-stage (cT1 Nx Mx) pending hysterectomy and imaging.**\n\nReasoning:\n1. FIGO staging of endometrial carcinoma (both FIGO 2009 and FIGO 2023) is a **surgical–pathological** system requiring assessment of **myometrial invasion depth, cervical stromal involvement, serosa/adnexa, parametria, vagina, peritoneum and lymph nodes**. None of these structures is present in a 3-block endocervical/endometrial/polyp curettage–resection.\n2. The specimen contains **no myometrium**; furthermore, \"scarce representation of **basal endometrium**\" means even the endometrial–myometrial junction is under-represented, so the endometrium-versus-myoinvasion question cannot be approached indirectly.\n3. The \"Endocervix\" fraction is **non-informative** — \"no normal endocervix identified\" documents an inadequate/unrepresentative cervical sample. It **neither confirms nor excludes** cervical stromal invasion, and fractional curettage cannot reliably establish cervical stromal invasion in any case (contamination/false-positive and sampling false-negative both occur). Stage II therefore cannot be claimed on this material.\n4. **Anticipated stage if hysterectomy confirms the current picture:** carcinoma limited to an endometrial polyp/endometrium with no LVSI in a grade 2 endometrioid (non-aggressive) tumour would correspond to **FIGO 2023 Stage IA1** (≈ FIGO 2009 Stage IA); <50% myometrial invasion would be **FIGO 2023 Stage IA2** (FIGO 2009 IA); ≥50% invasion would be **Stage IB**; cervical stromal invasion would be **Stage II**. This is an expectation, **not** an assigned stage.\n5. Note also that **FIGO 2023 stage can be modified by molecular class** (e.g. *POLE*-mutated early-stage disease is down-staged to **IAm<sub>POLE-mut</sub>**; p53-abnormal disease with any myoinvasion is up-staged to **IICm<sub>p53abn</sub>**). Because the molecular class is unknown here (below), even a future stage assignment must be issued as molecularly-integrated only after the recommended testing.\n\n---\n\n## MOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification)\n\n**Cannot be assigned — no molecular or immunohistochemical results are available. The subgroup is PENDING / UNCLASSIFIED (WHO \"NSMP\" must NOT be assigned by default).**\n\n- The report's COMMENTS section only **suggests** testing: \"On Block 3, immunohistochemistry studies are suggested, especially to determine biomarker profile including **Keratin Cocktail, P53, P16, Estrogen Receptors, Progesterone Receptors, Beta-Catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, and PMS2**.\" No stains were performed, and **no result** for any marker is reported.\n- Consequently: **MMR status unknown** (MLH1/PMS2/MSH2/MSH6 not done), **p53 status unknown**, and ***POLE* status unknown** — no *POLE* exonuclease-domain sequencing is mentioned or recommended in the report, yet it is obligatory for the WHO/ProMisE algorithm.\n- **NSMP is a diagnosis of exclusion** requiring a *POLE* wild-type, MMR-proficient, p53 wild-type result. With zero results in hand, defaulting to NSMP would be a category error; the honest classification is **\"molecular subgroup not determined on available material.\"**\n- **Feasibility:** the material is adequate in principle — the pathologist specifically nominated **Block 3 (endometrial polyps, 1 cc, carcinoma-involved)** for the panel, so IHC ± *POLE* sequencing can be run on existing blocks now, without re-biopsy, and repeated/confirmed later on the hysterectomy specimen.\n- **Caveat for the board:** p53 and MMR IHC on curettage material is generally concordant with hysterectomy, so an early result is actionable for surgical planning; discordances (especially subclonal p53 and \"multiple-classifier\" tumours, where *POLE* takes precedence) should be resolved on the resection specimen.\n\n---\n\n## LYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION\n\n**Not identified in the submitted material — reported as \"WITHOUT EVIDENCE OF VASCULONEURAL INVASION IN THIS SAMPLE.\" This is a negative finding of limited value, not a validated \"LVSI-absent\" status.**\n\n- The pathologist's own qualifier \"**in this sample**\" flags the sampling limitation.\n- Curettage/resection fragments lack intact myometrial vasculature and the tumour–myometrium interface, where LVSI is normally detected; a negative result here has low negative predictive value.\n- The FIGO 2023 / ESGO–ESTRO–ESP distinction between **focal** and **substantial (extensive) LVSI** — the parameter that actually drives adjuvant therapy and which up-stages non-aggressive tumours to **Stage IIB** when substantial — **cannot be evaluated** and must be re-assessed on the hysterectomy specimen.\n- The wording \"vasculoneural\" also implies perineural invasion was not seen; likewise not assessable.\n\n---\n\n## MYOMETRIAL INVASION\n\n**Not assessable — depth cannot be estimated from this specimen.**\n\n- The macroscopic description lists only mucohemorrhagic material and polypoid tissue fragments (0.5 cc + 3 cc + 1 cc); **no myometrium was submitted or identified**, and the report makes no statement about myoinvasion.\n- \"**Scarce representation of basal endometrium**\" explicitly documents that even the deep/basal endometrial compartment is poorly represented, further precluding any inference about the endomyometrial junction.\n- The presence of \"some polypoid fragments with adenocarcinoma involvement\" raises but does **not** resolve the possibility of a polyp-confined (non-invasive) carcinoma; conversely, absence of myometrium in a curettage never excludes deep invasion.\n- **Depth of invasion must be determined on the hysterectomy specimen** (with preoperative contrast-enhanced pelvic MRI as an interim estimate), reported as none / <50% / ≥50% of myometrial thickness.\n\n---\n\n## KEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES\n\n**Favourable / low-risk-leaning (from available data)**\n- **Endometrioid histotype** — WHO \"non-aggressive\" (low-grade) histological type.\n- **FIGO grade 2** (moderately differentiated) — intermediate, but still within the low-grade (G1–G2) category.\n- **No LVSI (and no perineural invasion) identified** in the examined fragments.\n- **Carcinoma involving endometrial polyps**, compatible with a possible polyp-confined/early tumour (would be FIGO 2023 IA1 if confirmed).\n- **No serous, clear-cell, undifferentiated or sarcomatous component described**; no extrauterine disease documented in this material.\n\n**Unfavourable / uncertainty-generating (drive the risk stratification gaps)**\n- **Molecular class entirely unknown** — the single largest prognostic gap. p53-abnormal would convert this into high-risk disease even at an early stage (and up-stage it to IICm under FIGO 2023); *POLE*-ultramutated would make it excellent-prognosis and a de-escalation candidate; MMR-deficient carries intermediate prognosis plus **Lynch syndrome** and immunotherapy implications.\n- **Myometrial invasion depth unknown**; **cervical stromal involvement unassessable** (Block 1 yielded no normal endocervix); **nodal status unknown**.\n- **Grade 2 + curettage sampling** — grade is upgraded on hysterectomy in a non-trivial proportion of cases, and a high-grade or non-endometrioid component may still be revealed.\n- **ER/PR status unknown** (recommended but not performed) — relevant to prognosis, to NSMP sub-stratification (ER-positive NSMP behaves better), and to any hormonal therapy option.\n- **Negative LVSI is unreliable** in this specimen type, and **substantial vs focal LVSI** cannot be graded.\n- **Age 47 (premenopausal)** — mandates Lynch syndrome consideration (age <50 with endometrial cancer), discussion of ovarian preservation/surgical menopause, and awareness of synchronous ovarian primaries in younger women with endometrioid carcinoma.\n\n**Net assessment:** the data support a **low-grade, non-aggressive endometrioid endometrial carcinoma, most likely early-stage**, but a definitive ESGO/ESTRO/ESP risk group (low vs intermediate vs high-intermediate vs high) **cannot be assigned** until surgical staging and molecular classification are complete.\n\n---\n\n## RECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT\n\n### 1. Complete the pathology work-up now (on existing blocks)\n- Perform the **IHC panel recommended on Block 3**: **MLH1, PMS2, MSH2, MSH6** (MMR), **p53**, **ER, PR**, **beta-catenin**, **p16**, and **keratin cocktail** (the latter for confirmation of epithelial nature / assessment of invasion patterns and, with p16/ER, to help distinguish endometrial from endocervical origin given the non-representative endocervical block).\n- **Add *POLE* exonuclease-domain sequencing (exons 9–14)** — not listed in the report but **mandatory** for the WHO 5th-edition/FIGO 2023 integrated classification and the only way to identify the de-escalation-eligible group.\n- Apply the **ProMisE/WHO hierarchy**: *POLE*-mutated → MMR-deficient → p53-abnormal → NSMP, and report **multiple classifiers** by that precedence.\n- **Reflex *MLH1* promoter hypermethylation** testing if MLH1/PMS2 loss is found; **germline testing / genetics referral** for unmethylated MLH1 loss or any MSH2/MSH6/PMS2 loss — reinforced by the patient's age of 47.\n\n### 2. Staging work-up before surgery\n- **Contrast-enhanced pelvic MRI** (best assessment of myometrial invasion depth and cervical stromal involvement — the two parameters left open by this specimen) plus **CT chest/abdomen/pelvis** (or PET-CT if extrauterine disease is suspected).\n- Baseline **CA-125** (optional; useful if extrauterine disease suspected), and preoperative anaesthetic/comorbidity assessment.\n\n### 3. Definitive surgery (the step that actually establishes the FIGO stage)\n- **Total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy**, preferably by **minimally invasive (laparoscopic/robotic) approach**, with **intact uterine removal** and avoidance of morcellation; **peritoneal assessment** with washings for cytology (recorded, though washings do not alter FIGO stage).\n- **Bilateral sentinel lymph node mapping with ultrastaging** — appropriate for apparent early-stage disease; convert to full pelvic ± para-aortic lymphadenectomy only for failed mapping or per institutional protocol/high-risk findings.\n- **Ovarian preservation:** generally **not** the default at age 47; bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy is recommended, with the option discussed individually only if disease proves grade 1–2, clinically confined to the uterus, without Lynch syndrome and without a family history of ovarian/breast cancer. **Bilateral salpingectomy at minimum** if ovaries are conserved. Counsel on surgical menopause and hormone-replacement options.\n- **Fertility-sparing progestin therapy is NOT indicated** here: it is restricted to grade 1 endometrioid carcinoma with no myometrial invasion, and this tumour is **grade 2** with invasion status unknown. Primary progestin/levonorgestrel-IUD therapy would be reserved for patients medically unfit for surgery.\n\n### 4. Adjuvant therapy — defer decision, then use molecularly integrated risk groups\nNo adjuvant recommendation can be finalized until final stage, grade, LVSI quantification and molecular class are available. Planned logic (ESGO/ESTRO/ESP, molecularly integrated):\n- ***POLE*-ultramutated, early stage (I–II):** excellent prognosis — **omit adjuvant treatment** (de-escalation), observation only.\n- **MMR-deficient, Stage IA–IB, grade 2, no/focal LVSI:** low to intermediate risk — **observation or vaginal brachytherapy**; MMR-d status also flags **Lynch syndrome screening** and makes **immune checkpoint inhibition** (e.g. pembrolizumab/dostarlimab with chemotherapy) the preferred option in the advanced/recurrent setting.\n- **p53-abnormal with any myometrial invasion:** high risk (**FIGO 2023 Stage IICm**) — **adjuvant chemotherapy (carboplatin–paclitaxel) ± external beam radiotherapy**; consider **HER2 testing** and trastuzumab-containing strategies if a serous-like/HER2-positive phenotype emerges.\n- **NSMP (if confirmed):** manage by classical pathology — **Stage IA, G2, LVSI-negative → low risk, observation** (vaginal brachytherapy optional, more so if age >60); **Stage IB or substantial LVSI or cervical stromal invasion → intermediate/high-intermediate risk → vaginal brachytherapy ± external beam radiotherapy**, with chemotherapy added for high-risk/advanced disease. ER/PR positivity supports endocrine options in the recurrent setting.\n\n### 5. Multidisciplinary and supportive measures\n- **Re-present at the gynae-oncology tumour board** once the hysterectomy pathology, sentinel node ultrastaging and the molecular panel are reported, to issue a **definitive molecularly integrated FIGO 2023 stage and risk group**.\n- **Genetic counselling referral** if MMR-deficient/Lynch-suspicious, with cascade testing and colorectal/gastric surveillance as indicated.\n- **Menopausal symptom management**, bone health, sexual health/vaginal dilator counselling if pelvic radiotherapy is used, and **weight/metabolic risk optimization** (obesity, diabetes, hypertension are standard endometrioid carcinoma co-factors — not documented in this report).\n- **Surveillance** after treatment: symptom-directed clinical review every 3–6 months for 2–3 years, then 6–12 monthly to 5 years; imaging as clinically indicated. Counsel on postmenopausal/abnormal bleeding as the key recurrence symptom.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Field | Answer | Basis / limitation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Diagnosis** | Endometrioid adenocarcinoma of endometrium, moderately differentiated, **FIGO grade 2**; involves endometrial polyp fragments | Report diagnosis line; \"FIGO II\" = **grade** 2, not stage |\n| **FIGO stage** | **Not assignable** (indeterminate); clinically presumed early-stage cT1 Nx Mx; would be FIGO 2023 IA1 if polyp/endometrium-confined at hysterectomy | Curettage/resection specimen, 3 blocks, ≈4.5 cc; no myometrium, cervix stroma, adnexa or nodes |\n| **Molecular subgroup** | **Cannot be assigned — pending**. Not NSMP by default | IHC only *suggested* on Block 3; no results; no *POLE* testing ordered |\n| **Lymphovascular invasion** | **Not identified** (\"no evidence of vasculoneural invasion **in this sample**\") — unreliable negative; focal vs substantial LVSI not evaluable | Pathologist's own qualifier; specimen type |\n| **Myometrial invasion** | **Not assessable** — no myometrium present; \"scarce representation of basal endometrium\" | Macroscopic description; no myoinvasion statement |\n| **Key prognostic features** | Favourable: endometrioid type, G2, no LVSI seen, polyp involvement. Unfavourable/unknown: molecular class, invasion depth, cervical/nodal status, ER/PR, age 47 (Lynch screening) | Report content + specimen limitations |\n| **Recommended management** | Complete IHC (MMR, p53, ER/PR, p16, beta-catenin, keratin) **+ *POLE* sequencing** on Block 3; pelvic MRI + CT; total hysterectomy + BSO with sentinel node mapping/ultrastaging; adjuvant therapy decided afterwards by molecularly integrated FIGO 2023 stage/ESGO risk group; Lynch screening/genetics | Standard of care applied to reported findings |\n\n**Bottom line for the board:** a histologically confirmed **FIGO grade 2 endometrioid endometrial adenocarcinoma involving endometrial polyps, with no LVSI seen in a limited curettage/resection sample**. The report supplies a **grade, not a stage** — \"FIGO II\" must be recorded as grade 2 — and the **FIGO stage, myometrial invasion depth and molecular subgroup are all currently undeterminable**. Surgical staging plus the recommended IHC panel with added *POLE* sequencing are the two actions that will convert this into a fully classified, treatment-defining diagnosis.",
