        "summary": "# Endometrial Pathology - Tumour Board Summary\nReport No. 2605694 - Source: /app/attachments/report_2605694_EN.pdf\n\n**Patient:** 47-year-old Female\n**Admission:** 05/07/2026 **Report Date:** 05/11/2026\n**Service:** OUTPATIENT\n**Specimens submitted as:** Labeled \"Endocervix\": mucohemorrhagic material 0.5 cc, Block 1; Labeled \"Endometrium\": mucohemorrhagic material 3 cc, Block 2; Labeled \"Endometrial polyps\": multiple light brown tissue fragments with raw surfaces, combined volume 1 cc, Block 3. Process labeled \"ENDOCERVIX, ENDOMETRIUM, ENDOMETRIAL POLYPS, RESECTION (3 SAMPLES)\"\n\n---\n\nDIAGNOSIS:\nEndometrioid pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated, FIGO Grade 2. Verbatim diagnosis from report: \"ENDOMETRIOID PATTERN ENDOMETRIAL ADENOCARCINOMA, MODERATELY DIFFERENTIATED, FIGO II, WITHOUT EVIDENCE OF VASCULONEURAL INVASION IN THIS SAMPLE.\" Additional findings: Some polypoid fragments with adenocarcinoma involvement (Block 3). No normal endocervix identified. Scarce representation of basal endometrium.\n\nThe report's \"FIGO II\" is interpreted as FIGO histologic grade (Grade 2 = moderately differentiated, 6-50% solid non-squamous growth by definition), not FIGO surgical stage, as no hysterectomy/myometrial assessment was performed.\n\nFIGO STAGE:\nCannot assign a definitive FIGO surgical stage from the available material.\n\nReasoning: The material is limited fragmented curettage/hysteroscopic resection specimens (0.5 cc endocervix, 3 cc endometrium, 1 cc polyps), not a hysterectomy with en bloc uterus, cervix, adnexa and lymph nodes. There is scarce basal endometrium, no myometrium present for depth assessment, no normal endocervix identified to evaluate cervical stromal invasion, and no lymph node, adnexal, omental or peritoneal staging tissue. The diagnosis states tumor present in endometrial and polypoid fragments and endocervical material labeled but without normal endocervix, so extrauterine spread cannot be assessed. Definitive surgical staging (FIGO 2023/2009) requires total hysterectomy + bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy with pathologic assessment of myometrial invasion, cervical stromal invasion, serosa, adnexa, lymph nodes and extrauterine disease.\n\nIf forced to provide a provisional biopsy-based impression: disease documented in endometrium/polyps only, without demonstrable lymphovascular invasion in sample, without assessable myometrium; confined disease cannot be assumed and staging is incomplete. Formal staging deferred pending hysterectomy.\n\nMOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification):\nCannot be assigned from the available material.\n\nReasoning: No immunohistochemistry results are reported in document 2605694. In COMMENTS, IHC studies are only SUGGESTED on Block 3, especially to determine biomarker profile including: Keratin Cocktail, P53, P16, Estrogen Receptors, Progesterone Receptors, Beta-Catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, and PMS2. Since p53 IHC (for p53-abnormal), MMR protein IHC (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 for MMR-deficient), and POLE sequencing were not performed/reported, classification into POLE-ultramutated, MMR-deficient, p53-abnormal, or NSMP (no specific molecular profile) is not possible per WHO 5th edition. Integrated classification requires those results.\n\nLYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION:\nAbsent in the examined samples as per report wording: \"WITHOUT EVIDENCE OF VASCULONEURAL INVASION IN THIS SAMPLE.\"\n\nCaveat: Assessment is limited to small fragmented curettage/polypectomy material (Blocks 1-3, total 4.5 cc). Scarcity of basal endometrium and absence of myometrium reduces sensitivity for detecting LVSI. True LVSI status should be re-evaluated on definitive hysterectomy specimen.\n\nMYOMETRIAL INVASION:\nCannot be determined / not assessable from this specimen.\n\nSpecimen findings: Mucohemorrhagic material and polypoid fragments only, with \"SCARCE REPRESENTATION OF BASAL ENDOMETRIUM\" and no myometrium described macro- or microscopically. No normal endocervix identified. No depth measurement, percentage, or <50% vs >=50% invasion can be provided. Myometrial invasion depth requires hysterectomy specimen.\n\nKEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES:\n- Histotype: Endometrioid pattern (favorable compared to high-risk histotypes like serous, clear cell, carcinosarcoma) - from DIAGNOSIS.\n- Grade: Moderately differentiated, FIGO Grade 2 / FIGO II - intermediate grade. Implies higher risk than Grade 1, lower than Grade 3.\n- Age: 47 years.\n- LVSI: Reported absent in this limited sample - favorable if confirmed on hysterectomy, but limited representation.\n- Myometrial invasion: Unknown - critical missing prognostic factor for risk stratification; scarce basal endometrium only.\n- Cervical involvement: Indeterminate - specimen labeled \"Endocervix\" 0.5 cc but \"NO NORMAL ENDOCERVIX IDENTIFIED\"; cannot rule out vs. contamination by endometrial tumor; no cervical stroma to assess stage II.\n- Polyp involvement: Some polypoid fragments with adenocarcinoma involvement (Block 3, 1 cc) - indicates tumor within polyps, may be origin or colonization.\n- Molecular status: Unknown - pending recommended IHC panel: Keratin Cocktail, P53, P16, ER, PR, Beta-Catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2. MMR-deficient would imply Lynch screening need; p53-abnormal would upstage risk; POLEmut would be favorable. Cannot assign NSMP until p53 wild-type and MMR proficient confirmed and POLE wild-type assumed.\n- Specimen limitations: Fragmented, low volume (0.5 + 3 + 1 cc), mucohemorrhagic, scarce basal endometrium - limits assessment of invasion, LVSI, and heterogeneity.\n\nRECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT:\nBased on endometrioid FIGO Grade 2 adenocarcinoma on biopsy without definitive stage or molecular subgroup:\n\n1.  Complete recommended biomarker workup on available Block 3 as suggested in report: IHC panel Keratin Cocktail, P53 (to identify p53-abnormal), P16, Estrogen Receptors, Progesterone Receptors, Beta-Catenin, and MMR proteins MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 (for MMR-deficient). If MMR loss, reflex MLH1 methylation and germline Lynch syndrome evaluation per guidelines. Consider POLE exonuclease domain mutation testing (exons 9-14) per WHO 5th/European guidelines for full integrated molecular classification, as IHC alone cannot detect POLEmut.\n\n2.  Referral to gynecologic oncology tumour board. Preoperative workup: detailed history, pelvic exam, transvaginal ultrasound and/or pelvic MRI to assess myometrial invasion <50% vs >=50%, cervical stromal invasion, adnexal disease; CT chest/abdomen/pelvis or as per local staging protocol to rule out extrauterine disease.\n\n3.  Definitive surgical staging: total hysterectomy + bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy, with peritoneal cytology (if collected), omental assessment, and sentinel lymph node mapping / systematic pelvic and para-aortic lymphadenectomy per FIGO 2023 surgical staging and ESGO-ESTRO-ESP risk guidelines. En bloc removal will allow final histotype, confirmation of grade (Grade 2), definitive myometrial invasion depth estimation, cervical stromal invasion, LVSI quantification (focal vs substantial/extensive), and FIGO stage assignment (e.g., Stage I-II if confined).\n\n4.  Risk stratification and adjuvant therapy to be determined postoperatively after integration of surgical stage + final LVSI status + molecular subgroup: \n    - If POLEmut: generally favorable, de-escalation may be considered even if high-grade features.\n    - If MMR-deficient: intermediate risk; immunotherapy considerations per recurrence; Lynch workup.\n    - If p53-abnormal: upstaged to high-risk regardless of otherwise low stage, typically prompting adjuvant chemoradiotherapy.\n    - If NSMP (p53 wild-type, MMR proficient, POLE wild-type) with grade 2: management by stage, depth of myometrial invasion, LVSI, cervical involvement per ESGO-ESTRO-ESP guidelines (vaginal brachytherapy vs EBRT vs chemoradiation).\n    \n    Until definitive staging, counsel patient that grade 2 endometrioid carcinoma confined to endometrium without LVSI would be intermediate risk, but final plan awaits surgery and molecular results.\n\n5.  Documentation: Preserve specimen identifiers (Blocks 1-3, Request No. 2605694, Dates 05/07/2026-05/11/2026) and ensure pathology addendum with IHC results is issued.",
