        "summary": "# Diagnostic summary — Request No. 2605694\n\nSource: anatomical pathology report, outpatient histological study. Female, 47 years. Admission 05/07/2026; report date 05/11/2026. Specimens (resection, 3 samples): endocervix (mucohemorrhagic material, 0.5 cc, Block 1); endometrium (mucohemorrhagic material, 3 cc, Block 2); endometrial polyps (multiple light brown fragments with raw surfaces, 1 cc, Block 3). Microscopic description: “See diagnosis.” No hysterectomy, myometrium, adnexa, lymph nodes, or completed biomarker results are in this report.\n\n## DIAGNOSIS\n\nEndometrioid-pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated, FIGO grade II.\n\nThe report wording is “ENDOMETRIOID PATTERN ENDOMETRIAL ADENOCARCINOMA, MODERATELY DIFFERENTIATED, FIGO II.” In this line, FIGO II is the histologic grade (grade 2), paired with “moderately differentiated,” not a surgical stage. Tumour is also present in some polypoid fragments. Histotype is reported as endometrioid pattern; confirmatory IHC was only suggested, not performed, so the pattern has not been further subclassified on this material.\n\n## FIGO STAGE\n\nFIGO surgical stage cannot be assigned from the available material.\n\nThis is an outpatient curettage/polyp resection, not a staging hysterectomy. There is no assessment of myometrial invasion depth, cervical stromal invasion, serosal or adnexal involvement, lymph nodes, or distant disease. “No normal endocervix identified” is a sampling statement (Block 1 was 0.5 cc of mucohemorrhagic material); it is not a documented finding of carcinoma in cervical stroma and must not be read as FIGO stage II. The “FIGO II” in the diagnosis is grade only.\n\n## MOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification)\n\nThe molecular subgroup cannot be assigned from the available material.\n\nNo IHC or molecular results are reported. The comment only suggests studies on Block 3 (keratin cocktail, p53, p16, estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, beta-catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, and PMS2). POLE status is not mentioned. Without performed MMR, p53, and POLE results, the case cannot be placed in POLE-ultramutated, MMR-deficient, p53-abnormal, or NSMP.\n\n## LYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION\n\nNot identified in this sample (reported as absent on the material examined).\n\nThe diagnosis states “WITHOUT EVIDENCE OF VASCULONEURAL INVASION IN THIS SAMPLE.” That statement covers vascular and neural invasion and is limited to these curettings. It does not exclude lymphovascular invasion in unsampled myometrium.\n\n## MYOMETRIAL INVASION\n\nDepth cannot be estimated.\n\nNo myometrium was submitted. The diagnosis notes scarce representation of basal endometrium, so the endometrial–myometrial junction is barely sampled. Invasion depth (none / <50% / ≥50%) is not assessable.\n\n## KEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES\n\nFeatures present on this report:\n- Histotype reported as endometrioid-pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma (IHC confirmation still suggested).\n- FIGO grade 2 (moderately differentiated).\n- Adenocarcinoma in some polypoid fragments; not stated to be confined to a polyp.\n- Vasculoneural invasion not identified in this sample.\n- Age 47 years.\n\nFeatures required for risk stratification that are absent:\n- Myometrial invasion depth (scarce basal endometrium only; no myometrium).\n- Cervical stromal involvement (no normal endocervix identified; involvement not documented).\n- Lymphovascular invasion on a hysterectomy specimen, nodal status, adnexa, and peritoneal findings — not in this specimen.\n- Molecular subgroup (suggested IHC not done; POLE not tested).\n\nRisk group and adjuvant indication therefore cannot be determined from this report alone. Grade 2 endometrioid-pattern carcinoma without vasculoneural invasion in the biopsy is not, by itself, a complete prognostic profile.\n\n## RECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT\n\nStage- and molecular-adapted management cannot be defined from this report, because surgical stage and molecular subgroup are both unavailable.\n\nReport-supported next steps:\n1. Perform the immunohistochemistry panel suggested on Block 3 (keratin cocktail, p53, p16, ER, PR, beta-catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) to support histotype and to assess MMR and p53 status.\n2. That suggested panel does not include POLE testing. Full WHO integrated classification still requires POLE status, because POLE-ultramutated takes precedence over MMR and p53 categories.\n3. Do not select adjuvant radiotherapy or chemotherapy from this curettage. Myometrial invasion, cervical stromal invasion, definitive lymphovascular invasion, nodal status, and molecular subgroup are not established.\n4. FIGO surgical stage can be assigned only after a staging hysterectomy specimen (or equivalent surgical staging) is examined. Tumour-board decisions on extent of surgery already performed or planned, and on adjuvant therapy, should wait for that pathology plus the pending biomarker profile.",
