        "summary": "DIAGNOSIS:\nEndometrioid-pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated (FIGO grade 2).\n\nThe diagnosis is taken from the resection of three samples labeled endocervix, endometrium, and endometrial polyps (request 2605694; report date 05/11/2026). Some polypoid fragments contain adenocarcinoma. No normal endocervix is identified. Basal endometrium is only scarcely represented.\n\nFIGO STAGE:\nCannot be assigned from the available material.\n\nThis specimen is limited sampling (endocervix 0.5 cc mucohemorrhagic material; endometrium 3 cc mucohemorrhagic material; endometrial polyps 1 cc tissue fragments), not a hysterectomy with surgical staging. There is no reported myometrium, no assessment of cervical stromal invasion as a staged finding, no serosa/adnexa, and no lymph nodes. “FIGO II” in the diagnosis is paired with “moderately differentiated” and is therefore interpreted as FIGO histologic grade 2, not surgical stage II. Absence of identifiable normal endocervix does not constitute documented cervical stromal invasion and cannot be used to infer stage II.\n\nMOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification):\nCannot be assigned from the available material.\n\nNo POLE sequencing is reported. Immunohistochemistry was only recommended, not performed. The pathologist suggested a biomarker panel on Block 3 (keratin cocktail, p53, p16, estrogen receptors, progesterone receptors, beta-catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, and PMS2). Without completed MMR/p53 IHC (and POLE status), the tumour cannot be placed in POLE-ultramutated, MMR-deficient, p53-abnormal, or NSMP.\n\nLYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION:\nAbsent in this sample.\n\nThe report states “without evidence of vasculoneural invasion in this sample.” This negative finding is limited to the submitted fragments and does not exclude LVSI in unsampled uterus.\n\nMYOMETRIAL INVASION:\nCannot be estimated.\n\nOnly scarce basal endometrium is represented. No myometrial tissue or depth of invasion is described. Invasion relative to myometrial thickness (none / <50% / ≥50%) is therefore unknown.\n\nKEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES:\n- Histotype/grade: endometrioid adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated (FIGO grade 2).\n- Specimen limitation: polypectomy/endometrial–endocervical sampling only; adenocarcinoma involves some polypoid fragments.\n- LVSI/vasculoneural invasion: not identified in this sample.\n- Myometrial invasion: not assessable (scarce basal endometrium; no myometrium reported).\n- Cervical status: no normal endocervix identified; cervical stromal invasion not established.\n- Molecular classification: not available; IHC/biomarker profile recommended but not performed.\n- Patient: 47-year-old woman; admission 05/07/2026.\n\nThese features are insufficient for formal ESGO/ESTRO/ESP-style risk group assignment, which requires hysterectomy findings (myometrial invasion, true LVSI, cervical stromal invasion) plus integrated molecular classification.\n\nRECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT:\nDefinitive stage- and molecular-directed therapy cannot be recommended from this report alone.\n\nGrounded in the reported findings:\n1. Complete the suggested immunohistochemistry on Block 3 (MMR proteins MLH1/MSH2/MSH6/PMS2, p53, and the remainder of the recommended panel) and obtain POLE testing if the integrated WHO classification is required for tumour-board decision-making.\n2. Proceed to complete surgical staging (hysterectomy with assessment of myometrial invasion, cervix, adnexa, and lymph nodes as indicated) because current material cannot assign FIGO surgical stage or depth of invasion.\n3. Defer adjuvant radiotherapy/chemotherapy selection until surgical stage and molecular subgroup are known. Grade 2 endometrioid carcinoma without documented LVSI, without known myometrial invasion, and without molecular results does not allow a reliable adjuvant plan from this sampling.\n\nUntil those data are available, management should be regarded as incomplete staging work-up rather than treatment of a defined FIGO stage or WHO molecular subgroup.",
