        "summary": "# Tumour Board Diagnostic Summary — Report 2605694\n\n**Specimen:** Endocervix, endometrium, endometrial polyps (curettage/polypectomy-type material — fragmented \"mucohemorrhagic\" tissue and polyp fragments; no intact hysterectomy specimen submitted)\n**Patient:** Female, 47 years | Admission 05/07/2026 | Report Date 05/11/2026\n\n---\n\n## DIAGNOSIS\n**Endometrioid-pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated, FIGO grade 2**, with tumour involvement of some polypoid fragments (endometrial polyp[s] harboring adenocarcinoma). No normal endocervical tissue was identified in the endocervical sample, and only scarce basal endometrium is represented.\n\n*(Note: the report's \"FIGO II\" designation refers to the histologic **grade** of the endometrioid carcinoma — corresponding to \"moderately differentiated\" — not to the surgical FIGO **stage**.)*\n\n---\n\n## FIGO STAGE\n**Cannot be definitively assigned from this material.** The specimen consists of curettage/polyp fragments only (endocervix, endometrium, endometrial polyps), not a hysterectomy specimen. There is no myometrium available for assessment (only \"scarce representation of basal endometrium\"), no cervical stroma to evaluate for stromal invasion (no normal endocervix identified, and no invasive component described there), and no adnexal, lymph node, or peritoneal/omental sampling. FIGO 2023 surgical staging for endometrial carcinoma requires assessment of the depth of myometrial invasion, cervical stromal involvement, adnexal/serosal/peritoneal spread, and lymph node status — none of which can be determined from curettage material.\n\nBased on the tissue available, the disease is **at minimum consistent with an intrauterine endometrioid adenocarcinoma (component of a FIGO Stage I–II picture)**, but formal staging requires either a definitive hysterectomy specimen with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (and, per guideline-based risk assessment, lymph node evaluation) or comprehensive imaging (pelvic MRI ± CT/PET) to complete the diagnostic work-up before a final FIGO stage can be assigned.\n\n---\n\n## MOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification)\n**Cannot be assigned from the available material.** The comment section explicitly states that immunohistochemistry (Keratin cocktail, p53, p16, ER, PR, β-catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) is **suggested/recommended** on the polyp block (Block 3) but has **not yet been performed**. Without p53 and mismatch-repair protein (MLH1/MSH2/MSH6/PMS2) IHC results (and without POLE mutation testing, which is not mentioned at all), none of the four WHO molecular subgroups — POLE-ultramutated, MMR-deficient, p53-abnormal, or NSMP — can be assigned at this time. Molecular classification should be finalized once the recommended IHC panel (and POLE sequencing, if indicated per institutional algorithm) is completed.\n\n---\n\n## LYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION\n**Absent** — the report states \"without evidence of vasculoneural invasion **in this sample**.\" This finding is limited to the tissue submitted (curettage/polyp fragments) and does not exclude LVI elsewhere in the uterus; a definitive assessment requires evaluation of the hysterectomy specimen.\n\n---\n\n## MYOMETRIAL INVASION\n**Cannot be estimated.** The report explicitly notes only \"scarce representation of basal endometrium,\" meaning myometrium is essentially not represented in the submitted material. No statement regarding depth of invasion (e.g., <50% vs. ≥50%) can be made from a curettage/polyp specimen. This assessment requires a hysterectomy specimen.\n\n---\n\n## KEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES\n- **Histologic type/grade:** Endometrioid adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated (FIGO grade 2) — an intermediate-grade tumour.\n- **Polyp involvement:** Tumour involves polypoid endometrial fragments, indicating at least an intraluminal/polypoid component of disease.\n- **LVI:** Not identified in the sample examined (favorable, but not confirmatory given limited/fragmented sampling).\n- **Myometrial invasion:** Unknown — cannot be risk-stratified (a critical gap, since depth of invasion is a major determinant of stage and adjuvant therapy).\n- **Cervical involvement:** No normal endocervix identified and no adenocarcinoma reported in the endocervical sample, but the specimen is not adequate to rule out cervical stromal extension.\n- **Molecular/IHC profile:** Not yet available (p53, MMR proteins, ER/PR, p16, β-catenin pending) — this is a major unresolved prognostic variable, since molecular subgroup (especially p53-abnormal vs. POLE-ultramutated vs. MMR-deficient vs. NSMP) substantially changes risk category and management per current ESGO-ESTRO-ESP risk stratification.\n- **Overall:** The case currently has a grade 2 endometrioid histology with no LVI seen and no cervical involvement identified, but definitive risk stratification is **not possible** due to lack of myometrial assessment and pending molecular/IHC data.\n\n---\n\n## RECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT\nGiven the incomplete diagnostic information, management should proceed in two coordinated steps:\n\n1. **Complete the diagnostic work-up before finalizing treatment planning:**\n   - Perform the **recommended IHC panel** (p53, MLH1/MSH2/MSH6/PMS2, ER, PR, p16, β-catenin, keratin cocktail) on the available polyp tissue block to establish the WHO molecular subgroup (and consider POLE mutation testing per local molecular pathology protocol), as this will materially change risk category and adjuvant therapy decisions.\n   - Obtain **pelvic MRI** (± chest/abdomen imaging as clinically indicated) to assess myometrial invasion depth, cervical involvement, and regional lymphadenopathy, since these cannot be determined from the curettage specimen.\n\n2. **Proceed to definitive surgical staging**, consistent with standard management of endometrioid endometrial adenocarcinoma:\n   - **Total hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (TH-BSO)**, with pelvic ± para-aortic lymph node assessment (sentinel lymph node mapping or systematic lymphadenectomy) guided by preoperative imaging and grade/risk group, to allow accurate FIGO surgical staging (myometrial invasion depth, cervical stromal involvement, adnexal/peritoneal spread, nodal status).\n   - **Adjuvant therapy** (observation, vaginal brachytherapy, external beam pelvic radiotherapy, and/or chemotherapy) should be determined only after the final surgical stage, LVI status on the full specimen, and molecular subgroup are known, per current ESGO-ESTRO-ESP endometrial cancer risk-stratification guidelines. In particular:\n     - If molecular testing shows a **POLE-ultramutated** profile, this would support a favorable prognosis and potential de-escalation of adjuvant treatment.\n     - A **p53-abnormal** profile would indicate high-risk disease and support consideration of adjuvant chemotherapy ± radiotherapy.\n     - An **MMR-deficient** profile has implications for both prognosis/adjuvant therapy discussions and hereditary cancer (Lynch syndrome) screening/genetic counseling referral.\n     - An **NSMP** profile would be risk-stratified using conventional clinicopathologic factors (grade, stage, LVI, myometrial invasion).\n\n**In summary:** definitive FIGO staging, molecular classification, and final management recommendations are **pending** completion of IHC studies and definitive surgical staging (hysterectomy with BSO and nodal assessment); the current report supports a diagnosis of moderately differentiated (grade 2) endometrioid endometrial adenocarcinoma with no LVI identified in the sample, but stage and molecular subgroup cannot be finalized from curettage/polyp material alone.",
