        "summary": "# Diagnostic summary — report 2605694\n\n**Source:** Histological report dated **05/11/2026** (as printed), for a 47-year-old woman. Three resection samples are labelled **“Endocervix” (Block 1), “Endometrium” (Block 2), and “Endometrial polyps” (Block 3)**.\n\n## DIAGNOSIS\n**Endometrioid-pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated, FIGO grade 2 (II).** Some polypoid fragments are involved by adenocarcinoma. “FIGO II” in this diagnostic context denotes histological grade, not surgical stage II.\n\n## FIGO STAGE\n**Cannot be assigned from the available material; no reliable most-likely surgical stage is established.** These are fragmented samples with scarce basal endometrium and no documented assessment of myometrial invasion, cervical stromal invasion, lymph nodes, or extrauterine spread.\n\nNo normal endocervix is identified, so cervical involvement cannot be determined from the specimen label alone. Carcinoma involving polypoid fragments does not establish confinement to a polyp or justify stage IA.\n\n## MOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification)\n**Cannot be assigned.** Immunohistochemistry was only suggested on **Block 3**; no results are reported. The recommended panel comprises **Keratin Cocktail, P53, P16, Estrogen Receptors, Progesterone Receptors, Beta-Catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, and PMS2**.\n\nThere is also no reported POLE mutation analysis. Consequently, none of **POLE-ultramutated, MMR-deficient, p53-abnormal, or NSMP** can be established. Endometrioid morphology alone does not establish NSMP.\n\n## LYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION\n**Absent/not identified in the examined sample**, with the report specifically stating **“without evidence of vasculoneural invasion in this sample.”** This limited-sample finding does not definitively exclude lymphovascular invasion elsewhere in the tumour.\n\n## MYOMETRIAL INVASION\n**Not assessable; neither depth in millimetres nor percentage invasion can be estimated.** Basal endometrium is scarcely represented, and no myometrial tissue, endomyometrial interface, or full-thickness uterine wall assessment is documented. Absence of invasion cannot be inferred.\n\n## KEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES\n- Established **endometrioid histology and FIGO grade 2**, with adenocarcinoma involving some polypoid fragments.\n- No vascular/neural invasion identified in the submitted material, subject to limited sampling.\n- Fragmentation and scarce basal endometrium prevent assessment of tumour confinement and invasion depth.\n- Cervical stromal involvement, tumour size, nodal status, extrauterine extent, and molecular subgroup are undetermined.\n\n**A definitive prognostic risk group cannot be assigned** without adequate staging pathology and molecular results.\n\n## RECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT\n1. **Gynaecological-oncology/tumour-board assessment for definitive management and staging.** If clinically suitable, plan definitive surgery with adequate uterine pathological assessment; myometrial depth, cervical stromal involvement, LVSI, and any nodal/extrauterine disease require evaluation. The report does not contain sufficient information to individualize the operative extent.\n2. **Complete the recommended Block 3 immunohistochemistry**, particularly the p53 and mismatch-repair markers needed for molecular assessment. Consider POLE mutation testing to complete the integrated molecular work-up; this would be additional to the report’s suggested IHC panel.\n3. **Select postoperative management after definitive stage, risk features, and molecular subgroup are available.** This report alone cannot establish whether observation, radiotherapy, chemotherapy, or another systemic treatment is appropriate, or support a specific adjuvant regimen.",
