        "summary": "# DIAGNOSTIC SUMMARY & TUMOUR BOARD REPORT\n**Reference:** Pathology Report No. 2605694 | **Patient Age:** 47 years (Female)  \n**Specimen:** Resection / sampling of Endocervix (Block 1), Endometrium (Block 2), and Endometrial polyps (Block 3)\n\n---\n\n### DIAGNOSIS:\n- **Histological Type:** Endometrioid pattern endometrial adenocarcinoma (involving endometrial tissue and polypoid fragments).\n- **Histological Grade:** FIGO Grade II (moderately differentiated).\n\n---\n\n### FIGO STAGE:\n- **Status:** **Cannot be definitively assigned from the available material.**\n- **Reasoning:** \n  - Endometrial cancer staging according to FIGO criteria (FIGO 2009 / 2023) is a surgical and pathological staging system requiring evaluation of a total hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy specimen, along with nodal and peritoneal assessment.\n  - The submitted specimen consists entirely of curettage/biopsy and polypectomy fragments (endocervix 0.5 cc, endometrium 3 cc, endometrial polyps 1 cc).\n  - Essential staging parameters cannot be determined:\n    1. *Depth of myometrial invasion:* Only a \"scarce representation of basal endometrium\" is present; no full-thickness myometrial wall is available to determine whether the tumor is confined to the endometrium, invades <50% of the myometrium (Stage IA), or invades ≥50% of the myometrium (Stage IB).\n    2. *Cervical involvement:* The endocervical sample contained only mucohemorrhagic material with \"no normal endocervix identified,\" precluding evaluation of cervical stromal invasion (Stage II).\n    3. *Extrauterine and nodal status:* Adnexal, serosal, parametrial/vaginal, peritoneal, and pelvic/paraaortic lymph node statuses (Stage III/IV) are unassessed.\n  - If disease is ultimately confined to the uterine corpus upon surgical resection, the most likely eventual surgical stage is Stage I (IA or IB), but definitive staging requires completion of primary surgical resection.\n\n---\n\n### MOLECULAR SUBGROUP (WHO 5th edition integrated classification):\n- **Status:** **The molecular subgroup cannot be assigned from the available material.**\n- **Reasoning:** \n  - According to the WHO 5th edition integrated classification and ESGO/ESTRO/ESP guidelines, endometrial carcinomas are categorized into four distinct molecular subgroups:\n    1. *POLE-ultramutated (POLEmut)*\n    2. *Mismatch repair-deficient (MMR-deficient / MMRd)*\n    3. *p53-abnormal (p53abn)*\n    4. *No specific molecular profile (NSMP)*\n  - In this report, immunohistochemistry (IHC) was only recommended/suggested by the pathologist (\"On Block 3, immunohistochemistry studies are suggested, especially to determine biomarker profile including Keratin Cocktail, P53, P16, Estrogen Receptors, Progesterone Receptors, Beta-Catenin, MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, and PMS2\") and was **not performed**.\n  - No molecular/sequencing testing for *POLE* exonuclease domain mutations was performed.\n  - Because no IHC results or molecular data are reported, the tumor cannot be categorized into any of the four WHO molecular subgroups at this time.\n\n---\n\n### LYMPHOVASCULAR INVASION:\n- **Absent.**\n- **Report Details:** The pathology report explicitly states \"WITHOUT EVIDENCE OF VASCULONEURAL INVASION IN THIS SAMPLE.\" (Definitive status will be confirmed on the final hysterectomy specimen).\n\n---\n\n### MYOMETRIAL INVASION:\n- **Status:** **Cannot be assessed / Insufficient data to determine depth.**\n- **Reasoning:** The received specimen consists solely of superficial curettage and polypectomy fragments (endocervix, endometrium, and endometrial polyps). The microscopic evaluation specifically notes a \"scarce representation of basal endometrium\" with no underlying myometrium included. Evaluation of the presence and depth of myometrial invasion (<50% vs ≥50%) requires histological examination of the full-thickness uterine wall from a hysterectomy specimen.\n\n---\n\n### KEY PROGNOSTIC FEATURES:\n1. **Histological Type and Grade (Favorable to Intermediate):**\n   - Endometrioid adenocarcinoma is a type I endometrial carcinoma associated with a generally more favorable prognosis than non-endometrioid histotypes (e.g., serous, clear cell, or carcinosarcoma).\n   - FIGO Grade II (moderately differentiated) places the tumor in an intermediate-grade category, carrying a higher recurrence risk than FIGO Grade I, but lower than FIGO Grade III.\n2. **Absence of Lymphovascular / Vasculoneural Invasion (Favorable):**\n   - Vasculoneural/lymphovascular invasion (LVSI) is not identified in the examined tissue, which is a favorable prognostic factor against early nodal dissemination, pending confirmation on the surgical resection specimen.\n3. **Patient Age (47 years):**\n   - Relatively young, pre/perimenopausal patient. Although younger age (<50 years) is associated with favorable baseline survival, the presence of a Grade II carcinoma standardly necessitates surgical staging.\n4. **Critical Missing / Pending Prognostic Parameters:**\n   - *Depth of Myometrial Invasion:* The primary driver of T-stage and local recurrence risk (<50% vs ≥50%) is currently unknown.\n   - *Cervical Stromal Invasion:* Unassessed (\"no normal endocervix identified\").\n   - *Nodal Status:* Preoperative/unassessed.\n   - *Molecular Subgroup:* Crucial for modern risk stratification under WHO 5th edition / ESGO-ESTRO-ESP guidelines. Identification of *POLE* mutation confers an excellent prognosis, MMR-deficiency indicates intermediate prognosis and triggers Lynch syndrome evaluation, while p53-abnormality shifts the tumor into a high-risk category warranting intensified adjuvant therapy.\n   - *Hormone Receptor Status:* ER/PR expression remains pending (suggested in the IHC panel).\n\n---\n\n### RECOMMENDED MANAGEMENT:\n1. **Immediate Diagnostic & Pathology Workup:**\n   - **Perform Suggested Immunohistochemistry (Block 3):** Carry out the suggested IHC panel, specifically MMR proteins (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2), p53, and ER/PR to establish the molecular subgroup and Lynch syndrome screening status. Consider *POLE* mutation sequencing if indicated by institutional algorithm.\n   - **Preoperative Staging Imaging:** Obtain a pelvic MRI with contrast (or expert transvaginal ultrasound) to estimate depth of myometrial invasion and assess for cervical involvement, alongside chest/abdominal imaging (CT scan) to rule out extrauterine disease or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy.\n2. **Primary Surgical Treatment (Standard of Care):**\n   - **Procedure:** Total hysterectomy and bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (TH/BSO), preferably performed via a minimally invasive approach (laparoscopic or robotic-assisted).\n   - **Nodal Assessment:** Sentinel lymph node (SLN) mapping and biopsy with pathological ultrastaging (or systemic pelvic ± para-aortic lymphadenectomy if SLN mapping fails or if macroscopic nodal involvement is encountered).\n   - **Fertility Preservation Note:** Conservative/fertility-sparing management (e.g., progestin therapy) is **contraindicated** because the tumor is FIGO Grade II (fertility preservation is restricted to well-differentiated FIGO Grade I endometrioid carcinoma strictly limited to the endometrium).\n3. **Adjuvant Therapy Decision-Making (Post-Surgical Staging & Molecular Integration):**\n   - Final adjuvant management will be determined by the integration of surgical-pathological stage and the completed molecular subgroup per ESGO-ESTRO-ESP guidelines:\n     - **Low Risk** (e.g., Stage IA, FIGO Grade 2, LVSI-negative, NSMP/MMRd, or Stage I–II POLEmut): Observation alone; no adjuvant therapy required.\n     - **Intermediate / High-Intermediate Risk** (e.g., Stage IB, FIGO Grade 2, NSMP/MMRd, or Stage IA with substantial LVSI): Vaginal brachytherapy (VBT) to optimize local pelvic control, or pelvic external beam radiotherapy (EBRT) if multiple adverse risk factors are present.\n     - **High Risk** (e.g., any myometrial invasion in p53-abnormal tumors, or Stage III/nodal-positive disease): External beam radiotherapy (EBRT) combined with systemic chemotherapy (e.g., carboplatin and paclitaxel).\n4. **Genetic Counseling & Screening:**\n   - If MMR IHC reveals loss of mismatch repair proteins (MMRd), perform reflex *MLH1* promoter hypermethylation testing (if MLH1/PMS2 lost) and refer the patient for genetic counseling and germline testing for Lynch syndrome.",
