        "summary": "# Report 2605374 — AI-assisted interpretation for the attending pathologist\n\n## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n\nThe report is internally consistent. The diagnosis of invasive adenocarcinoma is secure: desmoplastic stroma, adjacent HGD and a colorectal immunoprofile all point the same way. Review points, most important first:\n\n1. **Staging is limited by the biopsy.** The fragments do not represent depth of invasion, lymph nodes or margins. Desmoplasia proves invasion into at least the submucosa, which sets a floor of ≥T1 and nothing more. Under AJCC 8th the formal status is **pTX pNX**. The report does not support any higher definitive pT (T2–T4). Local T and N staging has to come from pelvic MRI.\n2. **Missing therapy-relevant molecular results, with a risk of running out of tissue.**\n   - RAS (KRAS/NRAS) and BRAF were not tested, and none of the IHC performed is a surrogate for them.\n   - All tissue is in one block: >15 fragments of 1–4 mm, some fibrinous. That block has already been cut for 10 IHC stains, and tumour cell fraction is not documented.\n   - A lower rectal carcinoma is a likely candidate for neoadjuvant therapy, so this block may be the only pre-treatment tumour tissue.\n3. **Documentation gaps.**\n   - There is no microscopic description (\"See diagnosis\"). Not recorded: tumour fraction, whether submucosa or muscularis propria is present, any mucinous or signet-ring component, PNI, and tumour budding.\n   - IHC is reported without percentages or patterns: HER2 % of stained cells and the scoring system used, p53 extent, and MMR internal controls are all missing.\n   - No clinical or endoscopic data are given (lesion size, distance from anal verge, IBD history).\n4. **Terminology to correct.**\n   - \"Microsatellite proteins\" should read MMR proteins (pMMR).\n   - \"Profile compatible with rectal primary\": IHC supports *colorectal* origin only. The rectal site rests on the specimen label and the adjacent HGD.\n   - \"p53 … (mutated)\" is an IHC surrogate, not a sequencing result.\n   - \"Moderately differentiated\" corresponds to WHO 5th *low grade*.\n5. **Findings that depend on sampling.** The grade and \"no LVI identified\" come from superficial, ulcerated fragments. A high-grade component may not have been sampled, and a negative LVI result on biopsy has low negative predictive value.\n6. **Pending studies:** none listed in the report.\n\nFields requested as \"definitive\" are completed with the definitive category the biopsy supports (≥pT1/pTX, pNX, no Bd grade, RAS/BRAF not tested) rather than with values the report does not contain.\n\n## SPECIMEN\n\nLower rectum tumour, biopsy (>15 fragments of 1–4 mm, all embedded in one block); 62-year-old male, outpatient; admission 04/29/2026, reported 05/05/2026.\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\n\n**Lower rectum, biopsy: invasive adenocarcinoma, NOS (conventional type), low grade (WHO 5th two-tier; moderately differentiated, G2). The tumour is extensively ulcerated, has desmoplastic stroma (invasion into at least the submucosa) and adjacent high-grade dysplasia, consistent with a primary colorectal (rectal) adenocarcinoma.**\nBiomarkers: CK7−/CK20+/CDX2+/SATB2+; pMMR; HER2-negative (IHC 1+); p53 aberrant (overexpression). Biopsy-level staging: ≥T1 floor (formally pTX), pNX.\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n\n| Parameter | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Histotype (WHO 5th) | Adenocarcinoma NOS (ICD-O 8140/3); no special subtype reported |\n| Grade | **Low grade** (moderately differentiated; AJCC G2), from the biopsy only |\n| Invasion | Invasive: desmoplasia means at least submucosal invasion; LVI not identified; PNI not reported |\n| pT (AJCC 8th) | Best available is **≥pT1** (biopsy floor); formal notation **pTX** |\n| pN / M | **pNX** (no nodes); no pathological M category (pM0 and MX are not valid); cM from CT |\n| Stage group | No pathological stage group (a diagnostic biopsy only feeds into cTNM); clinical stage from MRI + CT |\n| Margins | Not applicable (diagnostic biopsy); CRM/MRF status from MRI |\n| Tumour budding (ITBCC 2016) | Not reported; **no Bd grade assigned**, because Bd1–3 is defined at the invasive-front hotspot and this biopsy has none |\n| Precursor | HGD in some adjacent rectal crypts |\n| Lineage IHC | CK20+, CDX2+, SATB2+, CK7−, i.e. colorectal (no % reported) |\n| p53 | Overexpression, an aberrant pattern and surrogate for TP53 mutation (extent not reported) |\n| HER2 | **1+ = negative** (HERACLES, and also negative by gastric criteria); no ISH needed (% not reported) |\n| MMR | MLH1/MSH2/MSH6/PMS2 retained, i.e. **pMMR** (equivalent to MSS/MSI-L) |\n| RAS/BRAF, NTRK, POLE | **Not tested**; no IHC surrogate; pMMR does not imply wild-type |\n| Pending / recommended | Nothing pending. Recommend RAS/BRAF (ideally NGS) on this block, pelvic MRI, CT chest/abdomen/pelvis, CEA, colonoscopy, MDT review |\n\n## INTERPRETATION\n\n- **Diagnosis.**\n  - Desmoplastic stroma is the key biopsy criterion for invasion beyond the muscularis mucosae. It separates adenocarcinoma from HGD or intramucosal carcinoma.\n  - The adjacent HGD is an in situ precursor, which supports primary rectal origin through the dysplasia–carcinoma (adenoma–carcinoma) sequence rather than secondary involvement.\n  - CK7−/CK20+/CDX2+/SATB2+ confirms colorectal lineage. In a man at this site, it argues against the main mimics: direct extension of prostatic adenocarcinoma, anal-gland-type adenocarcinoma (typically CK7+/CK20−) and CK7+ metastases.\n  - WHO 5th: adenocarcinoma NOS. Subtypes defined by proportion (mucinous or signet-ring >50%) are assigned on the whole tumour, not on a biopsy.\n- **Grade.**\n  - Moderately differentiated equals WHO low grade (≥50% gland formation).\n  - WHO/ICCR grading is based on the least differentiated component (buds and poorly differentiated clusters excluded), so a biopsy can under-grade.\n  - Neoadjuvant treatment distorts later morphology, so this pre-treatment grade is the key reference.\n- **Staging (AJCC 8th).**\n  - A diagnostic biopsy counts towards clinical classification. pT is reserved for the definitive surgical specimen.\n  - The findings set a floor of ≥T1 and say nothing about muscularis propria or mesorectal invasion. Ulceration and desmoplasia should not be used to infer depth.\n  - In the extraperitoneal lower rectum, management is driven by MRI: cT, cN, mrEMVI, MRF/CRM involvement and the relationship to the sphincters and levators.\n- **Tumour budding (ITBCC 2016).**\n  - ITBCC method: buds (single cells or clusters of ≤4 cells) are counted on H&E in one 0.785 mm² hotspot at the invasive front. Bd1 = 0–4, Bd2 = 5–9, Bd3 = ≥10.\n  - These fragments have no invasive front, so the Bd scheme does not apply. Intratumoural budding (ITB) could be counted descriptively; ITBCC links ITB to nodal metastasis, but there is no validated biopsy cut-off for management.\n  - Budding becomes decisive if the lesion is locally excised as pT1 (it independently predicts nodal metastasis) or resected upfront without neoadjuvant therapy. It is not reported after neoadjuvant therapy (ICCR).\n- **MMR.**\n  - All four proteins are retained, i.e. pMMR, so MSI-H is unlikely. The tumour-level Lynch screen is negative, so no reflex BRAF V600E or MLH1-methylation testing is needed.\n  - The anti-PD-1 organ-preservation pathway used for dMMR/MSI-H rectal cancer does not apply.\n  - Caveats: a few MSI-H tumours retain non-functional protein, and POLE-ultramutated tumours are pMMR.\n  - The pre-treatment biopsy is the preferred tissue for MMR testing, because tissue after chemoradiotherapy can show artefactual MSH6 loss.\n- **HER2 (colorectal criteria).**\n  - HERACLES positive: 3+ (intense circumferential, basolateral or lateral membranous staining) in ≥50% of cells, or 2+ in ≥50% of cells with ISH HER2:CEP17 ≥2.0 in ≥50% of cells.\n  - 1+ (faint staining at any cellularity) is negative and needs no ISH. It is also negative under gastric criteria, so the result holds whichever scoring system was used (the report does not say).\n  - HER2-low has no therapeutic role in CRC; there were no responses in the IHC 1+ cohort of DESTINY-CRC01.\n  - HER2 is heterogeneous in CRC. Re-testing on a resection or metastasis is only worthwhile if metastatic disease develops and HER2-directed therapy is being considered.\n- **p53.** Overexpression is an aberrant (missense-type) pattern and indicates a probable TP53 mutation. It supports neoplasia and, together with pMMR and the adjacent HGD, fits the chromosomal-instability pathway. p53 has no staging or predictive role in routine CRC practice.\n- **Definitive IHC-based molecular classification:** pMMR (MSS-equivalent), HER2-negative, TP53-aberrant colorectal adenocarcinoma. RAS/BRAF: not tested. pMMR does not imply wild-type status, since BRAF V600E also occurs in pMMR CRC.\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n\n**Clinical / radiological**\n1. High-resolution pelvic MRI (rectal protocol) for cT, cN, mrEMVI, MRF/CRM, tumour height and sphincter/levator involvement. Add ERUS if the disease looks early (cT1–2) and local excision is being considered.\n2. CT chest/abdomen/pelvis for cM and a baseline CEA. Complete colonoscopy to look for synchronous neoplasia, if not already done.\n3. Correlate with endoscopy (size, distance from anal verge, circumferential extent) and history: IBD (would make the HGD colitis-associated), prior neoplasia, and family history (germline referral only if criteria are met, since the tumour is pMMR).\n4. MDT review with integrated cTNM. Because the tumour is pMMR, the standard multimodality pathway applies (TNT/CRT vs surgery vs organ preservation, guided by MRI).\n\n**Pathology / laboratory**\n5. Addendum to record:\n   - tumour cell fraction (%);\n   - whether submucosa or muscularis propria is present (carcinoma infiltrating muscularis propria bundles would raise the floor to ≥T2);\n   - any mucinous, signet-ring or micropapillary component, and PNI;\n   - HER2 scoring system, % of stained cells and staining pattern;\n   - p53 extent and pattern;\n   - MMR internal controls.\n6. Protect the remaining tissue: cut unstained sections for molecular testing now. If tumour fraction is insufficient, re-biopsy before neoadjuvant therapy.\n7. Extended RAS (KRAS/NRAS exons 2–4) and BRAF V600E on this block, preferably with a CRC NGS panel that also covers ERBB2 amplification, NTRK/RET fusions, POLE and TMB/MSI. This is mandatory before anti-EGFR therapy if disease becomes metastatic.\n8. No HER2 ISH needed (1+). MSI-PCR/NGS only if hereditary suspicion is strong or MMR staining was equivocal.\n9. On the definitive specimen:\n   - Local excision: depth of submucosal invasion, grade, LVI, ITBCC Bd, margin clearance.\n   - TME after neoadjuvant therapy: ypTNM, tumour regression score (AJCC/CAP 0–3), CRM (involved if ≤1 mm), mesorectal quality, ≥12 nodes, EMVI/LVI, PNI, tumour deposits; no budding.\n   - Upfront resection: full pTNM plus ITBCC Bd.",
