        "summary": "CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n- No internal immunophenotypic discordance; CK20+/CDX2+/SATB2+/CK7- profile concordant with rectal primary as stated.\n- Diagnosis firm on available material; no provisional qualifier affecting histologic type.\n- Specimen limitation: biopsy sampling only — >15 tiny fragments in 1-4 mm range in one block, extensively ulcerated. Depth of wall invasion, definitive pT depth, surgical margin status, nodal status, and formal ITBCC hotspot budding count are not evaluable on this material. LVI assessment has reduced sensitivity on limited ulcerated biopsy. PNI not stated.\n- Ancillary limitation: IHC reported qualitatively without percentage extent except HER2 1+; Ki-67 not reported; no KRAS/NRAS/BRAF testing reported. MMR IHC complete and interpretable.\n- No pending stain delaying current typing; predictive molecular testing remains to be completed for therapy planning.\n\nSPECIMEN\nLower rectum tumor biopsy, multiple small fragments in one block (Request 2605374, 62-year-old male, outpatient, admission 04/29/2026, report 05/05/2026).\n\nAI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\nLow-grade conventional colorectal adenocarcinoma NOS of lower rectum, pMMR/MSS-equivalent, HER2-negative (1+), p53-mutant pattern, invasive at least to submucosa (best available AJCC 8th: at least pT1NxMx on biopsy).\n\nKEY FINDINGS\n- Histologic type (WHO 5th Digestive System Tumours): adenocarcinoma NOS, conventional (tubular) type, moderately differentiated — two-tier grade: low-grade.\n- Grade: low-grade (conventional moderately differentiated).\n- Stage (AJCC 8th, best available on biopsy morphology): at least pT1NxMx; invasive adenocarcinoma with desmoplastic stromal response establishes at least submucosal invasion; exact wall-layer depth, pN and pM not evaluable on biopsy fragments — clinical TNM by imaging and surgical specimen for definitive T/N.\n- Margins: not applicable to biopsy — no inked surgical margin; tumour present in biopsy fragments; R-status: RX (not evaluable on biopsy).\n- Invasion: invasion present with significant desmoplastic reaction; lymphovascular invasion: L0 — none identified; perineural invasion: Pn0 — none documented/reported; ulceration: extensively ulcerated; adjacent rectal mucosa with high-grade dysplasia in some crypts (precursor/field change).\n- Immunophenotype (as reported, qualitative without quantified extent except HER2): CK20 positive, CDX2 positive, SATB2 positive; CK7 negative — colorectal/rectal profile; p53 overexpressed — mutant/missense-type pattern; HER2 score 1+ — negative by colorectal-specific criteria (0/1+ = negative, no ISH indicated); MLH1 retained, MSH2 retained, MSH6 retained, PMS2 retained.\n- Molecular classification from available IHC: pMMR, MSS-equivalent (low probability of MSI-H); CIN-like TP53-altered; HER2-negative; KRAS/NRAS/BRAF: not reported — classified as non-MSI-H, HER2-negative pending direct RAS/RAF assay.\n- Tumour budding (ITBCC 2016, 0.785 mm2 hotspot: Bd1 0-4, Bd2 5-9, Bd3 ≥10 buds): no bud count reported; no high-grade budding documented in description — best available on limited ulcerated fragments: Bd1 (low budding) as provisional record, with formal hotspot count on surgical excision.\n- Pending / recommended studies: KRAS/NRAS (including extended RAS) + BRAF V600E + MSI-PCR/NTRK only if indicated, clinical staging imaging, complete colonoscopy to exclude synchronous lesion, surgical pathology for definitive T/N, budding, LVI/PNI and margin/R assessment.\n\nINTERPRETATION\nMorphology of conventional gland-forming adenocarcinoma with desmoplastic stroma in lower rectal biopsy establishes invasive colorectal-type adenocarcinoma NOS per WHO Classification of Tumours 5th edition Digestive System Tumours. Moderately differentiated histology maps to two-tier low-grade (well-to-moderately differentiated = low-grade; poorly differentiated = high-grade). Extensive ulceration and desmoplasia reflect established invasive growth beyond intramucosal disease; by AJCC 8th this is at least pT1 (at least submucosal invasion) on available fragments, with full wall-layer T, nodal and distant categories not evaluable without imaging and surgical specimen. Adjacent crypts with high-grade dysplasia support conventional adenoma-carcinoma sequence / field precursor rather than de novo undifferentiated tumour. Absence of identified LVI (L0) on limited biopsy does not exclude focal vascular involvement; no PNI documented.\n\nImmunophenotype CK20+/CDX2+/SATB2+ with CK7- is classic lower gastrointestinal/rectal lineage and supports rectal primary over upper GI, pancreatobiliary or lung origin, consistent with stated interpretation. p53 overexpression in diffuse strong pattern indicates TP53 missense-type alteration, characteristic of chromosomal-instability pathway conventional CRC, often co-occurring with MSS status. Retained nuclear expression of MLH1/MSH2/MSH6/PMS2 defines pMMR, indicating MSS-equivalent biology with low probability of MSI-H/Lynch-associated hypermutability; definitive molecular class is therefore pMMR/MSS, CIN-like, TP53-altered. HER2 1+ by colorectal-specific scoring (HERACLES/Valtorta criteria where only strong intense membranous staining in >50% or >10% depending on guideline version qualifies as 3+ positive, 2+ equivocal for ISH) is negative and predicts lack of HER2-driven amplification, no anti-HER2 ISH triage needed. Together: low-grade, MSS/pMMR, HER2-negative, TP53-mutant rectal adenocarcinoma, ulcerated with desmoplasia, precursor high-grade dysplasia in background mucosa, provisional ITBCC Bd1 on limited material.\n\nRECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n- Correlate with endoscopic appearance, tumour distance from anal verge/dentate line, sphincter involvement, digital rectal exam and pelvic MRI for clinical T/N, mesorectal fascia and extramural vascular invasion assessment.\n- Complete colonoscopy with documentation of synchronous polyps/cancers; review bowel prep and family cancer history given rectal cancer at 62 years despite pMMR result.\n- Predictive molecular panel on biopsy or surgical specimen: extended KRAS/NRAS exons 2-4, BRAF V600E, plus MSI-PCR only if MMR question arises; consider NGS panel covering KRAS/NRAS/BRAF, PIK3CA, HER2 amplification, NTRK fusions per local metastatic workup protocol; no HER2 ISH needed for 1+ unless clinical trial criterion.\n- Surgical / neoadjuvant multidisciplinary review: definitive grading, T/N, LVI/PNI with elastic/D2-40 if needed, ITBCC budding hotspot count, margin/CRM and R-status on total mesorectal excision specimen; baseline CEA, chest/abdomen-pelvis staging CT, smoking/comorbidity and fertility/continence counseling as appropriate.\n- Pathology follow-up: deeper levels only if needed to exclude minor mucinous/poorly differentiated component; no additional IHC needed for lineage; retain block for molecular testing.",
