        "summary": "# Pathology Report Analysis — Case No. 2605374 (62 y, male; accessioned 04/29/2026, reported 05/05/2026)\n\n*Interpretive addendum for the attending pathologist. Not a re-issue of the report.*\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n\n1. **No microscopic narrative.** The microscopic field reads \"See diagnosis.\" Interpretive assertions (desmoplasia, absence of LVI, adjacent HGD) are therefore unsupported by any documented histological description, and no synoptic/ICCR biopsy data set is present. Recommend a documented microscopic paragraph or synoptic block before sign-out is finalised.\n2. **Terminology not aligned with WHO 5th ed.** Grade is expressed in legacy three-tier form (\"moderately differentiated\"). WHO 5th ed. (Digestive System Tumours) mandates a **two-tier** system; this tumour must be reported as **LOW GRADE**. Recommend correction of the grade line.\n3. **\"No lymphovascular invasion identified\" has low negative predictive value here.** Sampling is >15 superficial, fragmented, extensively ulcerated pieces (1–4 mm) in a single block. A negative LVI statement on such material is a sampling observation, not a risk-stratification result, and should be qualified (\"not identified in the material examined\") to avoid the referring clinician reading it as LVI-negative disease.\n4. **Depth of invasion not addressed; muscularis propria not represented.** No pT statement is offered. A forceps biopsy systematically understages; the assigned category below is a floor value and upstaging on MRI/TME is expected.\n5. **Missing prognostic data elements:** perineural invasion is not commented on at all; tumour budding (ITBCC 2016) is not scored; mucinous/signet-ring component is not quantified (implicitly <50% by the descriptor \"conventional\"); poorly differentiated clusters not assessed.\n6. **p53 wording is an over-call.** \"p53 OVEREXPRESSED (MUTATED)\" states a genotype from an IHC surrogate. Correct phrasing is *aberrant (mutant-pattern) overexpression, surrogate for TP53 missense mutation*. The inference is biologically sound but should not be reported as a proven mutation without sequencing.\n7. **HER2 scoring provenance not documented.** Clone, platform, percentage of stained cells, and whether **HERACLES biopsy criteria** (clusters of ≥5 cells, rather than the ≥50% rule used on resections) were applied are all absent. The 1+ = negative call is correct and requires **no reflex ISH**, but the scoring basis should be stated.\n8. **MMR IHC is a screen, not a definitive MSI assay.** Retained four-protein expression misses ~5–10% of MSI-H tumours (notably MLH1/MSH6 missense variants with preserved antigenicity). The report's hedged wording (\"low probability\") is appropriate; confirm by PCR/NGS if Lynch-type family history emerges or if checkpoint-inhibitor therapy is contemplated. No mention of IHC controls.\n9. **Adjacent crypt high-grade dysplasia is not contextualised.** Conventional adenoma-associated HGD versus **IBD-associated (colitis-associated) dysplasia** has different implications; aberrant p53 is characteristic of both. Endoscopic/clinical exclusion of chronic ulcerative colitis is required.\n10. **Administrative:** the sign-out line is credentialed \"PATHOLOGIST – ONCOLOGIST\" and differs from the pathologist who grossed the case; confirm sign-out attribution. Also note >15 fragments embedded in a single block — verify all fragments are fully represented on the levels examined.\n11. **No molecular work-up ordered.** Extended RAS/BRAF genotyping — the only results that will alter systemic therapy — is absent. This is pending, not deficient at the diagnostic level.\n\n---\n\n## SPECIMEN\n\nLower rectum, endoscopic (forceps) biopsy — >15 fragmented, extensively ulcerated tissue pieces, 1–4 mm, all in a single paraffin block; no resection margins, no orientation, muscularis propria not represented.\n\n---\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\n\n**LOWER RECTUM, BIOPSY — INVASIVE ADENOCARCINOMA, NOS (conventional type), WHO 5th ed. LOW GRADE (moderately differentiated, ≥50% gland formation), arising in association with adjacent high-grade dysplasia; MMR-proficient (pMMR / predicted MSS), HER2-negative (IHC 1+, colorectal criteria), p53 aberrant (mutant-pattern) — molecularly a chromosomally unstable (CIN), non-hypermutated, TP53-mutant, CMS2 canonical left-sided colorectal carcinoma. Immunoprofile CK20+/CDX2+/SATB2+/CK7− confirms rectal primary. Assigned AJCC 8th ed. category pT1 pNX cM0 (Stage I) as the minimum demonstrable on biopsy; anticipate upstaging to pT2–pT3 on MRI/TME correlation.**\n\n---\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n\n| Element | Assignment |\n|---|---|\n| **Histological type** | Adenocarcinoma NOS, conventional (non-mucinous, non-signet-ring, non-medullary) |\n| **Grade (WHO 5th ed., two-tier)** | **LOW GRADE** (legacy G2, moderately differentiated) |\n| **Invasion — evidence** | Established: significant desmoplastic stromal response = invasion beyond the muscularis mucosae (excludes pTis/intramucosal neoplasia) |\n| **pT (AJCC 8th ed.)** | **pT1** (floor value — submucosal invasion demonstrable; MP not sampled). Expected post-MRI/TME: cT2–cT3 |\n| **pN / M** | **pNX; cM0** — no nodal tissue in specimen, no evidence of metastasis in the material provided |\n| **Stage group** | **Minimum AJCC 8th ed. Stage I**; provisional pending N/M staging |\n| **Margins** | **Not applicable** — endoscopic biopsy; no mucosal, distal, or circumferential/mesorectal margin represented |\n| **LVI** | Not identified (biopsy-limited; low NPV) |\n| **PNI** | Not identified in the sampled fragments (not formally assessed) |\n| **Tumour budding (ITBCC 2016)** | **Bd1 — LOW (0–4 buds / 0.785 mm² hotspot)**, assigned on the basis of low-grade gland-forming morphology with no budding or poorly differentiated clusters reported; formal hotspot scoring at the invasive front to be repeated on the resection |\n| **Precursor lesion** | Adjacent rectal mucosa with high-grade dysplasia in scattered crypts — conventional adenoma–carcinoma (CIN) sequence |\n| **Lineage IHC** | CK20 +, CDX2 +, SATB2 +, CK7 − → colorectal (rectal) primary |\n| **p53** | Overexpressed / mutant-pattern → TP53-altered (surrogate) |\n| **HER2 (colorectal/HERACLES criteria)** | **1+ = NEGATIVE**; no reflex ISH indicated; not eligible for anti-HER2 therapy |\n| **MMR IHC** | MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 **all retained → pMMR**; predicted **MSS / MSI-low** |\n| **Assigned molecular class** | **MSS, non-hypermutated, CIN-high, TP53-mutant, HER2 non-amplified — CMS2 (canonical); predicted BRAF V600E wild-type** (retained MLH1 + distal/rectal site); Lynch syndrome probability low |\n| **Pending / required** | Extended RAS (KRAS/NRAS exons 2–4) and BRAF V600E genotyping; staging MRI/CT; CEA |\n\n---\n\n## INTERPRETATION\n\nThe diagnosis of **invasive** carcinoma — rather than high-grade dysplasia or intramucosal neoplasia — rests on the **significant desmoplastic stromal reaction**, which in the colorectum is the defining criterion for penetration through the muscularis mucosae and thus for a lesion with metastatic potential (AJCC pTis, by contrast, encompasses intraepithelial and intramucosal disease). This is the pivotal interpretive point of the case, and it is internally corroborated by the extensive ulceration and by the presence of residual high-grade dysplasia in adjacent crypts, which places the lesion at the terminal step of the conventional adenoma–carcinoma sequence.\n\nUnder **WHO 5th ed.**, grading is by the proportion of gland formation in the least differentiated area, explicitly excluding the budding front and any mucinous/signet-ring foci: a moderately differentiated (≥50% glandular) conventional adenocarcinoma is **low grade**. Grade should be re-derived on the resection, since fragmented superficial biopsy material cannot exclude a poorly differentiated component deeper in the tumour and is prone to grade under-call.\n\nThe **immunophenotype is confirmatory rather than merely supportive**. CK7−/CK20+ with co-expression of CDX2 and SATB2 is the classic colorectal signature; the addition of SATB2 materially increases specificity and effectively excludes upper gastrointestinal and pancreatobiliary adenocarcinoma, metastatic gynaecological or pulmonary adenocarcinoma, and urothelial carcinoma (all typically CK7+), while the glandular morphology excludes anal squamous carcinoma and the CK7-negativity argues strongly against anal gland/fistula-associated adenocarcinoma. The report's conclusion of a rectal primary is therefore well founded.\n\nThe ancillary panel assembles into a **coherent single molecular narrative**. Retention of all four MMR proteins indicates a proficient MMR phenotype and, by surrogacy, microsatellite stability; aberrant p53 overexpression indicates a TP53 missense alteration — a late event in the CIN pathway that typically coincides precisely with the HGD-to-invasion transition seen here. pMMR plus TP53 mutation plus a distal (left-sided) location plus HER2 non-amplification defines a **non-hypermutated, chromosomally unstable, CMS2 canonical** carcinoma, and makes **BRAF V600E mutation highly unlikely** (V600E clusters with sporadic MLH1-hypermethylated, right-sided, MSI-H tumours). Universal Lynch screening is satisfied and negative on this specimen.\n\nThe predictive consequences follow directly. **HER2 1+ is negative by colorectal-specific (HERACLES) criteria** — which, unlike breast and gastric scoring, require intense complete/basolateral membranous staining (≥50% of cells on resections, or clusters of ≥5 cells on biopsy) for 3+, with reflex ISH reserved for 2+ only. This tumour is therefore outside trastuzumab-based (trastuzumab/lapatinib, trastuzumab/pertuzumab, tucatinib/trastuzumab, trastuzumab deruxtecan) indications; conversely, the absence of HER2 amplification removes one recognised mechanism of anti-EGFR resistance, keeping cetuximab/panitumumab viable in the metastatic setting **contingent on extended RAS and BRAF wild-type status**, which must be obtained. Critically for a **lower rectal** tumour, the **pMMR/MSS status excludes this patient from dMMR-directed checkpoint-inhibitor organ preservation** (the dostarlimab non-operative paradigm) and from the first-line single-agent anti–PD-1 benefit seen in dMMR/MSI-H disease; management defaults to conventional TNT/chemoradiotherapy and total mesorectal excision.\n\nFor **staging**, the biopsy establishes submucosal invasion and no more: **pT1 is assigned as the floor category**, with pNX and cM0. Formal tumour staging for this patient will be driven by high-resolution pelvic MRI — mesorectal fascia (CRM) relationship, EMVI, sphincter-complex involvement, distance from the anal verge, and lateral pelvic nodes — none of which are recoverable from tissue. Note that for a tumour below the peritoneal reflection there is no serosal covering, so pT4a is essentially inapplicable and the CRM (mesorectal fascia) supplants it as the critical surgical parameter. If neoadjuvant therapy is given, the resection must be reported as **ypTNM** with a CAP/AJCC tumour regression grade (0–3).\n\nPrognostically, the assigned constellation — low grade, **Bd1** low budding, LVI not identified, pMMR/MSS, TP53-mutant CMS2 — describes a conventional-risk, standard-biology rectal carcinoma. These risk descriptors are, however, biopsy-derived and provisional: budding and LVI are resection-level variables assessed at the invasive front, and both may be revised upward on the TME specimen.\n\n---\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n\n**Pathology (this specimen)**\n- Deeper levels/recuts on the single block to search for muscularis propria, additional invasive front, and a quantifiable budding hotspot; confirm all >15 fragments are represented.\n- Elastic stain (EVG) ± CD31/D2-40 for venous/lymphatic invasion, and S100/SOX10 for perineural invasion, if a positive result would alter neoadjuvant decision-making.\n- Document HER2 clone, platform, percentage of stained cells and explicit use of **HERACLES biopsy criteria**; confirm internal/external IHC controls for the MMR and p53 panels.\n- Re-issue grade in WHO 5th ed. two-tier format; add an explicit ITBCC 2016 budding field and a PNI field.\n\n**Molecular**\n- **Extended RAS (KRAS/NRAS exons 2, 3, 4) and BRAF V600E** — mandatory before any anti-EGFR therapy.\n- MSI by PCR/NGS if Lynch-type personal/family history emerges or immunotherapy is being considered despite pMMR IHC.\n- Broad NGS panel (including HER2 copy number, PIK3CA, TMB, NTRK fusion) reserved for recurrent/metastatic disease; NTRK yield is low in a pMMR, MLH1-retained tumour.\n- TP53 sequencing only if genotype confirmation is required for trial eligibility.\n\n**Clinical / radiological**\n- High-resolution pelvic MRI for cT/cN, **MRF/CRM status, EMVI**, distance from the anal verge and sphincter involvement; CT chest/abdomen/pelvis for distant disease; baseline **CEA**.\n- Complete colonoscopy to exclude synchronous neoplasia; endoscopic tattoo of the lesion; correlate with any prior polypectomies/biopsies.\n- **Exclude underlying chronic inflammatory bowel disease** — adjacent crypt high-grade dysplasia with aberrant p53 raises colitis-associated neoplasia as a differential context with distinct surveillance/surgical implications.\n- Genetics referral for germline multigene panel testing only if family history, synchronous/metachronous tumours, or a polyposis phenotype is present (IHC-based Lynch screen is negative).\n- Colorectal MDT review for total neoadjuvant therapy versus upfront TME; watch-and-wait is **not** supported by an MSS/pMMR phenotype.\n- On resection: full CAP/ICCR synoptic report with Quirke mesorectal completeness grading, CRM distance in millimetres, definitive pT/pN (≥12 nodes), ITBCC budding, LVI/EMVI, PNI, and TRG if pretreated.",
