        "summary": "CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n- No morphologic-IHC discordance; IHC profile CK20+/CDX2+/SATB2+/CK7- concordant with rectal primary as stated.\n- Specimen limitation - biopsy only: pT category, LVI assessment, margin/R status, tumor budding per ITBCC 2016, and depth/extent of invasion have limited evaluation on this material. LVI reported as not identified but evaluation limited by fragmentation and ulceration.\n- Extensively ulcerated adenocarcinoma with significant desmoplastic reaction: evaluation of intact invasive front limited.\n- No pending IHC/molecular studies in report; all stated ancillary studies (CK7/CK20/CDX2/SATB2/p53/HER2/4 MMR) reported. No internal inconsistency.\n\nSPECIMEN\nLower rectum biopsy, multiple fragments (>15 pieces, 1-4 mm range) in one block.\n\nAI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\nRectum, lower, biopsy: Conventional adenocarcinoma, NOS, moderately differentiated / WHO 5th low-grade, extensively ulcerated with desmoplastic response, pMMR / MSS, p53 mutated pattern, HER2-negative (1+), with adjacent high-grade dysplasia in crypts.\n\nKEY FINDINGS\n- Histologic type: Conventional adenocarcinoma, NOS, WHO Classification Digestive Tumors 5th ed.\n- Grade: Moderately differentiated → two-tier WHO grade: Low-grade; conventional G2.\n- Ulceration: Present, extensive.\n- Desmoplasia: Significant, supports invasion.\n- Lymphovascular invasion: Not identified on this biopsy material; assessment limited (LVI = 0 in available fragments, definitive assessment needs excisional specimen).\n- Precursor: Adjacent rectal mucosa with high-grade dysplastic changes in some crypts.\n- Margins / R status: Not applicable to biopsy; RX / not assessable - needs surgical excision to determine R status and radial/mucosal margins.\n- AJCC 8th TNM (biopsy-based): Invasive adenocarcinoma confirmed → at least pT1; definitive pT category not assignable on biopsy alone → best available: pT1 minimum, formal category pTX until resection; pNX - not assessable on biopsy; pM - not assessable (clinical cM0 by default until proven otherwise). For staging summary: cT at least cT1NxMx, invasive carcinoma on biopsy.\n- Tumor budding ITBCC 2016: Not formally scorable on ulcerated, fragmented biopsy without defined invasive front; on available material no high-grade budding identified → if scored provisionally: Bd1, low budding (0-4 buds per 0.785 mm2 hotspot), definitive assessment to be finalized on resection specimen.\n- IHC: CK20 POSITIVE, CDX2 POSITIVE, SATB2 POSITIVE; CK7 NEGATIVE; pattern compatible with rectal primary as per report.\n- p53: Overexpressed / mutated pattern.\n- HER2: Negative, score 1+ by colorectal-specific criteria (no membranous amplification pattern; negative / no ISH indicated).\n- MMR/MSI: MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 expressed in usual manner / intact nuclear expression → pMMR, MSS phenotype, low probability of microsatellite instability.\n- Molecular: KRAS/NRAS/BRAF status not reported; classified as RAS/BRAF unknown, MSS/pMMR per IHC.\n- Pending studies per report: None; all IHC listed as completed. Recommended studies listed below.\n\nINTERPRETATION\nLower rectum biopsy from 62-year-old male (request 2605374, admission 04/29/2026, report 05/05/2026) shows conventional, moderately differentiated adenocarcinoma, NOS. Morphology of extensively ulcerated invasive glands with significant desmoplastic reaction establishes invasive adenocarcinoma; adjacent high-grade dysplasia indicates adenoma-carcinoma sequence precursor in rectal mucosa.\n\nWHO 5th edition: Conventional adenocarcinoma, NOS, of rectum. Two-tier histologic grade: Low-grade (corresponding to moderately differentiated, G2). The immunophenotype CK20+/CDX2+/SATB2+/CK7- supports lower GI / rectal origin and is concordant with morphology; interpretation as rectal primary is appropriate.\n\npMMR phenotype with retention of MLH1/MSH2/MSH6/PMS2 indicates microsatellite stable (MSS) tumor, low probability of MSI. This is sporadic-type MSS carcinoma per available IHC.\n\np53 overexpression indicates mutated / aberrant TP53 pattern, consistent with chromosomal instability pathway of conventional rectal adenocarcinoma, commonly associated with MSS, rectal-type.\n\nHER2 IHC 1+ = negative per colorectal HER2 scoring criteria (HERACLES / colorectal: 1+ weak incomplete/basolateral <50% = negative, no reflex ISH). No HER2-targeted therapy indication by IHC.\n\nNo lymphovascular invasion identified in this biopsy sample; negative reporting does not exclude LVI given biopsy limitations and ulceration.\n\nAJCC 8th: Invasion confirmed with desmoplasia; definitive depth is not fully established without muscularis propria assessment. At least pT1 invasive into lamina propria/submucosa is established; formal stage on biopsy = pTX. N-category and margin status need surgical excision for full staging. Adjacent HGD does not upgrade T but confirms associated precursor lesion.\n\nITBCC 2016 tumor budding: Formal category needs excisional specimen with intact invasive front; ulcerated fragmented biopsy is not suitable for definitive scoring. No excess budding focus described; provisionally low Bd1 if assessed, definitive Bd needs excisional specimen.\n\nOverall classification: Rectal conventional adenocarcinoma, NOS, low-grade (moderately differentiated), MSS/pMMR, p53-mutated, HER2-negative (1+), extensively ulcerated, biopsy.\n\nRECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n- Clinical/endoscopic correlation for tumor location (lower rectum), size, extent, and cTNM imaging; MDT discussion for neoadjuvant therapy planning given lower rectal location and ulceration.\n- Complete surgical excision (if clinically indicated) for definitive pT, LVI, PNIs, tumor budding ITBCC formal score, margin status including CRM if total mesorectal excision, and lymph node status.\n- Molecular testing for metastatic / targeted therapy planning per guidelines: KRAS exon 2/3/4, NRAS exon 2/3/4, BRAF V600E, plus extended panel if indicated. Consider NTRK fusion if RAS/BRAF wild-type per local protocol. MSI PCR not needed given intact pMMR IHC unless clinical concern for Lynch.\n- No reflex HER2 ISH needed - HER2 negative (1+) per colorectal criteria; consider HER2 ISH only if subsequent 2+ equivocal on resection.\n- No additional IHC needed for diagnosis; pMMR intact captures MSS prognostic group.\n- Correlate with high-grade dysplasia in adjacent mucosa to ensure complete sampling of precursor lesion on resection.",
