        "summary": "CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n- Specimen limitation: fragmented lower-rectum biopsy only (>15 pieces, 1–4 mm); muscularis propria, full wall, nodes, and radial/longitudinal margins are not represented.\n- No separate microscopic narrative (diagnosis stands alone); tumour budding is not formally scored on this material.\n- No pending IHC/molecular studies listed. No internal diagnostic discrepancy: morphology, CK7−/CK20+/CDX2+/SATB2+ phenotype, p53-mutant pattern, and retained MMR are concordant with conventional rectal adenocarcinoma, pMMR/CIN pathway.\n\nSPECIMEN\nLower rectum, endoscopic/biopsy fragments (labelled “Lower rectum biopsy”), male, 62 years (received 04/29/2026; reported 05/05/2026).\n\nAI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\nInvasive conventional (NOS) rectal adenocarcinoma, moderately differentiated / low-grade (WHO 5th, two-tier), pMMR, HER2-negative (1+), p53-mutant pattern; immunophenotype of rectal primary.\n\nKEY FINDINGS\n- Grade: moderately differentiated = low-grade (WHO 5th two-tier).\n- Stage (AJCC 8th, best available from this biopsy): pT1 pNx pMx (invasive adenocarcinoma with desmoplasia proven; higher T not demonstrated in these fragments).\n- Margins: not applicable (biopsy).\n- Invasion: extensively ulcerated invasive adenocarcinoma with significant desmoplasia; no LVI identified on the sampled tissue.\n- Tumour budding (ITBCC 2016): not described; no Bd2/Bd3 budding recorded → treat as Bd1 (low) on the available diagnostic text.\n- IHC: CK20+, CDX2+, SATB2+, CK7− (rectal/intestinal primary).\n- p53: overexpressed (mutant-type).\n- HER2 (colorectal criteria): 1+ = negative.\n- MMR: MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 retained → pMMR / MSS-like (low probability of MSI-H).\n- Adjacent mucosa: high-grade dysplasia in some crypts.\n- Molecular (RAS/BRAF/MSI PCR/NGS): not performed; pMMR by IHC.\n\nINTERPRETATION\nThe biopsy shows conventional colorectal-type adenocarcinoma of the lower rectum. Moderate gland-forming differentiation corresponds to WHO 5th-edition adenocarcinoma, NOS, two-tier low-grade (G2). Extensive ulceration plus a significant desmoplastic stromal reaction establish true invasion beyond mucosa; on biopsy-sized fragments this supports AJCC 8th pT1 as the highest T category that is morphologically proven (pT2–pT4 would need muscularis propria or deeper tissues). Nodes and distant sites are not in the specimen (pNx pMx). Absence of identified LVI is noted but is of limited negative predictive value in 1–4 mm fragments.\n\nThe immunophenotype CK7−/CK20+/CDX2+/SATB2+ is the expected rectal primary profile and argues against a CK7-dominant upper-GI, pancreatobiliary, or Müllerian metastasis. Mutant-type p53 overexpression together with retained MLH1/MSH2/MSH6/PMS2 places the tumour in the chromosomal-instability / pMMR (MSS-like) conventional pathway, consistent with adjacent high-grade crypt dysplasia (adenoma–carcinoma sequence) rather than an MSI-H/Lynch or MLH1-methylated phenotype. HER2 1+ is negative by colorectal-specific scoring and does not support HER2-targeted eligibility on this assay. ITBCC budding is not narrated as intermediate or high; budding is therefore classified Bd1 (low) from the report as written.\n\nRECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n- Correlate with endoscopy, DRE, and pelvic MRI/ERUS plus CT/CE for clinical TNM (mrT/N, EMVI, CRM) and distant disease; this biopsy stage is pT1 pNx pMx only.\n- On the resection (or additional invasive-front material), record ITBCC 2016 budding, LVI/PNI, pT depth, nodes, and CRM per AJCC 8th / CAP.\n- RAS (KRAS/NRAS) and BRAF V600 testing if anti-EGFR therapy is considered (pMMR conventional CRC).\n- MSI PCR/NGS optional confirmation; four-protein IHC already indicates pMMR.\n- No indication for anti-HER2 on current 1+ result; re-test HER2 on resection if clinically indicated.\n- Multidisciplinary correlation for neoadjuvant vs up-front resection per rectal-cancer pathway.",
