        "summary": "## CRITICAL FLAGS / REVIEW POINTS\n- **Biopsy-limited staging:** no anatomic level of invasion is documented. Desmoplasia supports invasion but does not establish submucosal or deeper extension; assigning pT1–pT4 would overstate the evidence.\n- **IHC–genotype distinction:** the report's p53 designation “mutated” is based on overexpression, without a molecular result. Retained MMR proteins establish a pMMR immunophenotype, not molecularly confirmed microsatellite stability.\n- **Missing scoring data:** no tumour-bud count or IHC percentages are supplied. HER2 membrane-staining pattern and extent are absent, limiting independent verification of the reported score against colorectal-specific criteria.\n\n## SPECIMEN\nLower rectal tumour, fragmented biopsy, entirely submitted in one block; report **2605374**, dated **05 May 2026**.\n\n## AI ASSISTANT'S PRELIMINARY DIAGNOSIS\n**Primary rectal conventional adenocarcinoma, invasive, WHO 5th edition low grade (moderately differentiated), with adjacent high-grade dysplasia; mismatch repair–proficient (pMMR) immunophenotype.**\n\n## KEY FINDINGS\n- **Grade:** low grade under the WHO two-tier system; adjacent high-grade dysplasia is a separate mucosal finding.\n- **AJCC 8 TNM:** **pTX** (invasion depth undocumented), **pNX** (no regional nodes sampled). M category and stage group are unassigned in the supplied report; absence of metastatic information is not M0.\n- **Margins:** clearance unassessed in this fragmented diagnostic biopsy.\n- **Invasion:** desmoplastic stromal response supports invasion. Lymphovascular invasion is not identified in sampled tissue; perineural invasion is not reported.\n- **Tumour budding—ITBCC 2016:** unscored; no invasive-front hotspot count is provided. Categories are Bd1, 0–4; Bd2, 5–9; Bd3, ≥10 buds per **0.785 mm²**. No Bd category is supported by the report.\n- **Lineage markers:** CK20+, CDX2+, SATB2+; CK7−, supporting colorectal differentiation. Staining percentages are not reported.\n- **MMR:** MLH1, PMS2, MSH2 and MSH6 retained: **pMMR**, with low probability of MSI as stated in the report.\n- **p53:** overexpression, an aberrant/mutation-pattern IHC result; TP53 sequence status is not reported.\n- **HER2:** reported **1+, IHC-negative** under colorectal scoring interpretation; this does not establish ERBB2 non-amplification. Independent rescoring lacks the necessary staining details.\n- **Molecular results:** no molecular MSI, KRAS/NRAS, BRAF, TP53 or ERBB2 amplification results are reported. This IHC panel does not establish those genomic classifications.\n- **Pending studies:** none stated. Recommended correlations/testing are listed below.\n\n## INTERPRETATION\nThe conventional morphology, colorectal-lineage immunophenotype and adjacent dysplasia support a primary rectal adenocarcinoma. Moderate differentiation maps to **low grade** in the WHO 5th edition two-tier scheme; the dysplastic component does not upgrade the invasive carcinoma. Desmoplasia supports stromal invasion, but neither it nor extensive ulceration establishes bowel-wall depth, supporting **pTX** rather than a depth-specific category. Negative LVI in this limited sample does not establish absence throughout the tumour. The supported biomarker classifications are pMMR, aberrant p53 overexpression and reported HER2 IHC negativity; molecular prognostic or treatment-selection genotypes should not be inferred from them.\n\n## RECOMMENDED CORRELATIONS AND STUDIES\n- Correlate with endoscopic localization, rectal-protocol pelvic MRI and clinical metastatic staging.\n- On adequately sampled/oriented tumour material, document invasion depth, margins, nodal status, LVI/PNI and ITBCC budding at an evaluable invasive front.\n- Qualify “p53 mutated” as **aberrant overexpression pattern** unless a molecular result is available. Review HER2 membrane intensity, pattern and percentage using colorectal-specific criteria if needed for treatment selection.\n- Obtain **KRAS/NRAS and BRAF** testing when relevant to systemic treatment planning. Use MSI PCR/NGS if molecular MSI classification is clinically needed or MMR IHC is discordant with other findings.",
